(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e2a.ff38bb74d8.js"]={"21":[{"id":"sdl21_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":1,"stem":"Viewed from the front, how far does the primary intestinal loop normally rotate in total?","choices":{"A":"About 90 degrees clockwise","B":"About 180 degrees clockwise","C":"About 90 degrees counterclockwise","D":"About 270 degrees counterclockwise","E":"About 360 degrees counterclockwise"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The loop rotates about 270 degrees counterclockwise: about 90 degrees during herniation and the remaining 180 degrees during return to the abdomen. Ninety degrees counterclockwise is only the herniation phase, and clockwise rotation is abnormal. A full 360 degrees overshoots that total. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the stomach rotates 90 degrees clockwise during the 4th to 6th weeks, so the left vagus comes to lie anteriorly (First Aid Gastrointestinal, normal embryology). COMLEX: the jejunum and ileum coil during rotation, whereas the large intestine lengthens without coiling (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"A full 360 degrees overshoots the normal total.","A":"Langman calls a 90-degree clockwise turn reversed rotation, which places the transverse colon behind the duodenum and the superior mesenteric artery.","C":"Ninety degrees counterclockwise is only the herniation phase.","B":"Clockwise rotation is abnormal."}},{"id":"sdl21_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure forms the axis of rotation of the primary intestinal loop?","choices":{"A":"Celiac arterial trunk","B":"Vitelline duct and yolk stalk","C":"Superior mesenteric artery","D":"Inferior mesenteric artery","E":"Left umbilical vein remnant"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The superior mesenteric artery forms the axis about which the primary intestinal loop rotates. The celiac trunk and inferior mesenteric artery supply the foregut and hindgut, and the vitelline duct connects the loop apex to the yolk sac. The umbilical vein runs in the cord to the liver and is not the pivot of the midgut loop. The celiac artery supplies the foregut and is not the axis of the primary intestinal loop. The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the hindgut, whereas the midgut loop rotates around the superior mesenteric artery. Moore states the returning midgut rotates 270 degrees around the axis of the SMA, while the omphaloenteric duct only attaches it to the umbilical vesicle. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cephalic limb of the primary loop forms the distal duodenum, jejunum, and part of the ileum (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the dorsal mesentery twists around the origin of this artery as the caudal limb moves to the right (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The umbilical vein runs in the cord to the liver and is not the pivot of the midgut loop.","A":"The celiac artery supplies the foregut and is not the axis of the primary intestinal loop.","D":"The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the hindgut, whereas the midgut loop rotates around the superior mesenteric artery.","B":"Moore states the returning midgut rotates 270 degrees around the axis of the SMA, while the omphaloenteric duct only attaches it to the umbilical vesicle."}},{"id":"sdl21_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":1,"stem":"During which week do the herniated intestinal loops begin to return to the abdominal cavity?","choices":{"A":"Fourth week","B":"Sixth week","C":"Eighth week","D":"Sixteenth week","E":"Tenth week"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Herniated loops begin to return during the 10th week, aided by regression of the mesonephros, slower liver growth, and abdominal cavity expansion. Herniation begins in the 6th week, and the return is finished by about the 12th week. In the fourth week the loops have not yet herniated; physiologic herniation does not begin until the 6th week. In the eighth week the loops are still herniated in the cord; retraction into the abdomen starts at the 10th week. By the sixteenth week the loops have long since returned, since retraction is complete by the 10th to 12th week. The 6th week is when the loops enter the extraembryonic cavity of the cord, not when they begin to return. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the proximal jejunum returns first and settles on the left side (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the cecal bud returns last and then descends into the right iliac fossa (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"In the fourth week the loops have not yet herniated; physiologic herniation does not begin until the 6th week.","C":"In the eighth week the loops are still herniated in the cord; retraction into the abdomen starts at the 10th week.","D":"By the sixteenth week the loops have long since returned, since retraction is complete by the 10th to 12th week.","B":"The 6th week is when the loops enter the extraembryonic cavity of the cord, not when they begin to return."}},{"id":"sdl21_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":1,"stem":"Which covering surrounds the herniated viscera in an omphalocele?","choices":{"A":"Skin","B":"Dura","C":"Amnion","D":"Chorion","E":"Pleura"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In omphalocele, the viscera herniate through an enlarged umbilical ring and are covered by a membrane of amnion and peritoneum separated by Wharton's jelly, which gives the sac a smooth, spherical look on ultrasound. Skin covers an umbilical hernia, and gastroschisis has no covering membrane. Dura is a meningeal covering of the central nervous system and plays no part in the omphalocele sac, which is amnion and peritoneum with Wharton's jelly. Skin does not cover an omphalocele; Langman describes the herniated viscera as covered by amnion. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes the omphalocele sac as peritoneum plus amnion, light gray and shiny (First Aid Gastrointestinal, ventral wall defects). COMLEX: omphalocele occurs in about 2.5 per 10,000 births with about 25% mortality (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Dura is a meningeal covering of the central nervous system and plays no part in the omphalocele sac, which is amnion and peritoneum with Wharton's jelly.","D":"Chorion is not part of the omphalocele sac.","E":"Pleura lines the thoracic cavity, whereas the omphalocele membrane.","A":"Skin does not cover an omphalocele."}},{"id":"sdl21_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":1,"stem":"During which week do midgut loops normally enter the umbilical cord as physiologic herniation?","choices":{"A":"Third week","B":"Fourth week","C":"Twelfth week","D":"Sixth week","E":"Twentieth week"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Rapid elongation of the loop and growth of the liver make the abdomen temporarily too small, so loops enter the extraembryonic cavity of the cord during the 6th week. They return beginning in the 10th week. The third week is too early; herniation into the cord is driven by midgut elongation and liver growth beginning in the 6th week. The fourth week precedes physiologic herniation, which begins in the 6th week. By the twelfth week the loops have finished returning to the abdomen rather than entering the cord. By the twentieth week bowel in the cord is abnormal, because loops normally return by the 10th to 12th week. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: coiling of the small intestinal loops and formation of the cecum occur while the gut is herniated (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: physiologic herniation persists until about the 10th week, so bowel in the cord base before then is normal (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The third week is too early.","B":"The fourth week precedes physiologic herniation, which begins in the 6th week.","C":"By the twelfth week the loops have finished returning to the abdomen rather than entering the cord.","E":"By the twentieth week bowel in the cord is abnormal, because loops normally return by the 10th to 12th week."}},{"id":"sdl21_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is the abdominal wall defect of gastroschisis usually located?","choices":{"A":"Through the enlarged umbilical ring itself","B":"Lateral to the umbilicus on the right","C":"Lateral to the umbilicus on the left side","D":"In the upper epigastric midline above it","E":"Just above the pubic symphysis in midline"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gastroschisis occurs lateral to the umbilicus, usually on the right, from abnormal body wall closure around the connecting stalk. Herniation through the umbilical ring itself is omphalocele. A suprapubic defect suggests bladder exstrophy. Gastroschisis is lateral to the umbilicus but usually on the right, not the left. Gastroschisis is a defect beside the umbilicus, not an upper epigastric midline defect. Herniation through an enlarged umbilical ring is omphalocele, whereas gastroschisis protrudes through the body wall lateral to the umbilicus. Langman attributes a midline suprapubic defect to bladder exstrophy from failed body wall closure in the pelvic region, whereas gastroschisis lies right of the umbilicus. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastroschisis is a lateral fold closure defect, while bladder exstrophy is a caudal fold defect (First Aid Gastrointestinal, ventral wall defects). COMLEX: gastroschisis is increasing in frequency, especially in babies of mothers under 20 (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Gastroschisis is lateral to the umbilicus but usually on the right, not the left.","D":"Gastroschisis is a defect beside the umbilicus, not an upper epigastric midline defect.","A":"Herniation through an enlarged umbilical ring is omphalocele, whereas gastroschisis protrudes through the body wall lateral to the umbilicus.","E":"Langman attributes a midline suprapubic defect to bladder exstrophy from failed body wall closure in the pelvic region, whereas gastroschisis lies right of the umbilicus."}},{"id":"sdl21_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"Meckel diverticulum results from persistence of which embryonic structure?","choices":{"A":"Urachal remnant","B":"Allantoic stalk","C":"Vitelline duct","D":"Cloacal membrane","E":"Umbilical vein"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A small persisting portion of the vitelline duct forms an outpocketing of the ileum, Meckel diverticulum. A persisting urachus comes from the allantois and drains urine. The cloacal membrane and umbilical vein are not related to the ileum. The allantois gives rise to the urachus and bladder region, not to an ileal outpouching. The cloacal membrane marks the caudal end of the hindgut and does not form an outpocketing of the ileum. Langman describes the urachus as the obliterated allantois linking the bladder apex to the umbilicus, and a patent lumen drains urine. Moore describes the ileal (Meckel) diverticulum as a remnant of the proximal embryonic omphaloenteric duct (yolk stalk), not the umbilical vein. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum is the most common congenital anomaly of the GI tract (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: the diverticulum usually causes no symptoms in adults (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The allantois gives rise to the urachus and bladder region, not to an ileal outpouching.","D":"The cloacal membrane marks the caudal end of the hindgut and does not form an outpocketing of the ileum.","A":"Langman describes the urachus as the obliterated allantois linking the bladder apex to the umbilicus, and a patent lumen drains urine.","E":"Moore describes the ileal (Meckel) diverticulum as a remnant of the proximal embryonic omphaloenteric duct (yolk stalk), not the umbilical vein."}},{"id":"sdl21_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"Along which border of the ileum does Meckel diverticulum arise?","choices":{"A":"Antimesenteric border","B":"Mesenteric border","C":"Duodenojejunal border","D":"Retroperitoneal border","E":"Ileocecal valve border"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Meckel diverticulum lies on the antimesenteric border of the ileum, about 40 to 60 cm from the ileocecal valve. It does not arise from the mesenteric border, the duodenojejunal flexure, or the ileocecal valve itself. The ileum bearing a Meckel diverticulum is intraperitoneal on its mesentery; the diverticulum arises on the antimesenteric border, not a retroperitoneal one. Langman places Meckel diverticulum on the antimesenteric border of the ileum, not the mesenteric border. Langman locates Meckel diverticulum on the ileum about 40 to 60 cm from the ileocecal valve, far from the duodenojejunal junction. Meckel diverticulum lies about 40 to 60 cm proximal to the ileocecal valve in Langman, not at the valve itself. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the rule of 2s lists 2 feet from the ileocecal valve and 2 inches long (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: Meckel diverticulum is twice as likely in males (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The ileum bearing a Meckel diverticulum is intraperitoneal on its mesentery; the diverticulum arises on the antimesenteric border, not a retroperitoneal one.","B":"Langman places Meckel diverticulum on the antimesenteric border of the ileum, not the mesenteric border.","C":"Langman locates Meckel diverticulum on the ileum about 40 to 60 cm from the ileocecal valve, far from the duodenojejunal junction.","E":"Meckel diverticulum lies about 40 to 60 cm proximal to the ileocecal valve in Langman, not at the valve itself."}},{"id":"sdl21_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"What anomaly results when the omphalomesenteric duct stays patent along its entire length?","choices":{"A":"Vitelline cyst","B":"Urachal sinus tract","C":"Duplication cyst","D":"Omphalocele sac","E":"Vitelline fistula"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A duct that stays patent throughout forms a vitelline, or umbilical, fistula connecting the ileal lumen with the umbilicus, so feces may drain at the umbilicus. A vitelline cyst forms when only the middle part persists between two fibrous cords. A urachal sinus is an allantoic remnant draining toward the bladder, not a patent omphalomesenteric duct reaching the ileum. Duplication cysts are abnormal proliferations of gut parenchyma, most often near the ileum, unrelated to a patent vitelline duct. An omphalocele sac is failed return of herniated loops through an enlarged umbilical ring, not a patent vitelline duct. A vitelline cyst forms when both ends of the duct become fibrous cords and only the middle persists, so it cannot drain feces to the umbilicus. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: umbilical drainage of feces points to a patent omphalomesenteric duct, whereas urine drainage points to a patent urachus (Langman Ch. 16). COMLEX: the vitelline duct connects the apex of the primary loop with the yolk sac (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A urachal sinus is an allantoic remnant draining toward the bladder, not a patent omphalomesenteric duct reaching the ileum.","C":"Duplication cysts are abnormal proliferations of gut parenchyma, most often near the ileum, unrelated to a patent vitelline duct.","D":"An omphalocele sac is failed return of herniated loops through an enlarged umbilical ring.","A":"A vitelline cyst forms when both ends of the duct become fibrous cords and only the middle persists, so it cannot drain feces to the umbilicus."}},{"id":"sdl21_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":5,"stem":"In which intestinal segment do most atresias and stenoses occur?","choices":{"A":"Jejunum","B":"Distal ileum","C":"Appendix","D":"Duodenum","E":"Colon"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Most atresias and stenoses occur in the duodenum, the fewest occur in the colon, and the jejunum and ileum have equal numbers (about 1 in 1500 births). The appendix is not a usual atresia site. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal atresia is associated with Down syndrome and shows a double bubble on x-ray (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia). COMLEX: atresias account for 95% of these defects and stenoses for only 5% (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman"},{"id":"sdl21_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"Which mechanism most likely causes atresia of the upper duodenum, just distal to the stomach?","choices":{"A":"Lack of recanalization","B":"Vascular accident","C":"Midgut volvulus","D":"Amniotic fluid injury","E":"Persistent vitelline cord"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Upper duodenal atresias are probably due to failure of the solid duodenal lumen to recanalize. Distal to the duodenum, atresias have been attributed to vascular accidents, which can follow volvulus, malrotation, or abdominal wall defects. Exposure of bowel to amniotic fluid injures bare loops in gastroschisis but does not cause upper duodenal atresia. A persistent vitelline cord causes obstruction by loops twisting around it, not upper duodenal atresia. Langman attributes atresias from the distal duodenum caudally, not the upper duodenum, to vascular accidents that compromise blood flow. Langman attributes atresias from the distal duodenum caudally to vascular accidents that may follow volvulus, whereas upper duodenal atresias reflect a lack of recanalization. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intestinal atresia presents with bilious vomiting and distension within the first 1 to 2 days of life (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia). COMLEX: the duodenal lumen is temporarily obliterated by epithelial proliferation before recanalizing (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Exposure of bowel to amniotic fluid injures bare loops in gastroschisis but does not cause upper duodenal atresia.","E":"A persistent vitelline cord causes obstruction by loops twisting around it, not upper duodenal atresia.","B":"A vascular accident accounts for atresias caudal to the upper duodenum, not atresia just distal to the stomach.","C":"Midgut volvulus is not the developmental mechanism of atresia of the upper duodenum."}},{"id":"sdl21_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"Misexpression of which gene families supports a non-vascular, differentiation-based cause of intestinal atresia?","choices":{"A":"RET and GDNF family receptor genes","B":"HOX genes and FGF family genes","C":"SHH and patched receptor genes","D":"PAX and SOX transcription factor genes","E":"WNT and BMP signaling pathway genes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Misexpression of HOX genes and of FGF family ligands and receptors disrupts epithelial-mesenchymal signaling and causes gut atresias, showing that faulty gut differentiation, not only vascular accidents, can produce these defects. RET mutations cause aganglionic megacolon. SHH from endoderm induces HOX expression in mesoderm for craniocaudal patterning; the notes tie atresia specifically to HOX and FGF misexpression. SOX2 and PAX genes specify regions and cell types (SOX2 the esophagus and stomach, PAX4/PAX6 pancreatic endocrine cells); gut atresia is tied to HOX and FGF misexpression instead. WNT and BMP4 signals are named for somite and muscle patterning (together they activate MyoD), whereas gut atresia is tied to HOX and FGF misexpression. RET mutations affect neural crest migration and cause aganglionic megacolon, not gut atresias. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: RET mutations impair neural crest migration and cause Hirschsprung disease (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: in 50% of atresias a region of bowel is lost, and in 20% a fibrous cord remains (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"SHH from endoderm induces HOX expression in mesoderm for craniocaudal patterning; the notes tie atresia specifically to HOX and FGF misexpression.","D":"SOX2 and PAX genes specify regions and cell types (SOX2 the esophagus and stomach, PAX4/PAX6 pancreatic endocrine cells); gut atresia is tied to HOX and FGF misexpression instead.","E":"WNT and BMP4 signals are named for somite and muscle patterning (together they activate MyoD), whereas gut atresia is tied to HOX and FGF misexpression.","A":"RET mutations affect neural crest migration and cause aganglionic megacolon, not gut atresias."}},{"id":"sdl21_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":1,"stem":"Which germ layer forms the epithelium lining the small intestine?","choices":{"A":"Ectoderm","B":"Neural crest","C":"Somatic layer","D":"Paraxial layer","E":"Endoderm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The epithelium of the digestive system and the parenchyma of its derivatives originate in endoderm. Connective tissue, muscle, and peritoneal components come from mesoderm. Ectoderm forms only the caudal anal canal. Neural crest forms enteric ganglia, not the mucosal epithelium, which derives from gut tube endoderm. Somatic mesoderm forms the body wall; gut wall mesoderm is the splanchnic layer, and the epithelium itself is endoderm. Paraxial mesoderm forms somites, not the intestinal epithelium, which is endodermal. Ectoderm contributes only the lower third of the anal canal, so it is not the source of the gut epithelium. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the caudal third of the anal canal is ectodermal and is supplied by the inferior rectal artery (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: gut differentiation depends on reciprocal epithelial-mesenchymal interactions (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Neural crest forms enteric ganglia, not the mucosal epithelium, which derives from gut tube endoderm.","C":"Somatic mesoderm forms the body wall; gut wall mesoderm is the splanchnic layer, and the epithelium itself is endoderm.","D":"Paraxial mesoderm forms somites, not the intestinal epithelium, which is endodermal.","A":"Ectoderm contributes only the lower third of the anal canal, so it is not the source of the gut epithelium."}},{"id":"sdl21_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":1,"stem":"Which embryonic tissue forms the smooth muscle of the small intestinal wall?","choices":{"A":"Splanchnic mesoderm","B":"Gut tube endoderm","C":"Surface ectoderm","D":"Paraxial mesoderm","E":"Somatic body wall mesoderm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The muscular and connective tissue components of the gut come from mesoderm, specifically the splanchnic (visceral) layer surrounding the endodermal tube. Endoderm forms the epithelium. Somatic and paraxial mesoderm form body wall and somite derivatives. Surface ectoderm forms the skin epidermis and only the caudal anal canal, not intestinal smooth muscle. Endoderm forms the epithelial lining and gland parenchyma, while muscle, connective tissue, and peritoneal components of the gut wall come from visceral mesoderm. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: endodermal sonic hedgehog induces HOX gene expression in the surrounding gut mesoderm (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: visceral peritoneum covers the gut while parietal peritoneum lines the body wall (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Surface ectoderm forms the skin epidermis and only the caudal anal canal, not intestinal smooth muscle.","B":"Endoderm forms the epithelial lining and gland parenchyma, while muscle, connective tissue, and peritoneal components of the gut wall come from visceral mesoderm."}},{"id":"sdl21_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":5,"stem":"Which structure is the most distal derivative of the midgut?","choices":{"A":"Distal third of the transverse colon and flexure","B":"Proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon","C":"Descending colon at the splenic flexure region","D":"Terminal ileum at the ileocecal valve junction","E":"Upper anal canal above the pectinate line"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The midgut ends at the junction of the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon with the distal third. The distal third of the transverse colon, the descending colon, and the upper anal canal are hindgut. The terminal ileum is proximal to the end. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the midgut receives vagal parasympathetic innervation and lies at the L1 vertebral level of its artery (First Aid Gastrointestinal, blood supply and innervation). COMLEX: the caudal limb of the primary loop forms the lower ileum, cecum, appendix, and ascending colon (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman"},{"id":"sdl21_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-day-old boy has sudden bilious vomiting after initially passing meconium. The pregnancy was uneventful and he was born at term. The abdomen is mildly distended, bowel sounds are high-pitched, and the extremities are warm with brisk capillary refill. Serum lactate is 1.1 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). An upper gastrointestinal series shows the small bowel on the right, and a contrast enema shows the cecum and colon on the left with the ileum entering the cecum from the right. Which developmental error best explains this anatomy?","choices":{"A":"Clockwise rotation of the primary loop","B":"Complete rotation with failed colonic fixation","C":"Failure of the loops to leave the abdomen","D":"Persistent attachment of the omphalomesenteric duct","E":"Rotation that stopped near 90 degrees"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When rotation stops near 90 degrees, the colon and cecum return first and settle on the left, and later loops move to the right. Right-sided small bowel, a left-sided colon, and an ileum entering the cecum from the right match this incomplete turn. Clockwise rotation places the transverse colon behind the duodenum. Failed colonic fixation leaves a mobile colon in its usual right-sided place. Loops that never leave the abdomen skip herniation and cannot produce this left-sided colon. Persistent omphalomesenteric duct attachment produces an ileal diverticulum or umbilical fistula.","boardPrep":"Step 1: malrotation leaves the small bowel clumped on the right and the colon on the left, with fibrous Ladd bands (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malrotation). COMLEX: malrotation predisposes to volvulus with compromised blood supply (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Clockwise rotation places the transverse colon behind the duodenum","B":"Failed colonic fixation leaves a mobile colon in its usual right-sided place","C":"Loops that never leave the abdomen skip herniation and cannot produce this left-sided colon","D":"Persistent omphalomesenteric duct attachment produces an ileal diverticulum or umbilical fistula"}},{"id":"sdl21_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-week-old girl suddenly develops bilious vomiting, abdominal distension, and bloody stools. An upper gastrointestinal study in the first week of life showed the duodenojejunal junction to the right of the spine. She is lethargic, the abdomen is distended and tender, and the extremities are cool. Heart rate is 190/min, blood pressure is 58/32 mm Hg, and capillary refill is 4 seconds. Serum lactate is 6.2 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). Which complication most likely threatens her bowel?","choices":{"A":"Telescoping of ileum into the cecum","B":"Twisting that compromises blood flow","C":"Spasm of an aganglionic colonic segment","D":"Ulceration of ectopic gastric mucosa","E":"Plugging of the ileum by thick meconium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The right-sided duodenojejunal junction marks malrotation, and the sudden shock, bloody stools, and lactate of 6.2 mmol/L mean the bowel has twisted and lost its blood flow. Telescoping of ileum into the cecum causes episodic pain and a right-sided mass. Spasm of an aganglionic colonic segment is the mechanism of Hirschsprung disease. Ulceration of ectopic gastric mucosa causes painless bleeding in a well-appearing child. Plugging of the ileum by thick meconium occurs in newborns with cystic fibrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: midgut volvulus is more common in infants, whereas sigmoid volvulus is more common in older adults (First Aid Gastrointestinal, volvulus). COMLEX: in gastroschisis, volvulus with compromised blood supply may kill large regions of bowel (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Telescoping of ileum into the cecum causes episodic pain and a right-sided mass","C":"Spasm of an aganglionic colonic segment is the mechanism of Hirschsprung disease","D":"Ulceration of ectopic gastric mucosa causes painless bleeding in a well-appearing child","E":"Plugging of the ileum by thick meconium occurs in newborns with cystic fibrosis"}},{"id":"sdl21_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has left flank pain and one episode of gross hematuria. She has never had abdominal surgery. The abdomen is soft, with left costovertebral angle tenderness and normal bowel sounds. CT shows a 4 mm left ureteral stone and, incidentally, the transverse colon passing posterior to the duodenum and behind the superior mesenteric artery. Which embryologic event best explains the colonic position?","choices":{"A":"Clockwise rotation of about 90 degrees","B":"Counterclockwise rotation of about 90 degrees","C":"Failed posterior attachment of the ascending colon","D":"Arrested descent of the cecal bud from the liver","E":"Return of the colon before the proximal jejunum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Reversed rotation is a clockwise turn of about 90 degrees that places the transverse colon behind the duodenum and the superior mesenteric artery. The incidental CT course of the colon posterior to both structures is that event. A short counterclockwise turn leaves the colon and cecum on the left side. Failed posterior attachment of the ascending colon leaves that colon mobile. Arrested descent of the cecal bud leaves the cecum high under the liver. Colon returning before jejunum is the pattern of a left-sided colon.","boardPrep":"Step 1: after normal rotation, the transverse colon passes in front of the duodenum (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the cecal bud lies temporarily in the right upper quadrant below the liver before descending (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A short counterclockwise turn leaves the colon and cecum on the left side","C":"Failed posterior attachment of the ascending colon leaves that colon mobile","D":"Arrested descent of the cecal bud leaves the cecum high under the liver","E":"Colon returning before jejunum is the pattern of a left-sided colon"}},{"id":"sdl21_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has a routine ultrasound at 20 weeks of gestation. She takes a prenatal vitamin and her fundal height matches dates. Examination is otherwise unremarkable. Ultrasound shows a smooth membrane-bound midline sac at the cord insertion that contains both bowel and liver. Targeted views of the fetal heart have not yet been obtained. Which associated finding should next be sought?","choices":{"A":"Renal agenesis","B":"Cardiac anomaly","C":"Hip dysplasia","D":"Cleft palate","E":"Clubfoot deformity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Omphalocele is a smooth membrane-bound sac of bowel and liver at the cord insertion, and it is associated with cardiac anomalies in about half of live-born infants. Neural tube defects and chromosomal anomalies are the other classic companions, so the heart is the finding to seek next. Renal agenesis is not part of the anomaly cluster linked to this midline sac. Hip dysplasia is a mechanical joint problem and is unrelated to this cord sac. Cleft palate results from failed palatal fusion and is a craniofacial event. Clubfoot is a positional foot deformity and is unrelated to this membrane sac.","boardPrep":"Step 1: omphalocele is associated with trisomies 13 and 18 and Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome (First Aid Gastrointestinal, ventral wall defects). COMLEX: omphalocele arises when bowel fails to return from physiologic herniation during weeks 6 to 10 (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Renal agenesis is not part of the anomaly cluster linked to this midline sac","C":"Hip dysplasia is a mechanical joint problem and is unrelated to this cord sac","D":"Cleft palate results from failed palatal fusion and is a craniofacial event","E":"Clubfoot is a positional foot deformity and is unrelated to this membrane sac"}},{"id":"sdl21_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy, born to an 18-year-old mother with limited prenatal care, has bowel outside the abdomen. She used cocaine in the second trimester. Thickened, uncovered loops protrude through a small defect just to the right of a normally inserted cord, and no other anomalies are found. The loops are matted and edematous. Which developmental error most likely caused this defect?","choices":{"A":"Failure of loops to return from the umbilical cord","B":"Persistence of a patent omphalomesenteric duct","C":"Delayed closure of the umbilical ring after birth","D":"Abnormal closure of the body wall around the stalk","E":"Failed recanalization of the solid gut lumen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastroschisis is caused by abnormal closure of the body wall around the connecting stalk, leaving bowel exposed to amniotic fluid. A right paraumbilical defect, a normal cord, no covering, matted loops, and no other anomalies match that error. Failure of loops to return leaves viscera inside a membrane at the cord. A patent omphalomesenteric duct leaks fecal fluid from ileum onto the umbilicus. Delayed umbilical ring closure after birth leaves a hernia covered by skin. Failed recanalization blocks the upper duodenal lumen and does not open the body wall.","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastroschisis is not commonly associated with chromosomal abnormalities and has a good prognosis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, ventral wall defects). COMLEX: gastroschisis occurs in about 1 per 10,000 births (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Failure of loops to return leaves viscera inside a membrane at the cord","B":"A patent omphalomesenteric duct leaks fecal fluid from ileum onto the umbilicus","C":"Delayed umbilical ring closure after birth leaves a hernia covered by skin","E":"Failed recanalization blocks the upper duodenal lumen and does not open the body wall"}},{"id":"sdl21_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman has a dating ultrasound at 9 weeks of gestation. She has had first-trimester nausea but no vaginal bleeding. Examination and fundal size are appropriate for dates. Several bowel loops lie within the base of the umbilical cord. The fetal heart, spine, and limbs look normal. Which interpretation is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Omphalocele with amnion covering","B":"Gastroschisis with uncovered loops","C":"Physiologic midgut herniation","D":"Congenital umbilical hernia","E":"Vitelline cyst at the umbilicus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bowel within the umbilical cord at 9 weeks is physiologic midgut herniation, which is present from the 6th week until return begins in the 10th week. A normal fetal heart, spine, and limbs fit this expected stage of development. An omphalocele is a pathologic sac diagnosed after bowel should already have returned. Gastroschisis loops lie bare beside the umbilicus rather than inside the cord. A congenital umbilical hernia is skin-covered and appears after birth. A vitelline cyst is a dilated middle duct segment between fibrous cords.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a congenital umbilical hernia is covered by skin and often closes spontaneously (First Aid Gastrointestinal, congenital umbilical hernia). COMLEX: herniation occurs because liver growth makes the abdomen temporarily too small (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An omphalocele is a pathologic sac diagnosed after bowel should already have returned","B":"Gastroschisis loops lie bare beside the umbilicus rather than inside the cord","D":"A congenital umbilical hernia is skin-covered and appears after birth","E":"A vitelline cyst is a dilated middle duct segment between fibrous cords"}},{"id":"sdl21_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy suddenly passes a large amount of maroon blood per rectum and then seems comfortable. He has never had abdominal surgery. He is pale, the abdomen is soft and nontender, and capillary refill is brisk. Hemoglobin is 8.2 g/dL (reference range: 11.0–14.0 g/dL) and the heart rate is 130/min. A technetium-99m pertechnetate scan shows a focus of uptake in the right lower quadrant. What most likely caused his bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Acid secretion by ectopic gastric mucosa","B":"Ischemia from an ileocolic intussusception","C":"Angiodysplasia within the cecal mucosal wall","D":"Hyperplasia of the ileal lymphoid follicles","E":"Erosion by a swallowed coin or button battery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Painless maroon bleeding with a pertechnetate-avid right lower quadrant focus is a Meckel diverticulum in which ectopic gastric mucosa secretes acid and ulcerates adjacent ileum. Anemia with a soft nontender abdomen fits that mucosal source of bleeding. Ileocolic intussusception causes crampy pain and usually a palpable abdominal mass. Angiodysplasia of the cecum is a degenerative lesion seen in older adults. Ileal lymphoid hyperplasia does not take up the tracer used in this scan. A swallowed button battery causes focal caustic injury and is not tracer avid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pertechnetate scan is called a Meckel scan because it detects heterotopic gastric mucosa (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: heterotopic pancreatic tissue in the diverticulum can also cause ulceration (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Ileocolic intussusception causes crampy pain and usually a palpable abdominal mass","C":"Angiodysplasia of the cecum is a degenerative lesion seen in older adults","D":"Ileal lymphoid hyperplasia does not take up the tracer used in this scan","E":"A swallowed button battery causes focal caustic injury and is not tracer avid"}},{"id":"sdl21_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl has colicky pain, bilious vomiting, and abdominal distension. She has never had abdominal surgery, and a sweat test in infancy was normal. The abdomen is distended, bowel sounds are high-pitched, and perfusion is preserved. Serum lactate is 1.0 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). At surgery a cyst lies between the ileum and the umbilicus, fixed by fibrous cords, with a loop of ileum wrapped around one cord. Which mechanism caused her obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Mucosal ulceration with free perforation","B":"Volvulus of loops on the vitelline ligaments","C":"Telescoping of the ileum into the ascending colon","D":"Aganglionic spasm of a distal colonic segment","E":"Inspissated meconium filling the ileal lumen"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A cyst between the ileum and the umbilicus, tethered by fibrous cords with a loop wrapped on a cord, is a vitelline cyst causing volvulus on those ligaments. Colicky pain, bilious vomiting, and distension with a normal lactate are mechanical obstruction from that wrap. Mucosal ulceration with perforation follows acid from ectopic tissue in a diverticulum. Telescoping into the ascending colon is intussusception driven by an intraluminal lead point. Aganglionic spasm is Hirschsprung disease and affects a distal colonic segment. Inspissated meconium plugs the ileum of a newborn with cystic fibrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum can cause intussusception, volvulus, or obstruction near the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: a vitelline cyst is also called an enterocystoma (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mucosal ulceration with perforation follows acid from ectopic tissue in a diverticulum","C":"Telescoping into the ascending colon is intussusception driven by an intraluminal lead point","D":"Aganglionic spasm is Hirschsprung disease and affects a distal colonic segment","E":"Inspissated meconium plugs the ileum of a newborn with cystic fibrosis"}},{"id":"sdl21_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has 12 hours of right lower quadrant pain and fever. He has never had abdominal surgery. Temperature is 38.2°C and there is focal right lower quadrant tenderness. The leukocyte count is 14,000/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL). Laparoscopy shows a normal appendix and an inflamed 5 cm outpouching of ileum about 50 cm from the ileocecal valve, and the wall contains mucosa, submucosa, and muscularis propria. How is this outpouching best classified?","choices":{"A":"Duplication cyst","B":"False diverticulum","C":"Pulsion pseudodiverticulum","D":"Pancreatic rest","E":"True diverticulum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"An outpouching that contains mucosa, submucosa, and muscularis propria is a true diverticulum and is a persistent vitelline duct remnant. The inflamed 5 cm ileal lesion about 50 cm from the ileocecal valve has every one of those wall layers. A duplication cyst is a separate tubular or cystic structure alongside the bowel. A false diverticulum contains only mucosa and submucosa pushed through a wall defect. A pulsion pseudodiverticulum is acquired and lacks a muscular coat. A pancreatic rest is a nodule of ectopic pancreas embedded in the wall.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum commonly presents in the first 2 years of life (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: about 2% of people have a Meckel diverticulum (lecture notes; Langman gives 2% to 4%).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A duplication cyst is a separate tubular or cystic structure alongside the bowel","B":"A false diverticulum contains only mucosa and submucosa pushed through a wall defect","C":"A pulsion pseudodiverticulum is acquired and lacks a muscular coat","D":"A pancreatic rest is a nodule of ectopic pancreas embedded in the wall"}},{"id":"sdl21_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old girl has bilious vomiting and abdominal distension. Her mother used cocaine throughout pregnancy. The abdomen is distended, bowel sounds are high-pitched, and capillary refill is brisk. Serum lactate is 1.2 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). A radiograph shows several dilated proximal loops and a gasless pelvis, and surgery shows a blind proximal jejunum separated from distal bowel by a V-shaped mesenteric gap. Which mechanism best explains this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Ischemic necrosis of a fetal intestinal segment","B":"Failure of solid-cord recanalization of the lumen","C":"Traction by a persistent vitelline fibrous cord","D":"Exposure of loops to amniotic fluid through a defect","E":"Rotation that stopped near 90 degrees of turning"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A blind proximal jejunum separated by a V-shaped mesenteric gap is ischemic necrosis of a fetal bowel segment after a vascular accident. Maternal cocaine use, bilious vomiting, and dilated proximal loops with a gasless pelvis fit that in utero loss of a segment. Failure of solid-cord recanalization explains a web in the upper duodenum. A persistent vitelline cord obstructs when loops twist around the tether. Amniotic exposure damages bare loops in gastroschisis, and no wall defect is present. Rotation that stops near 90 degrees leaves the colon on the left side.","boardPrep":"Step 1: jejunal and ileal atresia are associated with cystic fibrosis and gastroschisis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia). COMLEX: maternal tobacco smoking is a risk factor for jejunal and ileal atresia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Failure of solid-cord recanalization explains a web in the upper duodenum","C":"A persistent vitelline cord obstructs when loops twist around the tether","D":"Amniotic exposure damages bare loops in gastroschisis, and no wall defect is present","E":"Rotation that stops near 90 degrees leaves the colon on the left side"}},{"id":"sdl21_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy born at 32 weeks, weighing 1500 g, has bilious vomiting. The pregnancy was not complicated by vasoconstrictor exposure. The abdomen is distended and bowel sounds are tinkling. A radiograph shows a few dilated proximal loops and a gasless pelvis. At surgery the proximal jejunum ends blindly and the short distal small bowel is coiled around a thin mesenteric remnant. Which defect is present?","choices":{"A":"Membranous duodenal web","B":"Annular pancreas","C":"Apple peel atresia","D":"Multiple segmental atresias","E":"Midgut duplication cyst"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Apple peel atresia accounts for about 10 percent of atresias and consists of a blind proximal jejunum with shortened distal bowel coiled around a mesenteric remnant. Prematurity, low birth weight, and this helical distal bowel on a thin mesentery are that defect. A membranous web is a thin diaphragm inside bowel that remains in continuity. Annular pancreas is a ring of ventral bud tissue constricting the duodenum. Multiple segmental atresias are separate gaps rather than one helical distal bowel. A duplication cyst is a separate structure, most often near the ileum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: effects of an atresia depend on how much bowel is damaged and where it lies (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: a thin diaphragm separating larger and smaller pieces of bowel accounts for about 20% of atresias (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A membranous web is a thin diaphragm inside bowel that remains in continuity","B":"Annular pancreas is a ring of ventral bud tissue constricting the duodenum","D":"Multiple segmental atresias are separate gaps rather than one helical distal bowel","E":"A duplication cyst is a separate structure, most often near the ileum"}},{"id":"sdl21_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-day-old boy has bilious vomiting. An older sister required surgery for the same intestinal defect, and the pregnancy included no cocaine or other vasoconstrictors. The abdomen is distended and bowel sounds are high-pitched. At surgery there are several separate ileal atresias, the mesenteric vessels are intact, and there is no malrotation or volvulus. Which mechanism most likely explains his atresias?","choices":{"A":"Intrauterine volvulus with segment infarction","B":"Failed recanalization of the solid lumen","C":"Maternal vasoconstrictive drug exposure","D":"Faulty gene-directed gut differentiation","E":"Amniotic injury through an abdominal wall defect"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Several ileal atresias with intact vessels, no volvulus, and an affected sibling are due to faulty gene-directed gut differentiation, including misexpression of developmental genes and growth factor receptors. The sister's similar history and the intact mesenteric vessels are the clues in this newborn. Intrauterine volvulus infarcts bowel only after mesenteric vessels are occluded. Failed recanalization is the mechanism of atresia confined to the upper duodenum. Maternal vasoconstrictive drugs produce atresia by interrupting mesenteric blood flow. Amniotic injury requires bare bowel outside an abdominal wall defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: stenoses and multiple atresias each account for about 5% of these defects (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: about 33% of intestinal duplications are associated with other defects such as atresias (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Intrauterine volvulus infarcts bowel only after mesenteric vessels are occluded","B":"Failed recanalization is the mechanism of atresia confined to the upper duodenum","C":"Maternal vasoconstrictive drugs produce atresia by interrupting mesenteric blood flow","E":"Amniotic injury requires bare bowel outside an abdominal wall defect"}},{"id":"sdl21_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":5,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with atrial fibrillation has sudden severe periumbilical pain. He stopped warfarin three days ago after a fall. The rhythm is irregularly irregular, the abdomen is soft, and the pain far exceeds the tenderness. Serum lactate is 5.4 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). CT angiography shows an embolus occluding the superior mesenteric artery just beyond its origin. Which bowel segment is most likely to be spared from ischemia?","choices":{"A":"Proximal jejunum","B":"Terminal ileum","C":"Descending colon","D":"Cecum and appendix","E":"Ascending colon"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The descending colon is spared because it is a hindgut derivative supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery. The embolus blocks the superior mesenteric artery, and pain out of proportion with a lactate of 5.4 mmol/L reflects ischemia in the territory of that vessel. The proximal jejunum lies inside the territory of the occluded arterial trunk. The terminal ileum is perfused by ileal branches of the occluded trunk. The cecum and appendix depend on the ileocolic branch of the occluded trunk. The ascending colon is fed by right colic branches of the occluded trunk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the hindgut extends from the distal third of the transverse colon to the anal canal above the pectinate line (First Aid Gastrointestinal, normal embryology). COMLEX: the left third of the transverse colon is a hindgut derivative (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman","whyNot":{"A":"The proximal jejunum lies inside the territory of the occluded arterial trunk","B":"The terminal ileum is perfused by ileal branches of the occluded trunk","D":"The cecum and appendix depend on the ileocolic branch of the occluded trunk","E":"The ascending colon is fed by right colic branches of the occluded trunk"}},{"id":"sdl21_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has postprandial epigastric pain and a 6 kg weight loss. She has a 40 pack-year smoking history and widespread atherosclerotic disease. The abdomen is soft and nontender, with an epigastric bruit. Angiography shows a high-grade celiac trunk stenosis and a patent superior mesenteric artery, and the distal duodenum remains well perfused. Which landmark marks the embryologic boundary between foregut and midgut territory in her duodenum?","choices":{"A":"Pyloric sphincter","B":"Minor duodenal papilla","C":"Duodenojejunal flexure","D":"First-part duodenal bulb","E":"Major duodenal papilla"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The embryologic boundary between foregut and midgut in the duodenum is the major duodenal papilla, just distal to the entrance of the bile duct. Celiac stenosis with a patent superior mesenteric artery leaves the duodenum past that landmark well perfused. The pyloric sphincter is the gastric outlet and lies in proximal foregut territory. The minor papilla opens the accessory pancreatic duct and is not the regional boundary. The duodenojejunal flexure lies far downstream within established midgut territory. The duodenal bulb is proximal duodenum and stays under celiac arterial supply.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the foregut extends from the esophagus to the duodenum at the level of the ampulla of Vater (First Aid Gastrointestinal, normal embryology). COMLEX: the liver, pancreas, and biliary apparatus grow out of the upper duodenal endoderm (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The pyloric sphincter is the gastric outlet and lies in proximal foregut territory","B":"The minor papilla opens the accessory pancreatic duct and is not the regional boundary","C":"The duodenojejunal flexure lies far downstream within established midgut territory","D":"The duodenal bulb is proximal duodenum and stays under celiac arterial supply"}},{"id":"sdl21_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":5,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy feeds poorly and has a single median orbit. A loss-of-function mutation is found in a secreted signaling gene, and his mother did not have diabetes. The abdomen is soft and the abdominal wall is intact. Imaging also shows malformed intestinal rotation. In the developing gut, which signal from the endoderm patterned the surrounding mesoderm along the craniocaudal axis?","choices":{"A":"Retinoic acid","B":"Wnt signaling","C":"Bone morphogenetic protein 4","D":"Sonic hedgehog","E":"Fibroblast growth factor 10"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gut endoderm secretes sonic hedgehog, which induces HOX genes in the surrounding mesoderm and sets the craniocaudal organization of the gut. Holoprosencephaly with malformed intestinal rotation after loss of a secreted signal fits failure of that endodermal cue. Retinoic acid is a regional gradient and is not secreted by gut endoderm. WNT proteins come from neural tube and limb ectoderm, not from gut endoderm. Bone morphogenetic protein 4 ventralizes mesoderm and blocks neural induction. Fibroblast growth factor 10 acts during budding of the limb and the lung.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HOX genes in gut mesoderm, induced by endodermal signaling, regulate craniocaudal organization of the gut and its derivatives (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: an RA gradient causes region-specific transcription factors along the gut tube (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — signals triggering liver development","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman","whyNot":{"A":"Retinoic acid is a regional gradient and is not secreted by gut endoderm","B":"WNT proteins come from neural tube and limb ectoderm, not from gut endoderm","C":"Bone morphogenetic protein 4 ventralizes mesoderm and blocks neural induction","E":"Fibroblast growth factor 10 acts during budding of the limb and the lung"}},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":3,"stem":"A 6-week-old boy has had intermittent bilious vomiting for 10 days but feeds eagerly between episodes. He passed meconium on the first day of life. He is afebrile, the abdomen is soft and flat, and capillary refill is brisk. Serum lactate is 1.0 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). An upper gastrointestinal series shows the duodenojejunal junction and all small-bowel loops to the right of the midline, and laparotomy shows pink, untwisted bowel with the cecum high in the abdomen. Which finding most likely explains his vomiting?","choices":{"A":"Hypertrophied pyloric circular muscle","B":"Peritoneal bands crossing the duodenum","C":"A fibrous vitelline cord to the umbilicus","D":"A solid duodenal plug never recanalized","E":"Absent ganglion cells in the rectum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Right-sided small bowel and a high cecum indicate malrotation from an incomplete 270 degree counterclockwise turn, and peritoneal bands from the cecum cross and compress the duodenum. Pink untwisted bowel, a normal lactate, and intermittent bilious vomiting fit duodenal compression by those bands. Hypertrophied pyloric muscle blocks the stomach outlet above the entry of bile. A fibrous vitelline cord tethers ileum and would leave the bowel twisted and ischemic. A never-recanalized duodenal plug causes complete obstruction from the first feed. Absent rectal ganglion cells cause distal obstruction with distension and delayed meconium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Malrotation leaves the small bowel on the right and the colon on the left, and its Ladd bands can obstruct the duodenum (First Aid 2025 GI pathology). COMLEX: Bilious vomiting in an infant is treated as malrotation with possible volvulus until imaging proves otherwise.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hypertrophied pyloric muscle blocks the stomach outlet above the entry of bile","C":"A fibrous vitelline cord tethers ileum and would leave the bowel twisted and ischemic","D":"A never-recanalized duodenal plug causes complete obstruction from the first feed","E":"Absent rectal ganglion cells cause distal obstruction with distension and delayed meconium"}},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":3,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman has a routine ultrasound at 18 weeks. She takes a prenatal vitamin, and fundal height matches dates. Free-floating bowel with a ragged outline protrudes through a small defect just to the right of a normally inserted cord, and no membrane surrounds it. The fetal heart and spine look normal. At delivery the protruding loops are thick, edematous, and matted. What most likely caused these bowel wall changes?","choices":{"A":"Pressure from a covering amnion","B":"A chromosomal defect of gut mesoderm","C":"Failed recanalization of the lumen","D":"Direct exposure to amniotic fluid","E":"Torsion around a vitelline cord"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastroschisis leaves bare loops beside a normal cord, and the thick matted wall comes from direct exposure to amniotic fluid. The fetal heart and spine are normal, which fits a defect that is usually isolated. A covering amnion would enclose the viscera in a smooth sac at the cord. Chromosomal defects of mesoderm accompany membrane-covered sacs and not this isolated defect. Failed recanalization narrows the upper duodenal lumen and does not thicken exteriorized bowel. Torsion around a vitelline cord twists loops that remain inside the abdomen.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gastroschisis lies lateral to the umbilicus, usually on the right, with no covering membrane, and the bowel may be damaged by amniotic fluid (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: Unlike omphalocele, gastroschisis is rarely linked to chromosomal defects, so survival is excellent.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A covering amnion would enclose the viscera in a smooth sac at the cord","B":"Chromosomal defects of mesoderm accompany membrane-covered sacs and not this isolated defect","C":"Failed recanalization narrows the upper duodenal lumen and does not thicken exteriorized bowel","E":"Torsion around a vitelline cord twists loops that remain inside the abdomen"}},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":3,"stem":"A 3-week-old boy has a persistently moist umbilicus. He feeds well and voids clear urine in a normal stream. He is afebrile, a small opening sits at the base of the umbilicus, and the abdomen is soft. Yellow-green, stool-like material repeatedly stains the diaper at that opening, and a thin catheter passes easily into it. Contrast injected through the catheter would most likely fill which structure?","choices":{"A":"Urinary bladder","B":"Proximal jejunum","C":"Transverse colon","D":"Sigmoid colon","E":"Distal ileum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Yellow-green stool at the umbilicus is a vitelline fistula from a duct patent along its entire length, and that duct opens into the distal ileum. Contrast injected at the umbilicus therefore fills the distal ileum, about 40 to 60 cm from the ileocecal valve on the antimesenteric border. A urinary bladder connection is urachal and would drain urine rather than stool. The proximal jejunum has no embryonic channel running out to the umbilicus. The transverse colon never communicates with the umbilicus through a yolk-sac duct. The sigmoid colon is hindgut and develops from the cloaca.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A vitelline fistula is a fully patent vitelline duct that links the ileum to the umbilicus and can discharge feces there (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: Fecal drainage from an infant's umbilicus should prompt imaging of a tract to the ileum.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A urinary bladder connection is urachal and would drain urine rather than stool","B":"The proximal jejunum has no embryonic channel running out to the umbilicus","C":"The transverse colon never communicates with the umbilicus through a yolk-sac duct","D":"The sigmoid colon is hindgut and develops from the cloaca"}},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":3,"stem":"A 1-day-old girl vomits after every feed, and the vomitus contains no bile. The pregnancy was complicated by polyhydramnios, and her mother did not use cocaine. The abdomen is scaphoid and bowel sounds are quiet. A radiograph shows two large gas bubbles in the upper abdomen and no gas beyond them. Surgery finds a complete mucosal diaphragm in the upper duodenum just proximal to the major papilla, with a normal wall and normal mesenteric vessels. Which developmental process most likely failed?","choices":{"A":"Perfusion of a fetal intestinal segment","B":"Recanalization of the duodenal lumen","C":"Migration of enteric neural crest","D":"Fusion of the dorsal mesentery","E":"Regression of the vitelline duct"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A complete mucosal diaphragm in the upper duodenum just proximal to the major papilla, with a normal wall and vessels, means recanalization of the duodenal lumen failed. Nonbilious vomiting and two upper-abdominal gas bubbles with no distal gas place the block above the entry of bile. Loss of fetal perfusion destroys a segment of bowel and its mesentery. Failed enteric neural crest migration causes aganglionosis of the distal colon. Fusion of the dorsal mesentery to the posterior wall only fixes bowel position. Regression of the vitelline duct concerns the ileum and the umbilicus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Upper duodenal atresia most likely reflects failed recanalization, whereas atresias from the distal duodenum onward are attributed to vascular accidents or faulty gut differentiation (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: A newborn with a double bubble and no distal gas has complete duodenal obstruction needing surgery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Loss of fetal perfusion destroys a segment of bowel and its mesentery","C":"Failed enteric neural crest migration causes aganglionosis of the distal colon","D":"Fusion of the dorsal mesentery to the posterior wall only fixes bowel position","E":"Regression of the vitelline duct concerns the ileum and the umbilicus"}},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":3,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman has intermittent melena and fatigue. She does not take anticoagulants. She is pale, the abdomen is soft, and the heart rate is 96/min. Hemoglobin is 9.6 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL). A resected jejunal mass is a circumscribed proliferation of bland smooth muscle in the muscularis externa, with intact villi and crypts over it. The tumor's tissue of origin shares its embryonic source with which other component of the jejunal wall?","choices":{"A":"Absorptive cells of the villi","B":"Paneth cells in the crypts","C":"Visceral peritoneum on the loop","D":"Neurons of the myenteric plexus","E":"Epithelium over Peyer patches"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Smooth muscle of the muscularis externa arises from splanchnic mesoderm, which also forms the visceral peritoneum and the connective tissue of the gut wall. This jejunal muscle tumor therefore shares its embryonic source with the visceral peritoneum on the loop. Absorptive cells of the villi are endodermal epithelium of the gut lining. Paneth cells in the crypts are endodermal secretory cells of the epithelium. Neurons of the myenteric plexus migrate in from the neural crest. Epithelium over Peyer patches is endoderm covering lymphoid tissue in the mucosa.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gut epithelium comes from endoderm, smooth muscle, connective tissue, and visceral peritoneum from splanchnic mesoderm, and enteric ganglia from neural crest (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: A bleeding small bowel mass can arise from the muscle wall while the mucosa over it stays intact.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — signals triggering liver development","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Absorptive cells of the villi are endodermal epithelium of the gut lining","B":"Paneth cells in the crypts are endodermal secretory cells of the epithelium","D":"Neurons of the myenteric plexus migrate in from the neural crest","E":"Epithelium over Peyer patches is endoderm covering lymphoid tissue in the mucosa"}},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has 18 hours of anorexia and pain that began at the umbilicus and then moved to the left lower quadrant. She has never had abdominal surgery. Temperature is 38.4°C, there is left lower quadrant tenderness with rebound, and bowel sounds are present. The leukocyte count is 16,000/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL). CT shows an inflamed appendix arising from a cecum in the left lower abdomen, the entire colon to the left of midline, small bowel on the right, and the ileum entering the cecum from the right. During her embryonic development, which part of the gut most likely reentered the abdominal cavity first?","choices":{"A":"Proximal jejunum","B":"Distal ileum","C":"Colon and cecum","D":"Middle jejunal loops","E":"Hindgut segments"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A left-sided colon, right-sided small bowel, and an ileum entering from the right occur when the primary loop rotates only about 90 degrees, so the colon and cecum reenter the abdomen first and settle on the left. Left lower quadrant appendicitis in this adult is that same rotational pattern. The proximal jejunum returns first only after a completed normal rotation. The distal ileum returns late and does not decide which side the colon occupies. Middle jejunal loops follow the proximal bowel during ordinary return. Hindgut segments are not part of the herniated midgut loop.","boardPrep":"Step 1: When the primary loop rotates only 90 degrees, the colon and cecum return first and settle on the left, producing a left-sided colon (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: Appendicitis in a patient with malrotation can cause left-sided pain, so imaging guides the diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The proximal jejunum returns first only after a completed normal rotation","B":"The distal ileum returns late and does not decide which side the colon occupies","D":"Middle jejunal loops follow the proximal bowel during ordinary return","E":"Hindgut segments are not part of the herniated midgut loop"}},{"id":"sdl21_q31","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"Intrauterine vascular compromise causes intestinal atresia in which region?","choices":{"A":"Proximal duodenum, just beyond the stomach","B":"Appendix alone","C":"Bowel distal to the duodenal-jejunal junction","D":"Thoracic esophagus","E":"Body of the stomach"},"correct":"C","explanation":"An intrauterine vascular accident causes atresia distal to the duodenal-jejunal junction. Failed recanalization, not focal ischemia, is the mechanism that closes the proximal duodenum. The appendix is not the segment these vascular atresias occupy. The esophagus is foregut and is outside the bowel segments lost after a fetal vascular accident. The stomach is not the site of atresia from an intrauterine vascular accident.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Failed recanalization, not focal ischemia, is the mechanism that closes the proximal duodenum","B":"The appendix is not the segment these vascular atresias occupy","D":"The esophagus is foregut and is outside the bowel segments lost after a fetal vascular accident","E":"The stomach is not the site of atresia from an intrauterine vascular accident"}},{"id":"sdl21_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":1,"stem":"Which artery supplies the ileum?","choices":{"A":"Superior mesenteric artery","B":"Celiac trunk","C":"Inferior mesenteric artery","D":"Internal pudendal artery","E":"Proper hepatic artery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ileum is midgut and is supplied by the superior mesenteric artery. The celiac trunk supplies the proximal duodenum above the major duodenal papilla, not the ileum. The inferior mesenteric artery supplies hindgut derivatives, not the ileum. The internal pudendal artery supplies the anal canal below the pectinate line, not the ileum. The proper hepatic artery stays in the celiac distribution and does not feed the ileum.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The celiac trunk supplies the proximal duodenum above the major duodenal papilla, not the ileum","C":"The inferior mesenteric artery supplies hindgut derivatives, not the ileum","D":"The internal pudendal artery supplies the anal canal below the pectinate line, not the ileum","E":"The proper hepatic artery stays in the celiac distribution and does not feed the ileum"}},{"id":"sdl21_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-day-old boy has bilious vomiting and a mildly distended abdomen. Prenatal ultrasound showed polyhydramnios, and a contrast study stops in the third portion of the duodenum, past the ampulla. He is afebrile, the abdominal wall is intact, and there is no peritoneal sign. Serum sodium is 137 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L) and bicarbonate is 23 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). The atretic segment lies in the territory of which artery?","choices":{"A":"Inferior mesenteric artery","B":"Internal pudendal artery","C":"Left colic artery","D":"Celiac trunk","E":"Superior mesenteric artery"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Duodenum distal to the major duodenal papilla is midgut and is supplied by the superior mesenteric artery. The inferior mesenteric artery feeds hindgut, which begins farther distal than the third portion of the duodenum. The internal pudendal artery supplies the anal canal below the pectinate line. The left colic artery is an inferior mesenteric branch to the descending colon. The celiac trunk supplies only the duodenum above the major duodenal papilla.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The inferior mesenteric artery feeds hindgut, which begins farther distal than the third portion of the duodenum","B":"The internal pudendal artery supplies the anal canal below the pectinate line","C":"The left colic artery is an inferior mesenteric branch to the descending colon","D":"The celiac trunk supplies only the duodenum above the major duodenal papilla"}},{"id":"sdl21_q34","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old girl vomits after every feed, and the vomitus contains no bile. Pregnancy was complicated by polyhydramnios. The abdomen is soft and not distended, and the abdominal wall is closed. Serum sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L) and bicarbonate is 24 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Imaging shows the blockage proximal to the ampulla. Which artery supplied that duodenal segment in the embryo?","choices":{"A":"Superior mesenteric artery","B":"Celiac trunk","C":"Inferior mesenteric artery","D":"Ileocolic artery","E":"Superior rectal artery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Duodenum proximal to the major duodenal papilla is caudal foregut and is supplied by the celiac trunk. The superior mesenteric artery supplies duodenum only distal to the major duodenal papilla. The inferior mesenteric artery is the hindgut vessel and does not supply the proximal duodenum. The ileocolic artery is a superior mesenteric branch to the ileocecal region. The superior rectal artery continues the inferior mesenteric artery to the upper rectum.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The superior mesenteric artery supplies duodenum only distal to the major duodenal papilla","C":"The inferior mesenteric artery is the hindgut vessel and does not supply the proximal duodenum","D":"The ileocolic artery is a superior mesenteric branch to the ileocecal region","E":"The superior rectal artery continues the inferior mesenteric artery to the upper rectum"}}],"22":[{"id":"sdl22_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":1,"stem":"Which artery supplies the cecum and ascending colon?","choices":{"A":"Celiac arterial trunk","B":"Internal iliac artery","C":"Superior mesenteric artery","D":"Median sacral artery","E":"Splenic artery"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The cecum and ascending colon are midgut derivatives and are supplied by the superior mesenteric artery through its ileocolic and right colic branches. The celiac trunk and splenic artery supply foregut structures, and the internal iliac and median sacral arteries supply pelvic structures rather than midgut colon. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the midgut ends where the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon meet the distal third (First Aid Gastrointestinal, normal embryology). COMLEX: the ileocolic, right colic, and middle colic branches serve the colonic part of this territory (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":1,"stem":"Where along the transverse colon does midgut territory end and hindgut territory begin?","choices":{"A":"Junction of its proximal two-thirds and distal third","B":"Junction of its proximal third and distal two-thirds","C":"Midpoint between the two colic flexures","D":"Hepatic flexure at the right colic angle","E":"Splenic flexure at the left colic angle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The midgut extends to the junction of the proximal two-thirds and distal third of the transverse colon; the distal third belongs to the hindgut. The proximal-third split reverses the proportions, the midpoint is not the boundary, the hepatic flexure lies well within midgut territory, and the boundary lies just before the splenic flexure rather than at it. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: this boundary is why the splenic flexure region is a watershed between superior and inferior mesenteric supply (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI blood supply). COMLEX: hindgut derivatives continue caudally to the pectinate line of the anal canal (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following large intestine structures develops from the midgut?","choices":{"A":"Sigmoid colon","B":"Vermiform appendix","C":"Descending colon","D":"Rectum","E":"Upper anal canal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The midgut forms the cecum, vermiform appendix, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. The sigmoid colon, descending colon, rectum, and upper anal canal all come from the hindgut and receive inferior mesenteric supply. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the appendix forms from the distal end of the cecal bud (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: about half of appendices lie retrocecal or retrocolic because the appendix grows during colonic descent (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":1,"stem":"Which connective tissue layer anchors the ascending and descending colon to the posterior abdominal wall?","choices":{"A":"Scarpa fascia","B":"Camper fascia","C":"Toldt fascia","D":"Denonvilliers fascia","E":"Transversalis fascia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"As the ascending and descending colon settle against the posterior wall, their dorsal mesenteries fuse with the parietal peritoneum through Toldt fascia. Scarpa and Camper fascia are layers of the anterior abdominal subcutaneous tissue, Denonvilliers fascia lies between rectum and prostate, and transversalis fascia lines the inner abdominal wall. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: surgeons mobilize the colon along the white line of Toldt during colectomy (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the mesentery of the sigmoid colon persists, so this segment stays intraperitoneal (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":1,"stem":"Once their dorsal mesenteries fuse with the parietal peritoneum, the ascending and descending colon are classified as which type of structure?","choices":{"A":"Primarily retroperitoneal","B":"Intraperitoneal on a mesentery","C":"Infraperitoneal in the pelvis","D":"Extraperitoneal in the wall","E":"Secondarily retroperitoneal"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The colon first hangs on a dorsal mesentery and becomes secondarily retroperitoneal after fusion with the posterior wall. Primary retroperitoneal organs, such as the kidneys, develop without a mesentery; intraperitoneal describes segments that keep a mesentery, such as the transverse and sigmoid colon; infraperitoneal and extraperitoneal describe pelvic or body wall positions. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the duodenum except its first part and the pancreas except its tail are also secondarily retroperitoneal (First Aid Gastrointestinal, retroperitoneal structures). COMLEX: the transverse mesocolon fuses with the greater omentum rather than the posterior wall (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":1,"stem":"After midgut rotation, which process carries the cecum from the right upper quadrant to the right lower quadrant?","choices":{"A":"Rotation of the stomach","B":"Elongation of the colon","C":"Enlargement of the liver","D":"Regression of the mesonephros","E":"Fusion of the mesocolon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The cecal bud lies first in the right upper quadrant below the liver, and lengthening of the colon carries the cecum and appendix caudally into the right iliac fossa. Gastric rotation shapes the foregut, liver growth and mesonephric regression influence the earlier return of herniated loops, and mesocolic fusion fixes the colon after it is in place. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cecal bud is the last part of the herniated loop to reenter the abdomen (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: a cecum that stays high produces a subhepatic appendix with atypical right upper quadrant pain (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure forms between the anal and urogenital openings after the cloaca is divided?","choices":{"A":"Genital tubercle","B":"Urogenital fold","C":"Anal column","D":"Perineal body","E":"Labioscrotal swelling"},"correct":"D","explanation":"When the cloacal membrane ruptures, the tip of the dividing mesodermal wedge remains between the anal and urogenital openings as the perineal body (Langman-only detail; not in SDL 22 lecture notes). The genital tubercle, urogenital folds, and labioscrotal swellings are external genital primordia, and anal columns are mucosal folds of the upper anal canal. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut; tip of urorectal septum → perineal body, Figs. 15.36–15.37); SDL 22 lecture notes (urorectal septum context). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the perineal body anchors the perineal muscles and can be torn during vaginal delivery (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the allantois enters the anterior cloaca and the hindgut enters the posterior cloaca (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — foregut partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":1,"stem":"At the end of which week does the anal membrane (posterior cloacal membrane) break down?","choices":{"A":"Fourth week","B":"Seventh week","C":"Fifth week","D":"Tenth week","E":"Twelfth week"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The anal membrane (the posterior portion of the cloacal membrane) degenerates at the end of the seventh week, opening communication between the anal pit and the hindgut. The fourth and fifth weeks are too early because the cloaca is still being partitioned, and the tenth and twelfth weeks correspond to return of the herniated midgut. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (imperforate anus / anal membrane); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rectourethral and rectovaginal fistulas occur in about 1 in 5,000 live births (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: these defects likely reflect misexpression of genes during epithelial–mesenchymal signaling (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — foregut partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":1,"stem":"If the urorectal septum fails to extend far enough caudally, how is the hindgut opening displaced?","choices":{"A":"Posterior shift of the hindgut opening","B":"Lateral shift of the hindgut opening","C":"Cranial shift of the hindgut opening","D":"Duplication of the hindgut opening","E":"Anterior shift of the hindgut opening"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A small cloaca or a urorectal septum that stops short causes the hindgut opening to shift anteriorly, so it can empty into the urethra, vagina, or perineum. Posterior, lateral, and cranial displacement are not described, and duplication is a separate anomaly unrelated to septal growth. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (anorectal malformations vs imperforate anus); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut; Hindgut abnormalities, Figs. 15.36–15.37). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: an anterior opening explains meconium in the urine of a boy with a rectourethral fistula (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: some variants end in rectal atresia or a fibrous cord to the perineum (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — foregut partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":1,"stem":"Which epithelium lines the anal canal just below the pectinate line?","choices":{"A":"Pseudostratified columnar epithelium","B":"Transitional epithelium","C":"Stratified squamous epithelium","D":"Simple cuboidal epithelium","E":"Stratified cuboidal epithelium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Below the pectinate line the ectodermal mucosa is stratified squamous epithelium that continues to the perianal skin. Pseudostratified columnar epithelium lines the airways, transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract, simple cuboidal epithelium lines ducts and tubules, and stratified cuboidal epithelium lines larger gland ducts. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tumors below the line are typically squamous cell carcinomas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: an anal transitional zone lies at the junction of the two epithelial types (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 5 — hemorrhoids and anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":1,"stem":"The superior rectal artery is the terminal continuation of which vessel?","choices":{"A":"Inferior mesenteric artery","B":"Internal iliac artery","C":"Internal pudendal artery","D":"Median sacral artery","E":"Left colic artery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The superior rectal artery continues the inferior mesenteric artery, the artery of the hindgut, and supplies the anal canal above the pectinate line. The internal iliac and internal pudendal arteries supply the region below the line, the median sacral artery is a small midline branch of the aorta, and the left colic artery is a separate, more proximal branch. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: internal hemorrhoids above the line drain to the superior rectal vein and portal system (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: sigmoidal branches arise from the same parent vessel between the left colic and terminal branch (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 5 — hemorrhoids and anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":1,"stem":"Which mucosal landmark lies immediately above the pectinate line?","choices":{"A":"Anal verge","B":"Perianal skin","C":"Intersphincteric groove","D":"Anal columns","E":"Levator ani"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pectinate line, which marks the junction of endodermal and ectodermal parts of the anal canal, lies just below the anal columns. The anal verge and perianal skin lie below it at the outer margin, the intersphincteric groove lies between the sphincters below the line, and the levator ani is pelvic floor muscle rather than a mucosal landmark. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: visceral pain fibers serve the canal above the line, and somatic fibers serve it below (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: the cranial two-thirds of the anal canal come from the primitive anorectal canal (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 5 — hemorrhoids and anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":1,"stem":"Parasympathetic ganglion cells of the enteric nervous system derive from which embryonic cells?","choices":{"A":"Splanchnic mesoderm cells","B":"Paraxial mesoderm cells","C":"Surface ectoderm cells","D":"Neural tube cells","E":"Neural crest cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Enteric parasympathetic ganglion cells come from neural crest cells that migrate from the neural folds into the bowel wall. Splanchnic mesoderm forms the gut muscle and connective tissue, paraxial mesoderm forms somites, surface ectoderm forms epidermis, and the neural tube forms the central nervous system. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neural crest derivatives also include adrenal medulla, melanocytes, and Schwann cells (First Aid Embryology, germ layer derivatives). COMLEX: the endothelin-3 and EDNRB pathway also supports these migrating precursors (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":1,"stem":"The RET gene that is mutated in congenital megacolon encodes which type of protein?","choices":{"A":"Ligand-gated ion channel","B":"G protein-coupled receptor","C":"Nuclear hormone receptor","D":"Receptor tyrosine kinase","E":"Serine threonine kinase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"RET encodes a receptor tyrosine kinase required for proliferation, survival, and directed migration of enteric neural crest precursors. Ligand-gated channels, G protein-coupled receptors, nuclear hormone receptors, and serine threonine kinases are other signaling classes not encoded by RET. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gain-of-function RET mutations cause MEN 2A and 2B, the opposite of the loss of function in megacolon (First Aid Endocrinology, MEN syndromes). COMLEX: RET is discussed further with neural crest development in Langman Ch. 18.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":1,"stem":"Which bowel segment is involved in nearly every case of aganglionic megacolon?","choices":{"A":"Rectum","B":"Cecum","C":"Appendix","D":"Jejunum","E":"Ileum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aganglionosis begins distally and extends proximally for a variable distance, so the rectum is involved in nearly all cases. The cecum and appendix are affected only in long-segment or total colonic disease, and the jejunum and ileum only in rare extended forms. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: suction rectal biopsy is the diagnostic test (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: in about 80% the defect extends to the midpoint of the sigmoid, and in about 3% the whole colon is affected (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man has sudden severe periumbilical pain. He has atrial fibrillation and stopped apixaban four days ago after a fall. The abdomen is soft and much less tender than the pain, the pulse is 112/min, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Serum lactate is 5.1 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L), and CT angiography shows an embolus occluding the first anterior aortic branch below the celiac trunk, just past its origin. Which colonic segment is at greatest risk of infarction?","choices":{"A":"Descending colon","B":"Sigmoid colon","C":"Ascending colon","D":"Rectum","E":"Distal transverse colon"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The occluded vessel is the superior mesenteric artery, so the ascending colon, a midgut segment, is at greatest risk of infarction. Pain out of proportion to a soft abdomen, atrial fibrillation, and a lactate of 5.1 mmol/L indicate acute mesenteric ischemia from loss of midgut flow. The descending colon is hindgut and remains perfused by the inferior mesenteric artery after this embolus. The sigmoid colon is hindgut and receives inferior mesenteric blood that this occlusion does not interrupt. The rectum is supplied by the superior rectal artery, a terminal branch of the inferior mesenteric artery. The distal transverse colon lies in hindgut territory and is reached by the left colic artery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute mesenteric ischemia classically follows embolism from atrial fibrillation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, acute mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: pain out of proportion to examination is the early bedside clue (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — ischemic bowel disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The descending colon is hindgut and remains perfused by the","B":"The sigmoid colon is hindgut and receives inferior mesenteric blood","D":"The rectum is supplied by the superior rectal artery, a","E":"The distal transverse colon lies in hindgut territory and is"}},{"id":"sdl22_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman has left lower quadrant pain and bloody diarrhea two days after open aortic aneurysm repair. During that operation the anterior aortic branch arising at L3 was ligated, and she had no prior colitis. The left lower quadrant is tender, the pulse is 98/min, and blood pressure is 128/74 mm Hg. Colonoscopy shows ischemic mucosa from just beyond the splenic flexure through the rectosigmoid. Which other structure normally depends on the same arterial territory?","choices":{"A":"Lower third of the anal canal","B":"Cecum and vermiform appendix","C":"Proximal two-thirds of transverse colon","D":"Upper two-thirds of the anal canal","E":"Terminal ileum and ileocecal valve"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The ligated vessel is the inferior mesenteric artery, the artery of the hindgut, and that territory includes the upper two-thirds of the anal canal along with the distal colon and rectum. Ischemia beginning just beyond the splenic flexure and extending through the rectosigmoid shows loss of this hindgut supply. The lower third of the anal canal is surface ectoderm supplied by the inferior rectal arteries. The cecum and vermiform appendix are midgut structures supplied by the ileocolic artery. The proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon receive middle colic flow from the superior mesenteric artery. The terminal ileum and ileocecal valve lie in superior mesenteric territory and keep their own arterial trunk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic ischemia after aortic surgery reflects loss of inferior mesenteric inflow (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: the left colic, sigmoidal, and superior rectal arteries are the named branches of this vessel (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The lower third of the anal canal is surface ectoderm","B":"The cecum and vermiform appendix are midgut structures supplied by","C":"The proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon receive middle colic","E":"The terminal ileum and ileocecal valve lie in superior mesenteric"}},{"id":"sdl22_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man has crampy left upper quadrant pain and maroon stool two days after septic shock. His blood pressure remained 78/42 mm Hg for several hours, and he has no known embolic source. The left upper quadrant is tender and the pulse is 104/min. Serum lactate is 2.8 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L), and colonoscopy shows a short segment of ischemic mucosa centered on the splenic flexure. The ischemic region lies between the terminal branches of which arteries?","choices":{"A":"Ileocolic and right colic arteries","B":"Middle colic and left colic arteries","C":"Right colic and middle colic arteries","D":"Left colic and sigmoidal arteries","E":"Sigmoidal and superior rectal arteries"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Low-flow ischemia centered on the splenic flexure is a watershed infarct at the midgut-hindgut boundary, where terminal branches of the middle colic artery meet those of the left colic artery. Prolonged hypotension with a short ischemic segment at that flexure selects the junction where collateral flow is poorest. The ileocolic and right colic arteries are both superior mesenteric branches that meet along the ascending colon. The right colic and middle colic arteries are both superior mesenteric branches and anastomose within the proximal colon. The left colic and sigmoidal arteries are both inferior mesenteric branches supplying the descending and sigmoid colon. The sigmoidal and superior rectal arteries share an inferior mesenteric origin and meet in the distal colon.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction are the classic watershed sites for colonic ischemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: hypotension rather than occlusion is the usual cause of this pattern (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — ischemic bowel disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The ileocolic and right colic arteries are both superior mesenteric","C":"The right colic and middle colic arteries are both superior","D":"The left colic and sigmoidal arteries are both inferior mesenteric","E":"The sigmoidal and superior rectal arteries share an inferior mesenteric"}},{"id":"sdl22_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has 12 hours of colicky pain, distension, and vomiting. She has had two similar milder episodes and has never undergone abdominal surgery. The abdomen is distended and tympanitic, and the pulse is 108/min. The leukocyte count is 12,400/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL). CT shows a dilated cecum in the left upper quadrant with a whirl of mesenteric vessels at its base. Which anatomic feature of her colon most likely allowed this twist?","choices":{"A":"Elongated mesentery of the sigmoid colon","B":"Narrowed lumen at the ileocecal valve","C":"Persistent free mesentery of the ascending colon","D":"Absent ganglion cells in the rectum","E":"Thickened teniae coli of the cecum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Failure of the ascending colon to fuse with the posterior wall leaves a free mesentery, allowing cecal volvulus around the vascular pedicle. Recurrent colic, a cecum displaced into the left upper quadrant, and a mesenteric whirl are the clues to that closed-loop twist. An elongated mesentery of the sigmoid colon allows the sigmoid to twist while the cecum stays in the right iliac fossa. A narrowed lumen at the ileocecal valve can slow emptying but does not swing the cecum into the left upper quadrant. Absent ganglion cells in the rectum cause constipation from a narrow distal segment and do not rotate the cecum into the left upper quadrant. Thickened teniae coli change the shape of the cecum but do not create a pedicle that can twist.","boardPrep":"Step 1: volvulus of the cecum and colon can progress to ischemic gangrene (SDL 22 notes). COMLEX: volvulus is one of the four major causes of intestinal obstruction, most often in the sigmoid colon (First Aid Gastrointestinal, volvulus).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — intestinal obstruction","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An elongated mesentery of the sigmoid colon allows the sigmoid","B":"A narrowed lumen at the ileocecal valve can slow emptying","D":"Absent ganglion cells in the rectum cause constipation from a","E":"Thickened teniae coli change the shape of the cecum but"}},{"id":"sdl22_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man has two days of cramping pain and bilious vomiting. He has never had abdominal surgery and has no groin or ventral hernia. The abdomen is mildly distended and the pulse is 96/min. CT shows dilated small-bowel loops lying behind the right colon inside an abnormal peritoneal recess. Which developmental error most likely produced this recess?","choices":{"A":"Incomplete fusion of the ascending mesocolon","B":"Persistent vitelline duct at the umbilicus","C":"Failed return of the herniated midgut","D":"Reversed rotation of the midgut loop","E":"Arrested descent of the sigmoid colon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Incomplete fusion of the ascending mesocolon with the posterior abdominal wall leaves retrocolic pockets that can trap and obstruct small intestine. Bilious vomiting and dilated loops behind a right-sided colon, without prior surgery, point to that congenital recess. A persistent vitelline duct at the umbilicus forms a diverticulum, sinus, or fistula rather than a pocket behind the colon. Failed return of the herniated midgut leaves bowel outside the abdomen within a sac at the umbilicus. Reversed rotation of the midgut loop places the transverse colon behind the duodenum instead of forming a right-sided recess. Arrested descent of the sigmoid colon would misplace the left colon and would not open a recess behind the right colon.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hernias and adhesions are leading causes of small bowel obstruction (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal obstruction). COMLEX: internal hernias can occur without surgical history when mesenteric fusion is abnormal (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 1 — midgut rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A persistent vitelline duct at the umbilicus forms a diverticulum,","C":"Failed return of the herniated midgut leaves bowel outside the","D":"Reversed rotation of the midgut loop places the transverse colon","E":"Arrested descent of the sigmoid colon would misplace the left"}},{"id":"sdl22_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy has 18 hours of fever, anorexia, and right upper quadrant pain. He has had no cough or jaundice. Temperature is 38.4°C, the pulse is 110/min, and tenderness is greatest just below the right costal margin. The leukocyte count is 15,800/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL). Ultrasound shows a normal gallbladder and an inflamed appendix lying beneath the right hepatic lobe at the end of a high-riding colon. Which developmental process most likely failed?","choices":{"A":"Counterclockwise rotation of the midgut","B":"Caudal descent of the cecal bud","C":"Fusion of the descending mesocolon","D":"Recanalization of the appendiceal lumen","E":"Return of loops from the umbilical cord"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After rotation the cecal bud lies in the right upper quadrant and normally descends into the right iliac fossa, so failed caudal descent leaves a subhepatic appendix. Fever, leukocytosis, and an inflamed appendix beside a normal gallbladder explain the right upper quadrant pain. Counterclockwise rotation of the midgut is what places the colon on the right, and its failure would leave the colon on the left. Fusion of the descending mesocolon attaches the left colon to the posterior wall and does not set cecal height. Recanalization of the appendiceal lumen is not a step in forming the appendix, which is not laid down as a solid cord. Return of loops from the umbilical cord brings the bowel back into the abdomen, and failure leaves an umbilical wall defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: periumbilical pain migrating to McBurney point is the classic presentation when the appendix is in its usual position (First Aid Gastrointestinal, appendicitis). COMLEX: atypical appendix positions delay diagnosis and raise perforation risk (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — appendicitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Counterclockwise rotation of the midgut is what places the colon","C":"Fusion of the descending mesocolon attaches the left colon to","D":"Recanalization of the appendiceal lumen is not a step in","E":"Return of loops from the umbilical cord brings the bowel"}},{"id":"sdl22_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy has a flat perineum and has passed no stool. He has fed poorly since an otherwise uncomplicated delivery. There is no anal opening, and the abdomen is becoming distended. Over the next day, green particulate material appears in the urine. Contrast imaging shows a fistula from the rectum into the urethra. Which embryologic error most likely caused this malformation?","choices":{"A":"Arrested caudal growth of the urorectal septum","B":"Failed proliferation of proctodeal ectoderm","C":"Failed recanalization of the rectal lumen","D":"Absent neural crest cells in the rectum","E":"Persistent vitelline duct at the umbilicus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Arrested caudal growth of the urorectal septum leaves the hindgut opening in the urethra, producing a rectourinary fistula. A flat perineum without an anal opening, followed by meconium in the urine, is the result of that incomplete cloacal separation. Failed proliferation of proctodeal ectoderm would disturb the anal pit and would not open the rectum into the urinary tract. Failed recanalization of the rectal lumen is not how the rectum forms, and it would not create a fistula into the urethra. Absent neural crest cells in the rectum produce a narrow distal segment with a patent anus and no urinary fistula. A persistent vitelline duct at the umbilicus connects the ileum to the umbilicus rather than the rectum to the urethra.","boardPrep":"Step 1: meconium in the urine of a newborn boy signals a rectourinary tract communication (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: anorectal malformations often occur with vertebral, cardiac, renal, and limb defects in VACTERL association (First Aid Genetics, VACTERL).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Failed proliferation of proctodeal ectoderm would disturb the anal pit","C":"Failed recanalization of the rectal lumen is not how the","D":"Absent neural crest cells in the rectum produce a narrow","E":"A persistent vitelline duct at the umbilicus connects the ileum"}},{"id":"sdl22_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old girl has passed no stool and has mild abdominal distension. Prenatal ultrasound had been reported as normal. There is no anal opening in the usual position, and meconium is present at the vaginal introitus. Vital signs are stable, and a radiograph shows several dilated bowel loops without free air. Abnormal partitioning between which two embryonic compartments most likely caused this finding?","choices":{"A":"Allantoic duct and yolk sac","B":"Midgut and hindgut loops","C":"Urogenital sinus and anorectal canal","D":"Mesonephric and paramesonephric ducts","E":"Bladder and the urachus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The urorectal septum normally separates the urogenital sinus from the anorectal canal, and incomplete partitioning leaves a rectovaginal fistula. Meconium at the vaginal introitus with no normally placed anus shows that the hindgut still opens into the urogenital compartment. The allantoic duct and yolk sac are extraembryonic structures and do not partition the perineum. The midgut and hindgut loops meet near the transverse colon, far from the vaginal opening. The mesonephric and paramesonephric ducts form internal reproductive ducts and do not separate the rectum from the vagina. The bladder and the urachus meet along the anterior abdominal wall, and a defect there leaks urine at the umbilicus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rectovaginal fistula is the female counterpart of the rectourethral fistula (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the cloaca is endoderm-lined and bounded ventrally by surface ectoderm (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The allantoic duct and yolk sac are extraembryonic structures and","B":"The midgut and hindgut loops meet near the transverse colon,","D":"The mesonephric and paramesonephric ducts form internal reproductive ducts and","E":"The bladder and the urachus meet along the anterior abdominal"}},{"id":"sdl22_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy has not passed meconium, and his abdomen is distended. He was born at term and his urine has stayed clear. A normally placed anal dimple is present, and a thin bluish membrane bulges at its center. No meconium appears in the urine or on the perineum, and imaging shows no fistula. The persistent tissue separates the hindgut from which structure?","choices":{"A":"Allantois","B":"Urachus","C":"Phallus","D":"Bladder","E":"Anal pit"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This form of imperforate anus is persistence of the anal membrane, which keeps the hindgut closed off from the anal pit, the ectodermal depression of the proctodeum. A normally placed dimple with a bulging membrane and no fistula shows that the barrier is the membrane rather than a misplaced rectal opening. The allantois runs from the cloaca toward the umbilicus and does not face the hindgut across this membrane. The urachus is the fibrous remnant of the allantois between the bladder dome and the umbilicus. The phallus is formed by the genital tubercle, anterior to the anal region, and is not sealed by this membrane. The bladder develops from the urogenital sinus, anterior to the partition that divides the cloaca.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an isolated membrane without a fistula distinguishes this lesion from septal defects (SDL 22 notes). COMLEX: the anal membrane is the posterior portion of the cloacal membrane (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — intestinal atresia","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The allantois runs from the cloaca toward the umbilicus and","B":"The urachus is the fibrous remnant of the allantois between","C":"The phallus is formed by the genital tubercle, anterior to","D":"The bladder develops from the urogenital sinus, anterior to the"}},{"id":"sdl22_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has a painful, bleeding perianal ulcer for three months. He has longstanding HIV infection and has not been taking antiretroviral therapy. The pulse is 82/min and blood pressure is 126/78 mm Hg, and a tender ulcer is visible at the anal margin. The CD4 count is 180/µL (reference range: 500–1,500/µL). Anoscopy shows an ulcerated mass entirely below the pectinate line, and biopsy shows invasive squamous cells. Which artery is the main arterial supply to the region of this tumor?","choices":{"A":"Superior rectal artery","B":"Middle colic artery","C":"Sigmoidal artery","D":"Inferior rectal artery","E":"Median sacral artery"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Below the pectinate line the anal canal is ectodermal and is supplied by the inferior rectal artery, a branch of the internal pudendal artery. A painful squamous carcinoma confined below that line therefore lies in inferior rectal territory. The superior rectal artery supplies the endodermal anal canal above the pectinate line. The middle colic artery is a superior mesenteric branch that supplies the transverse colon. The sigmoidal artery supplies the sigmoid colon from the inferior mesenteric artery. The median sacral artery descends on the anterior sacrum and is not the main supply of the lower anal canal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tumors below the line drain to the superficial inguinal nodes (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: HIV and HPV infection raise the risk of anal squamous cancer (general knowledge; no First Aid anal-cancer entry verified).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The superior rectal artery supplies the endodermal anal canal above","B":"The middle colic artery is a superior mesenteric branch that","C":"The sigmoidal artery supplies the sigmoid colon from the inferior","E":"The median sacral artery descends on the anterior sacrum and"}},{"id":"sdl22_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has painless bright red blood coating the stool for two weeks. She has had two vaginal deliveries and has no pain with defecation. The pulse is 76/min, blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg, and no external anal lesion is seen. Hemoglobin is 13.1 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL). Anoscopy shows engorged vascular cushions arising above the pectinate line. Biopsy of the mucosa overlying these cushions would most likely show which epithelium?","choices":{"A":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium","B":"Simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells","C":"Transitional epithelium with umbrella cells","D":"Simple cuboidal epithelium with microvilli","E":"Stratified cuboidal epithelium with ducts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Above the pectinate line the mucosa is lined by simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells. Painless bleeding from cushions above the line, with a normal hemoglobin, samples that glandular surface. Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium lines the airways, including the trachea and bronchi. Transitional epithelium with umbrella cells lines the urinary tract from the renal pelvis through much of the urethra. Simple cuboidal epithelium with microvilli lines small ducts and tubules rather than colorectal mucosa. Stratified cuboidal epithelium lines larger glandular ducts, such as the ducts of sweat glands.","boardPrep":"Step 1: internal hemorrhoids are painless because the region above the line has visceral innervation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, hemorrhoids). COMLEX: tumors above the line are typically adenocarcinomas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — hemorrhoids","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium lines the airways, including the trachea","C":"Transitional epithelium with umbrella cells lines the urinary tract from","D":"Simple cuboidal epithelium with microvilli lines small ducts and tubules","E":"Stratified cuboidal epithelium lines larger glandular ducts, such as the"}},{"id":"sdl22_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man has sharp anal pain with every bowel movement and streaks of blood on the paper for three weeks. He has hard stools and has never been told he has inflammatory bowel disease. The pulse is 74/min and blood pressure is 122/76 mm Hg. Examination shows a posterior midline tear at the anal verge. The tear runs from the outer margin to just below the pectinate line. The epithelium at the site of this tear derives from which embryonic tissue?","choices":{"A":"Endoderm of the hindgut","B":"Mesoderm of the urorectal septum","C":"Endoderm of the allantois","D":"Ectoderm of the neural tube","E":"Ectoderm of the anal pit"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Below the pectinate line the tear is an anal fissure in ectoderm of the anal pit. Sharp pain on defecation and a posterior midline tear place the injury in that somatically sensitive segment. Endoderm of the hindgut lines the canal above the pectinate line, where the epithelium is columnar. Mesoderm of the urorectal septum contributes to the perineal body and does not form this surface lining. Endoderm of the allantois contributes to the bladder and urachus rather than the anal lining. Ectoderm of the neural tube forms the central nervous system and does not cover the anal canal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: anal fissures are painful because they lie below the line in somatically innervated tissue (First Aid Gastrointestinal, anal fissure). COMLEX: most fissures occur in the posterior midline (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — anal canal disorders","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Endoderm of the hindgut lines the canal above the pectinate","B":"Mesoderm of the urorectal septum contributes to the perineal body","C":"Endoderm of the allantois contributes to the bladder and urachus","D":"Ectoderm of the neural tube forms the central nervous system"}},{"id":"sdl22_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-day-old boy has not passed meconium and now has abdominal distension and bilious vomiting. He was born at term and has passed urine normally. The abdomen is distended and the pulse is 150/min. Rectal examination produces an explosive release of stool. Contrast enema shows a narrow rectosigmoid with the colon proximal to it measuring 4 cm in diameter. Which mechanism best explains the dilation of the proximal colon?","choices":{"A":"Tonic contraction of the distal aganglionic segment","B":"Absent ganglia in the dilated proximal segment","C":"Mechanical obstruction by inspissated meconium","D":"Excess ganglion cells in the distal segment","E":"Fibrotic stricture from ischemic injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hirschsprung disease leaves the distal bowel without ganglia that normally signal relaxation, so that segment stays tonically contracted and the innervated colon above it dilates. Delayed meconium, explosive stool after rectal examination, and a narrow rectosigmoid with proximal dilation show this functional obstruction. Absent ganglia in the dilated proximal segment would narrow that segment, but the enema shows it is the wide portion. Mechanical obstruction by inspissated meconium plugs the terminal ileum and does not leave a narrow rectosigmoid transition. Excess ganglion cells in the distal segment would not produce a narrow rectosigmoid with a dilated colon above it. A fibrotic stricture from ischemic injury is an acquired scar and would not be present in the first days of life.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the narrow segment is the diseased one, and the dilated bowel above it is normal (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: Hirschsprung disease is associated with Down syndrome (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 5 — defecation reflex","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Absent ganglia in the dilated proximal segment would narrow that","C":"Mechanical obstruction by inspissated meconium plugs the terminal ileum and","D":"Excess ganglion cells in the distal segment would not produce","E":"A fibrotic stricture from ischemic injury is an acquired scar"}},{"id":"sdl22_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-week-old girl has chronic constipation and abdominal distension. Her older brother required a colonic resection in infancy for a similar obstruction. The abdomen is distended and vital signs are normal. Contrast enema shows a narrow rectum with a transition zone 3 cm above the anal verge. Genetic testing shows a heterozygous loss-of-function variant in RET. Which developmental process was most likely impaired?","choices":{"A":"Recanalization of the colonic lumen","B":"Partitioning of the cloaca","C":"Differentiation of colonic smooth muscle","D":"Rotation of the primary intestinal loop","E":"Craniocaudal migration of enteric precursors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Loss of RET signaling stops the craniocaudal migration of neural crest enteric precursors, leaving the distal bowel without ganglia. A narrow rectum, a transition zone, and an affected sibling match Hirschsprung disease from that failed migration. Recanalization of the colonic lumen, if it failed, would narrow or occlude the lumen itself rather than the nerve supply. Partitioning of the cloaca, when incomplete, opens the rectum into the urinary tract or the vagina. Differentiation of colonic smooth muscle is a mesodermal process, and smooth muscle is still present in the narrow distal segment. Rotation of the primary intestinal loop sets where the bowel lies and how its mesentery is fixed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the most distal bowel is affected because it is reached last by migrating precursors (SDL 22 notes). COMLEX: in about 80% of cases the aganglionic segment extends to the midpoint of the sigmoid (Langman Ch. 15); EDNRB–EDN3 also supports enteric crest migration (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Recanalization of the colonic lumen, if it failed, would narrow","B":"Partitioning of the cloaca, when incomplete, opens the rectum into","C":"Differentiation of colonic smooth muscle is a mesodermal process, and","D":"Rotation of the primary intestinal loop sets where the bowel"}},{"id":"sdl22_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-day-old boy has delayed passage of meconium and abdominal distension. He was born at 39 weeks and has had no fever. The abdomen is soft but distended, and the anus is patent. A suction biopsy is taken from the rectum 2 cm above the pectinate line. Absence of ganglion cells in which layers of the bowel wall would confirm the suspected diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Mucosal crypts and lamina propria","B":"Muscularis mucosae and serosa","C":"Serosa and mesenteric fat","D":"Submucosal and myenteric plexuses","E":"Celiac and mesenteric ganglia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hirschsprung disease is confirmed when neural-crest-derived ganglion cells are absent from the submucosal and myenteric plexuses on a rectal biopsy. Delayed meconium and distension, with the sample taken 2 cm above the pectinate line, target the distal segment these cells fail to reach. Mucosal crypts and the lamina propria hold epithelium and immune cells, not the enteric ganglion cell bodies. The muscularis mucosae and serosa are structural layers and do not contain the parasympathetic ganglia of the bowel. The serosa and mesenteric fat lie outside the intramural nerve plexuses and do not hold these neuron cell bodies. The celiac and mesenteric ganglia are prevertebral sympathetic ganglia that sit outside the bowel wall.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diagnosed by absence of ganglion cells on rectal suction biopsy; RET mutation in the REcTum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: biopsies are taken ≥2 cm above the pectinate line to avoid the normal hypoganglionic zone (general knowledge).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — GI wall layers","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mucosal crypts and the lamina propria hold epithelium and immune","B":"The muscularis mucosae and serosa are structural layers and do","C":"The serosa and mesenteric fat lie outside the intramural nerve","E":"The celiac and mesenteric ganglia are prevertebral sympathetic ganglia that"}},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":3,"stem":"A 71-year-old man has three months of fatigue and a change in bowel habit, with a 4 kg weight loss. He has no history of inflammatory bowel disease. The abdomen is soft and the pulse is 88/min. Colonoscopy shows an ulcerated mass in the descending colon 8 cm distal to the splenic flexure. Hemoglobin is 9.8 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and carcinoembryonic antigen is 14 ng/mL (reference range: <3.0 ng/mL). Which vessel is the main arterial trunk that the surgeon plans to divide at its origin to control the branches feeding this segment?","choices":{"A":"Superior mesenteric artery","B":"Middle colic artery","C":"Superior rectal artery","D":"Inferior mesenteric artery","E":"Internal pudendal artery"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The descending colon is hindgut, and the trunk divided at its origin to devascularize it is the inferior mesenteric artery, by way of the left colic branch. An ulcerated carcinoma with anemia and a carcinoembryonic antigen of 14 ng/mL sits in that hindgut segment. The superior mesenteric artery supplies the midgut, and its territory ends in the transverse colon near the splenic flexure. The middle colic artery is a superior mesenteric branch that supplies the transverse colon. The superior rectal artery is the terminal continuation of the inferior mesenteric artery and supplies the rectum. The internal pudendal artery supplies the anal canal below the pectinate line by way of the inferior rectal artery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The hindgut runs from the distal third of the transverse colon to the upper anal canal and is supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery (First Aid 2025 GI embryology). COMLEX: Surgical anatomy gives most of the transverse colon to the middle colic artery, while the descending colon depends on the left colic branch of the IMA (Moore Ch. 5).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — colonic ischemia","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The superior mesenteric artery supplies the midgut, and its territory","B":"The middle colic artery is a superior mesenteric branch that","C":"The superior rectal artery is the terminal continuation of the","E":"The internal pudendal artery supplies the anal canal below the"}},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old man is undergoing laparoscopic right colectomy for a cecal mass found after a positive fecal immunochemical test. He has had two months of fatigue and has never had abdominal surgery. At operation the ascending colon is lifted medially off the posterior abdominal wall along a nearly bloodless fascial plane, and the mesentery is kept intact. Hemoglobin is 10.2 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and carcinoembryonic antigen is 8 ng/mL (reference range: <3.0 ng/mL). Which developmental event created this plane?","choices":{"A":"Mesocolon fusing with the posterior wall","B":"Persistence of a free mesentery for the colon","C":"Rotation of the gut loop around its artery","D":"Closure of the umbilical ring after return","E":"Elongation of the colon during cecal descent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fusion of the ascending mesocolon with the peritoneum of the posterior body wall creates the bloodless fascial plane used to lift the colon while the mesentery stays intact. The surgeon can separate the ascending colon from the wall along that plane and still keep the mesentery, with its vessels, intact. A persistent free mesentery leaves the colon mobile on a pedicle, so there is no bloodless attachment to the wall. Rotation of the gut loop around its artery sets the position of the bowel but does not attach the colon to the wall. Closure of the umbilical ring completes return of the intestine and is related to defects of the body wall. Elongation of the colon during cecal descent carries the cecum toward the right iliac fossa without forming this fascial layer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The ascending and descending colon become secondarily retroperitoneal when their mesentery fuses to the posterior wall with Toldt fascia between the layers (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: The Toldt plane lets surgeons mobilize the colon without disrupting its mesentery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — volvulus","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A persistent free mesentery leaves the colon mobile on a","C":"Rotation of the gut loop around its artery sets the","D":"Closure of the umbilical ring completes return of the intestine","E":"Elongation of the colon during cecal descent carries the cecum"}},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":3,"stem":"A 1-day-old girl has not passed meconium, and her abdomen is distended. No meconium has appeared at the vagina or the urethra. A shallow anal dimple lies in the normal position, and a perineal body separates the urogenital openings. Imaging shows a rectum that ends blindly 3 cm above the pelvic floor and is joined to the perineal skin by a thin fibrous cord. Which mechanism best explains this defect?","choices":{"A":"Persistent membrane at the level of the anal pit","B":"Urorectal septum stopping short caudally","C":"Faulty epithelial-mesenchymal gene signaling","D":"A cloaca too small to divide normally","E":"Absent neural crest cells in the rectum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A rectum ending blindly above the pelvic floor and joined to the perineum by a fibrous cord, with no fistula and a normal perineal body, is rectoanal atresia caused by faulty epithelial-mesenchymal gene signaling. The normally placed dimple and the absence of meconium in the urine or vagina show that the rectal end never reached the skin. A persistent membrane at the level of the anal pit would leave the rectum ending on that membrane, not on a fibrous cord above the pelvic floor. A urorectal septum stopping short caudally shifts the hindgut opening forward into the urethra or the vagina. A cloaca too small to divide normally shifts the hindgut opening forward into the urethra or vagina and produces a fistula. Absent neural crest cells in the rectum cause a narrow distal segment with a patent anal opening.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rectoanal atresias reflect faulty epithelial-mesenchymal signaling, rectourethral and rectovaginal fistulas reflect cloacal or urorectal septum defects, and imperforate anus reflects a persistent anal membrane (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: The level and presence of a fistula guide the surgical plan for anorectal malformations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A persistent membrane at the level of the anal pit","B":"A urorectal septum stopping short caudally shifts the hindgut opening","D":"A cloaca too small to divide normally shifts the hindgut","E":"Absent neural crest cells in the rectum cause a narrow"}},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has painless bright red bleeding with defecation for six weeks. He has no pain on stool passage. The pulse is 80/min, blood pressure is 134/78 mm Hg, and no mass is felt at the anal verge. Anoscopy shows a 2 cm mass in the anal canal. Biopsy shows invasive glands of simple columnar epithelium, with no stratified squamous epithelium in any sample, and carcinoembryonic antigen is 7 ng/mL (reference range: <3.0 ng/mL). Which artery most likely provides the main blood supply to the mucosa at this site?","choices":{"A":"Inferior rectal artery","B":"Superior rectal artery","C":"Middle colic artery","D":"Left colic artery","E":"Inferior epigastric artery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Simple columnar glandular mucosa marks the anal canal above the pectinate line, a hindgut segment supplied by the superior rectal artery, the continuation of the inferior mesenteric artery. Painless bleeding from columnar mucosa without squamous epithelium places the mass in that endodermal segment. The inferior rectal artery, a branch of the internal pudendal artery, supplies the stratified squamous canal below the pectinate line. The middle colic artery supplies the transverse colon and does not reach the anal mucosa. The left colic artery supplies the descending colon, well above this anal mass. The inferior epigastric artery runs in the anterior abdominal wall and does not supply intestinal mucosa.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Above the pectinate line the canal is endoderm-derived columnar mucosa supplied by the superior rectal artery, and below it is ectoderm-derived squamous mucosa supplied by the inferior rectal artery (First Aid 2025 GI anatomy). COMLEX: Adenocarcinoma arises above the pectinate line and squamous cell carcinoma below it.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The inferior rectal artery, a branch of the internal pudendal","C":"The middle colic artery supplies the transverse colon and does","D":"The left colic artery supplies the descending colon, well above","E":"The inferior epigastric artery runs in the anterior abdominal wall"}},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":3,"stem":"A 4-day-old boy has not passed meconium and has progressive abdominal distension. He was born at term and has been feeding poorly. The abdomen is distended and the pulse is 152/min. Digital rectal examination is followed by an explosive release of stool. A suction biopsy 2 cm above the anal verge shows no ganglion cells in the submucosa, and sequencing finds no variant in RET but a loss-of-function variant in a G protein-coupled receptor for a vasoconstrictor peptide. Which gene is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"CFTR","B":"SPINK1","C":"APC","D":"EDNRB","E":"FOXP3"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Absent distal ganglion cells with explosive stool after rectal examination indicate Hirschsprung disease, and the mutated G protein-coupled receptor for a vasoconstrictor peptide is EDNRB, an endothelin receptor required for neural crest colonization of the bowel. The usual receptor tyrosine kinase, RET, is not the gene altered here. CFTR defects cause meconium ileus from inspissated meconium, and ganglion cells are still present in the bowel wall. SPINK1 encodes a pancreatic trypsin inhibitor linked to pancreatitis and has no role in enteric neuron migration. APC is a tumor suppressor gene whose loss causes colorectal adenomatous polyposis rather than absent ganglia. FOXP3 defects cause IPEX through loss of regulatory T cells and do not remove enteric ganglion cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hirschsprung disease results from failed neural crest migration, most often from loss-of-function RET variants, with EDNRB and EDN3 as other causes (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Rectal suction biopsy showing absent ganglion cells confirms the diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CFTR defects cause meconium ileus from inspissated meconium, and ganglion","B":"SPINK1 encodes a pancreatic trypsin inhibitor linked to pancreatitis and","C":"APC is a tumor suppressor gene whose loss causes colorectal","E":"FOXP3 defects cause IPEX through loss of regulatory T cells"}},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has a polyp removed at screening colonoscopy. She has no rectal bleeding or weight loss. The abdomen is soft. The lesion is a 12 mm tubular adenoma of the descending colon, and histology shows dysplastic columnar epithelium arising from the colonic crypts. The same embryonic gut region that produced this epithelium also forms the epithelial lining of which organ?","choices":{"A":"Vermiform appendix","B":"Distal ileum","C":"Lower anal canal","D":"Gallbladder","E":"Urinary bladder"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The descending colon is a hindgut derivative, and hindgut endoderm also forms the epithelial lining of the urinary bladder and the urethra. Dysplastic columnar epithelium in a descending-colon adenoma therefore shares its embryonic source with the bladder lining. The vermiform appendix develops from the midgut, with the cecum, and does not share the hindgut epithelial source. The distal ileum is a midgut derivative formed with the small intestine, not with the descending colon. The lower anal canal is lined by epithelium derived from the ectoderm of the anal pit. The gallbladder grows from the foregut as an outgrowth of the hepatic diverticulum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hindgut endoderm lines the distal colon, rectum, and upper anal canal and also forms the lining of the bladder and urethra (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: Shared hindgut origin explains why anorectal malformations often involve the urinary tract.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 5 — origins of gut components","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The vermiform appendix develops from the midgut, with the cecum,","B":"The distal ileum is a midgut derivative formed with the","C":"The lower anal canal is lined by epithelium derived from","D":"The gallbladder grows from the foregut as an outgrowth of"}}],"23":[{"id":"sdl23_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":1,"stem":"In which part of the small intestine are goblet cells most numerous?","choices":{"A":"Proximal duodenum","B":"Distal duodenum","C":"Proximal jejunum","D":"Terminal ileum","E":"Middle jejunum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Goblet cells increase in number from the duodenum to the terminal ileum. The duodenum and jejunum have fewer of them. The proximal duodenum is marked by Brunner glands, not peak goblet cell density, which is reached toward the distal ileum. The distal duodenum still has relatively few goblet cells; their density rises steadily to its highest in the ileum. The proximal jejunum is specialized for absorption with tall plicae and villi, not for maximal goblet cell density, which is ileal. The middle jejunum has fewer goblet cells than the ileum, where mucus-secreting cells reach their highest density. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23); First Aid Gastrointestinal (GI histology). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the colon has crypts with abundant goblet cells but no villi (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: on routine H&E, the mucinogen-filled apex of a goblet cell looks empty because the mucinogen is lost during preparation (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The proximal duodenum is marked by Brunner glands, not peak goblet cell density, which is reached toward the distal ileum.","B":"The distal duodenum still has relatively few goblet cells; their density rises steadily to its highest in the ileum.","C":"The proximal jejunum is specialized for absorption with tall plicae and villi, not for maximal goblet cell density, which is ileal.","E":"The middle jejunum has fewer goblet cells than the ileum, where mucus-secreting cells reach their highest density."}},{"id":"sdl23_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":5,"stem":"What portion of the small intestine does the jejunum make up?","choices":{"A":"Lower three-fifths of the small intestine","B":"Middle one-fifth of the small intestine","C":"Upper two-fifths of the small intestine","D":"Upper one-tenth of the small intestine","E":"Lower one-fifth of the small intestine"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The jejunum is about 2.5 m long and makes up the upper two-fifths of the small intestine. The ileum is the lower three-fifths, and the duodenum is about 25 cm long. The jejunum is not one-fifth of the small intestine; it is the upper two-fifths, about 2.5 m long. One-tenth better approximates the short duodenum (about 25 cm), not the 2.5-m jejunum. The lower part of the small intestine is the ileum, which is three-fifths, not a one-fifth jejunal segment. The jejunum is the upper two-fifths. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the small intestine is the longest part of the digestive tract, at more than 6 m (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the ileum ends at the ileocecal junction, where the distal ileum joins the cecum (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"B":"The jejunum is not one-fifth of the small intestine.","D":"One-tenth better approximates the short duodenum (about 25 cm), not the 2.5-m jejunum.","E":"The lower part of the small intestine is the ileum, which is three-fifths, not a one-fifth jejunal segment.","A":"The jejunum is the upper two-fifths."}},{"id":"sdl23_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":1,"stem":"Where are plicae circulares most numerous?","choices":{"A":"Proximal duodenum next to the pylorus","B":"Middle and distal ileum segments","C":"Terminal ileum at the ileocecal valve","D":"Cecum and proximal ascending colon","E":"Distal duodenum and proximal jejunum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Plicae circulares are most numerous in the distal duodenum and the beginning of the jejunum. They become smaller and fewer by the middle of the ileum; the lecture notes likewise describe the jejunum as having the tallest, most numerous plicae and the distal ileum as markedly reduced. Plicae circulares only begin about 5 to 6 cm distal to the pylorus, so the pylorus-adjacent duodenum lacks them. At the terminal ileum plicae are markedly reduced in height and frequency. The cecum and colon lack plicae circulares and villi altogether; these folds belong to the small intestine. Plicae circulares become reduced in size and frequency in the middle of the ileum rather than reaching their peak there. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: on oral contrast, the jejunum has a feathered look because its plicae circulares are taller and more numerous than the ileum's (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: these circular folds are permanent ridges that extend one-half to two-thirds of the way around the lumen (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Plicae circulares only begin about 5 to 6 cm distal to the pylorus, so the pylorus-adjacent duodenum lacks them.","C":"At the terminal ileum plicae are markedly reduced in height and frequency.","D":"The cecum and colon lack plicae circulares and villi altogether; these folds belong to the small intestine.","B":"Plicae circulares become reduced in size and frequency in the middle of the ileum rather than reaching their peak there."}},{"id":"sdl23_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure provides the major amplification of the small intestinal luminal surface?","choices":{"A":"Plicae circulares folds","B":"Mucosal intestinal villi","C":"Crypts of Lieberkühn","D":"Enterocyte microvilli","E":"Lateral cell plications"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Microvilli of the enterocytes provide the largest amplification of the luminal surface (20- to 600-fold), with several thousand per cell. Plicae and villi amplify it less, and lateral plications enlarge the lateral membrane. Crypts of Lieberkühn are glands housing stem cells and Paneth cells. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Enterocytes, Fig. 17.21). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in a malabsorption workup, steatorrhea can be screened for with a Sudan stain for fecal fat (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: under the light microscope, the closely packed microvilli form a striated border on the apex of the enterocyte (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Crypts of Lieberkühn are glands housing stem cells and Paneth cells.","A":"Plicae circulares are the circular folds of the intestinal wall.","B":"Mucosal villi are the finger-like projections of the mucosa.","E":"Lateral plications increase the lateral membrane for transport enzymes."}},{"id":"sdl23_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":1,"stem":"Which tissue layer forms the core of a plica circularis?","choices":{"A":"Submucosa","B":"Lamina propria","C":"Epithelium","D":"Muscularis mucosae","E":"Subserosa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Plicae circulares are permanent transverse folds with a core of submucosa, so they are permanent folds that also carry the villi on their surface. Lamina propria forms the core of each villus, a smaller mucosal projection that sits on the folds. Epithelium covers the surface of the folds and villi but does not form their core. Muscularis mucosae belongs to the mucosa and does not form the core of a plica. Subserosa sits beneath the serosa on the outside of the wall, far from the luminal folds. Sources: Pawlina ch 17, small intestine, plicae circulares.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Plicae circulares are submucosal folds, villi have a lamina propria core, and microvilli are apical cell projections (Pawlina ch 17). COMLEX: Plicae make the valvulae conniventes that cross the full width of dilated small bowel on radiographs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Lamina propria forms the core of each","C":"Epithelium covers the surface of the folds","D":"Muscularis mucosae belongs to the mucosa and","E":"Subserosa sits beneath the serosa on the"}},{"id":"sdl23_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":5,"stem":"Which protein anchors the actin core at the tip of each microvillus?","choices":{"A":"Myosin I","B":"Spectrin","C":"Villin","D":"Dystrophin","E":"Titin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The actin microfilaments of the microvillus core are anchored to villin at the tip. Myosin I attaches them to the plasma membrane along the sides. Spectrin is a terminal web component, not the protein anchoring actin at the microvillus tip. Dystrophin links actin to laminin in skeletal muscle and is lost in Duchenne muscular dystrophy, not a microvillus tip protein. Titin anchors myosin thick filaments to the Z line of the sarcomere, not the actin core of microvilli. Myosin I attaches the actin core laterally to the microvillus plasma membrane; villin anchors it at the tip. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Enterocytes, Fig. 17.21). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tight junctions in the ileum are relatively impermeable, so solutes must be moved across by active transport (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: elongated mitochondria cluster in the apical enterocyte cytoplasm, between the terminal web and the nucleus (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron transport","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"B":"Spectrin is a terminal web component, not the protein anchoring actin at the microvillus tip.","D":"Dystrophin links actin to laminin in skeletal muscle and is lost in Duchenne muscular dystrophy, not a microvillus tip protein.","E":"Titin anchors myosin thick filaments to the Z line of the sarcomere, not the actin core of microvilli.","A":"Myosin I attaches the actin core laterally to the microvillus plasma membrane; villin anchors it at the tip."}},{"id":"sdl23_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibacterial products are found in Paneth cell secretory vesicles?","choices":{"A":"Lysozyme and α-defensins","B":"Mucinogen and trefoil factors","C":"Pepsinogen and gastric lipase","D":"Secretin and cholecystokinin","E":"Intrinsic factor and gastrin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Paneth cell vesicles contain lysozyme, α-defensins, other glycoproteins, an arginine-rich protein, and zinc. Mucinogen comes from goblet cells, and the hormones come from enteroendocrine cells. Pepsinogen and gastric lipase are gastric chief cell products, not Paneth cell contents. Intrinsic factor and gastrin are gastric parietal cell and G-cell products, not Paneth cell antimicrobial granules. First Aid states Paneth cells in the crypts of Lieberkühn secrete defensins, lysozyme, and TNF, not mucinogen and trefoil factors. First Aid states Paneth cells in the crypts of Lieberkühn secrete defensins, lysozyme, and TNF, not secretin and cholecystokinin. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lysozyme digests the cell walls of certain groups of bacteria (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: Paneth cells may occasionally be found in small numbers in the normal colon, and their number may rise in some diseases (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Pepsinogen and gastric lipase are gastric chief cell products, not Paneth cell contents.","E":"Intrinsic factor and gastrin are gastric parietal cell and G-cell products, not Paneth cell antimicrobial granules.","B":"First Aid states Paneth cells in the crypts of Lieberkühn secrete defensins, lysozyme, and TNF, not mucinogen and trefoil factors.","D":"First Aid states Paneth cells in the crypts of Lieberkühn secrete defensins, lysozyme, and TNF, not secretin and cholecystokinin."}},{"id":"sdl23_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Why is the basal portion of a goblet cell intensely basophilic?","choices":{"A":"Abundant smooth ER with lipid droplets","B":"Dense packing of acidic mucinogen granules","C":"Stacked apical mitochondria with actin","D":"Large glycogen stores with many lysosomes","E":"Heterochromatic nucleus with extensive rER"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The narrow basal stem holds a heterochromatic nucleus, extensive rER, and free ribosomes, which make it intensely basophilic. The mucinogen granules sit at the apex. Smooth ER with lipid droplets characterizes lipid-processing enterocytes, not the basophilic goblet cell stem. Mucinogen granules fill the apical cup, not the basal stem, and are lost on routine H&E. Mitochondria do gather in the basal cytoplasm, but the intense basophilia comes from the heterochromatic nucleus, extensive rER, and free ribosomes. The narrow basal stem of a goblet cell is filled with nucleus, rER, and free ribosomes, not glycogen stores and lysosomes. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a large Golgi apparatus forms a wide cup around newly formed mucinogen granules above the goblet cell nucleus (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: microvilli are more obvious on immature goblet cells in the deep half of the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Smooth ER with lipid droplets characterizes lipid-processing enterocytes, not the basophilic goblet cell stem.","B":"Mucinogen granules fill the apical cup, not the basal stem, and are lost on routine H&E.","C":"Mitochondria do gather in the basal cytoplasm, but the intense basophilia comes from.","D":"The narrow basal stem of a goblet cell is filled with nucleus, rER, and free ribosomes, not glycogen stores and lysosomes."}},{"id":"sdl23_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":5,"stem":"What activates the apical receptors of open-type intestinal enteroendocrine cells?","choices":{"A":"Stretch of the muscularis","B":"Taste receptor stimulation","C":"Vagal acetylcholine release","D":"Circulating thyroid hormone","E":"Lamina propria histamine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Open enteroendocrine cells have taste receptors on their apical membrane, and activating them triggers a G-protein cascade and peptide release. Closed cells lie mainly in the lower gland. Muscularis stretch is a mechanical stimulus to the enteric nervous system, not the activator of apical receptors on open enteroendocrine cells. Vagal acetylcholine acts from the basal side, whereas open-type cells sense lumen contents through apical taste receptors. Lamina propria histamine acts from below, not on apical luminal receptors of open-type cells. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP released in the small intestine stimulates insulin release from the pancreas (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: somatostatin and histamine from enteroendocrine cells act as paracrine hormones that do not circulate (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"A":"Muscularis stretch is a mechanical stimulus to the enteric nervous system, not the activator of apical receptors on open enteroendocrine cells.","C":"Vagal acetylcholine acts from the basal side, whereas open-type cells sense lumen contents through apical taste receptors.","E":"Lamina propria histamine acts from below, not on apical luminal receptors of open-type cells."}},{"id":"sdl23_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is the small intestinal stem cell niche located?","choices":{"A":"Tips of the intestinal villi","B":"Middle third of each villus surface","C":"Within the muscularis mucosae layer","D":"Lower half of the intestinal gland","E":"Within the duodenal submucosal glands"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Stem cells sit at the base of the intestinal gland, and the niche is restricted to the lower half of the gland. Cells then migrate upward onto the villus and are shed at the tip. Villus tips are where aged cells undergo apoptosis and are shed, not where stem cells live. The mid-villus holds migrating differentiated cells; proliferation is confined to the crypt. The muscularis mucosae is a smooth muscle layer, not an epithelial stem cell niche. Duodenal submucosal (Brunner) glands secrete alkaline mucus and do not host the intestinal stem cell niche. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in celiac disease, histology shows villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and intraepithelial lymphocytosis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: the niche is also called the zone of cell replication (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Villus tips are where aged cells undergo apoptosis and are shed, not where stem cells live.","B":"The mid-villus holds migrating differentiated cells; proliferation is confined to the crypt.","C":"The muscularis mucosae is a smooth muscle layer, not an epithelial stem cell niche.","E":"Duodenal submucosal (Brunner) glands secrete alkaline mucus and do not host the intestinal stem cell niche."}},{"id":"sdl23_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Where do aging absorptive cells of the small intestine undergo apoptosis and slough off?","choices":{"A":"Tips of the villi","B":"Base of the crypts","C":"Lower half of the gland","D":"Over Peyer patch domes","E":"Between gland openings"},"correct":"A","explanation":"New cells arise from stem cells at the base of the intestinal glands and migrate upward onto the villi, reaching the villus tips, where they undergo apoptosis and are shed into the lumen. The base of the crypts is where Paneth cells, which migrate downward, reside. The lower half of the gland is the stem cell niche where new cells are produced. The domes over Peyer patches hold M cells for antigen sampling, not the usual extrusion zone. Shedding between gland openings is the pattern described for the large intestine, which has no villi. Sources: Pawlina ch 5 and ch 17, epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Small intestinal cells move from crypt stem cells up to the villus tips, while Paneth cells move down (Pawlina ch 17). COMLEX: Rapid epithelial turnover explains mucositis and diarrhea after chemotherapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The base of the crypts is where","C":"The lower half of the gland is","D":"The domes over Peyer patches hold M","E":"Shedding between gland openings is the pattern"}},{"id":"sdl23_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Increased expression of which transcription factor commits crypt progenitor cells to the secretory lineage?","choices":{"A":"Notch1","B":"Math1","C":"Cdx2","D":"Pdx1","E":"Ptf1a"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cells committed to the secretory lineage (goblet, enteroendocrine, and Paneth cells) have increased Math1 expression. When Math1 is inhibited, cells follow the default pathway and become enterocytes. Pdx1 (pancreatic and duodenal homeobox 1) specifies the duodenum during gut tube patterning and is upregulated in the developing pancreatic buds; it does not choose the crypt secretory lineage. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: enteroendocrine cells appear to divide only once before differentiating (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: enteroendocrine cells migrate with absorptive and goblet cells, but more slowly (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Pdx1 (pancreatic and duodenal homeobox 1) specifies the duodenum during gut tube patterning and is upregulated in the developing pancreatic buds; it does not choose the crypt secretory lineage."}},{"id":"sdl23_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":1,"stem":"Where are Peyer patches preferentially located in the ileal wall?","choices":{"A":"Along the mesenteric border itself","B":"Within the inner muscularis externa","C":"Opposite the mesenteric attachment","D":"Beneath the serosal mesothelium","E":"Within the myenteric nerve plexus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Peyer patches are large and numerous in the ileum and lie preferentially on the side of the intestine opposite the mesenteric attachment. They sit in the lamina propria and often extend into the submucosa. Peyer patches favor the antimesenteric wall, not the mesenteric border. Peyer patches sit in the lamina propria and submucosa, not within the muscularis externa. Peyer patches lie in the lamina propria and submucosa, not beneath the serosa. The myenteric plexus is a nerve network between muscle layers, not the site of Peyer patches. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peyer patches are unencapsulated lymphoid tissue in the lamina propria and submucosa of the ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Peyer patches). COMLEX: in gross specimens, these aggregated nodules look like clusters of white specks (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Peyer patches favor the antimesenteric wall, not the mesenteric border.","B":"Peyer patches sit in the lamina propria and submucosa, not within the muscularis externa.","D":"Peyer patches lie in the lamina propria and submucosa, not beneath the serosa.","E":"The myenteric plexus is a nerve network between muscle layers, not the site of Peyer patches."}},{"id":"sdl23_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":1,"stem":"From what is the secretory component of secretory IgA derived?","choices":{"A":"Plasma cell J chain peptide","B":"Cleaved polymeric Ig receptor","C":"Goblet cell mucin glycoprotein","D":"Paneth cell defensin precursor","E":"Hepatocyte bile acid carrier"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After the receptor-dIgA complex reaches the apical surface, the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor is cleaved. Its bound extracellular portion becomes the secretory component. The J chain comes from the plasma cell and links the IgA dimer. Goblet cell mucin forms the protective mucus gel, not the secretory component of sIgA. Paneth cell defensins are innate antimicrobial peptides, unrelated to the secretory component. The secretory component is the cleaved extracellular part of the enterocyte pIgR that stays bound to the IgA dimer, not a hepatocyte bile acid carrier. Pawlina describes the J chain as a plasma-cell product within dimeric IgA. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (GALT; Immunoglobulin-mediated responses, Fig. 17.24; Folder 17.5). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the polymeric Ig receptor carries dIgA across the enterocyte by transcytosis; only at the apical surface is it cleaved, and the retained fragment is the secretory component (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the receptor is a 75-kDa transmembrane glycoprotein synthesized by enterocytes (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Goblet cell mucin forms the protective mucus gel, not the secretory component of sIgA.","D":"Paneth cell defensins are innate antimicrobial peptides, unrelated to the secretory component.","E":"The secretory component is the cleaved extracellular part of the enterocyte pIgR that stays bound to the IgA dimer, not a hepatocyte bile acid carrier.","A":"Pawlina describes the J chain as a plasma-cell product within dimeric IgA."}},{"id":"sdl23_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibody do most plasma cells in the intestinal lamina propria secrete?","choices":{"A":"Monomeric IgG","B":"Pentameric IgM","C":"Monomeric IgE","D":"Monomeric IgD","E":"Dimeric IgA"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Most lamina propria plasma cells secrete dimeric IgA rather than IgG. Some make pentameric IgM or IgE, and IgM can use a similar transcytotic route. IgE is made by only some lamina propria plasma cells; most make dimeric IgA. IgD is a membrane B-cell receptor on mature naive B cells alongside IgM, not the antibody lamina propria plasma cells secrete. IgG is the more common antibody elsewhere, but most lamina propria plasma cells of the intestine secrete dimeric IgA. Some intestinal plasma cells produce pentameric IgM, but most secrete dimeric IgA. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (GALT; Immunoglobulin-mediated responses, Fig. 17.24; Folder 17.5). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B cells stimulated in Peyer patch germinal centers become IgA-secreting plasma cells that settle in the lamina propria (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Peyer patches). COMLEX: some IgE binds lamina propria mast cells and sensitizes them to luminal antigens (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"IgE is made by only some lamina propria plasma cells; most make dimeric IgA.","D":"IgD is a membrane B-cell receptor on mature naive B cells alongside IgM, not the antibody lamina propria plasma cells secrete.","A":"IgG is the more common antibody elsewhere, but most lamina propria plasma cells of the intestine secrete dimeric IgA.","B":"Some intestinal plasma cells produce pentameric IgM, but most secrete dimeric IgA."}},{"id":"sdl23_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man has burning epigastric pain that starts about an hour after meals and is partly relieved by food. He has taken ibuprofen daily for knee osteoarthritis for two years. The abdomen is soft, with mild epigastric tenderness and no peritoneal signs. Biopsy just distal to the pylorus shows branched tubular glands in the submucosa, and fluid from those glands has a bicarbonate concentration of 70 mEq/L. Besides protecting the mucosa, what does this secretion do?","choices":{"A":"Secretes lysozyme and defensins into the crypt lumens","B":"Brings chyme near the pancreatic enzyme pH optimum","C":"Absorbs conjugated bile acids from the passing chyme","D":"Produces intrinsic factor needed for vitamin B12 uptake","E":"Concentrates dimeric IgA for transport to the lumen"},"correct":"B","explanation":"These bicarbonate-rich submucosal glands raise the pH of acidic gastric chyme into the range where pancreatic enzymes work best. A biopsy just beyond the pylorus showing branched tubular glands identifies that secretion. Lysozyme and defensins come from Paneth cells at crypt bases, not from these alkaline submucosal glands. Conjugated bile acids are reclaimed by ileal enterocytes, far distal to this biopsy site. Intrinsic factor is produced by gastric parietal cells, which are not in this duodenal sample. Dimeric IgA reaches the lumen by enterocyte transcytosis, not by secretion from these glands.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bicarbonate from Brunner glands neutralizes acid, and pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate secretion is stimulated by secretin (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI secretory products). COMLEX: the duct of each Brunner gland opens into the lumen of an intestinal crypt (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lysozyme and defensins come from Paneth cells at crypt","C":"Conjugated bile acids are reclaimed by ileal enterocytes, far","D":"Intrinsic factor is produced by gastric parietal cells, which","E":"Dimeric IgA reaches the lumen by enterocyte transcytosis, not"}},{"id":"sdl23_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has a segment of small bowel removed after a stab wound. She had been well, with no chronic diarrhea or weight loss. At laparotomy the injured loop lay free on a mesentery in the left upper abdomen. The specimen has tall villi and tall, closely spaced circular folds, with no submucosal glands and no large lymphoid aggregates. Which segment was removed?","choices":{"A":"Duodenum","B":"Ileum","C":"Jejunum","D":"Appendix","E":"Rectum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Tall villi with tall, closely spaced circular folds, and neither submucosal glands nor large lymphoid aggregates, identify jejunum. Trauma resection of that pattern is a mid-small-bowel segment rather than duodenum or ileum. Submucosal alkaline glands are the landmark of the proximal duodenum, and this specimen has none. Large aggregated lymphoid nodules are characteristic of ileum, and this specimen lacks them. The appendix is a colonic outpouching and does not bear tall circular folds of this kind. Rectal mucosa is flat, without villi or plicae circulares.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ileum contains Peyer patches, and its plicae circulares are limited to its proximal part (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: the jejunum gradually changes its morphology as it becomes the ileum, with no sharp boundary (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Submucosal alkaline glands are the landmark of the proximal","B":"Large aggregated lymphoid nodules are characteristic of ileum, and","D":"The appendix is a colonic outpouching and does not","E":"Rectal mucosa is flat, without villi or plicae"}},{"id":"sdl23_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":5,"stem":"A 39-year-old man is stabbed in the posterior abdomen. He is tender in the right upper quadrant, the pulse is 108/min, and blood pressure is 104/68 mm Hg. CT shows the injury confined to the portion of duodenum fixed against the posterior abdominal wall. Which layer forms the outer covering of this segment?","choices":{"A":"Serosa","B":"Mesothelium","C":"Submucosa","D":"Muscularis","E":"Adventitia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The retroperitoneal duodenum is fixed to the posterior wall and is covered by adventitia rather than a free serosa. The stab wound is limited to that fixed segment. A serosal coat covers intraperitoneal bowel, which this fixed duodenal segment is not. Mesothelium lines a peritoneal surface, and this segment has lost that free surface. Submucosa lies deep to the mucosa and is not the outer covering of the wall. Muscularis is an inner muscle coat and is not the outermost layer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the duodenum is about 25 cm long and is the first, shortest, and widest part of the small intestine (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the myenteric plexus between the two muscularis externa layers contains postsynaptic parasympathetic neuron cell bodies (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"A":"A serosal coat covers intraperitoneal bowel, which this fixed","B":"Mesothelium lines a peritoneal surface, and this segment has","C":"Submucosa lies deep to the mucosa and is not","D":"Muscularis is an inner muscle coat and is not"}},{"id":"sdl23_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman has months of diarrhea and fatigue. A sister has autoimmune thyroid disease, and she has eaten wheat daily. She is pale and the abdomen is soft, without a rash. Hemoglobin is 9.2 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL) and IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase is 96 U/mL (reference range: <4 U/mL). Duodenal biopsy shows a flat mucosa, crypt hyperplasia, and many intraepithelial lymphocytes. Which surface-amplifying structure is lost?","choices":{"A":"Plicae","B":"Villi","C":"Crypts","D":"Teniae","E":"Rugae"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This is celiac disease, and the flat mucosa means the villi have been lost while crypts hypertrophy. Iron deficiency, a high anti-tissue transglutaminase titer, and intraepithelial lymphocytes support that injury. Plicae are the circular folds of submucosa, and a flat mucosal biopsy does not describe their loss. Crypts are hyperplastic in this biopsy, so they have not been lost. Teniae are longitudinal muscle bands of the colon and are not a small-bowel surface amplifier. Rugae are gastric folds and are not present in a duodenal biopsy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease mainly affects the distal duodenum and/or proximal jejunum and is linked to HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac serology includes IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase and anti-endomysial antibodies (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — carbohydrate digestion and lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Plicae are the circular folds of submucosa, and a","C":"Crypts are hyperplastic in this biopsy, so they have","D":"Teniae are longitudinal muscle bands of the colon and","E":"Rugae are gastric folds and are not present in"}},{"id":"sdl23_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy has leg swelling and pale, bulky stools. He has never had abdominal surgery, and there is no jaundice. He has pitting edema to the knees and a soft abdomen. Serum albumin is 2.1 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Small-bowel biopsy shows dilated central lymphatic capillaries in the villi. Why do absorbed lipoprotein particles normally enter these vessels rather than villus blood capillaries?","choices":{"A":"Villus blood capillaries lack endothelial fenestrations","B":"Lymphatic capillaries actively pump in triglyceride","C":"Portal venous blood rapidly degrades fat particles","D":"Lipoproteins cross the tight junctions into lymph","E":"Particles are too large for the blood capillaries"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Chylomicrons are too large to enter villus blood capillaries, so they pass into the central lacteal. Edema, low albumin, fatty stools, and dilated villus lymphatics show that this route is blocked. Villus blood capillaries are fenestrated, so lack of fenestrations is not why fat particles avoid them. Lymphatic capillaries do not actively pump triglyceride into their lumen. Portal blood does not rapidly degrade chylomicrons, which normally bypass the portal vein. Lipoproteins do not cross tight junctions to reach lymph.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an enterocyte packages resynthesized lipid as chylomicrons and discharges it into the intercellular space (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: smooth muscle from the muscularis mucosae runs alongside each lacteal and may squeeze lymph out of it (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Villus blood capillaries are fenestrated, so lack of fenestrations","B":"Lymphatic capillaries do not actively pump triglyceride into their","C":"Portal blood does not rapidly degrade chylomicrons, which normally","D":"Lipoproteins do not cross tight junctions to reach"}},{"id":"sdl23_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old healthy man in a nutrition study has a jejunal biopsy taken while he is absorbing a mixed meal. He has no diarrhea or weight loss. The abdomen is soft and vital signs are normal. Electron microscopy shows widened spaces between the lateral surfaces of adjacent enterocytes. What structural change explains these widened spaces?","choices":{"A":"Rupture of the apical tight junction protein strands","B":"Loss of the underlying enterocyte basal lamina sheet","C":"Separation of interdigitating cell-surface folds","D":"Shedding of aged enterocytes at the villus tips","E":"Fusion of mucinogen granules at the cell apex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"During absorption the interdigitating lateral folds of neighboring enterocytes separate, opening the intercellular space as fluid and solute leave the cell. The biopsy was taken while absorption was active, which is when those spaces widen. The apical tight-junction strands are not ruptured by ordinary absorption. The enterocyte basal lamina remains intact during this widening. Shedding of aged cells occurs at the villus tip and does not open lateral spaces along the villus. Fusion of mucinogen granules is a goblet-cell event and does not widen lateral spaces.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the duodenum and jejunum, hypotonic contents allow water and small solutes to cross leaky tight junctions by solvent drag (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: contraction of the terminal web spreads the microvilli apart and exposes more surface area (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The apical tight-junction strands are not ruptured by ordinary","B":"The enterocyte basal lamina remains intact during this","D":"Shedding of aged cells occurs at the villus tip","E":"Fusion of mucinogen granules is a goblet-cell event and"}},{"id":"sdl23_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman has had watery diarrhea for four months, without fever or blood. Stool cultures and a test for Clostridium difficile toxin have been negative. She is not volume depleted, and the abdomen is soft and nontender. A jejunal biopsy shows, at the base of the glands, cells with basophilic basal cytoplasm and large eosinophilic refractile apical vesicles. What probably causes that eosinophilia?","choices":{"A":"Heme-rich cytochromes","B":"Glycogen granule stores","C":"Neutral mucin polymer","D":"Arginine-rich protein","E":"Iron-ferritin deposits"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Paneth-cell granules are eosinophilic because they are packed with arginine-rich antimicrobial proteins. Basal basophilia and apical refractile vesicles at the gland base identify those cells. Heme-rich cytochromes are not the material stored in these apical vesicles. Glycogen does not produce large refractile eosinophilic apical granules. Neutral mucin is pale on routine stains and belongs to goblet cells, not these basal cells. Iron-ferritin deposits are not the content of these apical secretory vesicles.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paneth cells in the crypts of Lieberkühn secrete defensins, lysozyme, and TNF (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: zinc is also stored in these refractile apical vesicles (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Heme-rich cytochromes are not the material stored in these apical","B":"Glycogen does not produce large refractile eosinophilic apical","C":"Neutral mucin is pale on routine stains and belongs","E":"Iron-ferritin deposits are not the content of these apical secretory"}},{"id":"sdl23_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":5,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has chronic loose stools without bleeding or weight loss. He does not use laxatives. An ileal biopsy stained with hematoxylin and eosin shows many epithelial cells with pale, empty-looking apical cups and narrow basal stems. Why does the apex of these cells look empty?","choices":{"A":"Lipid droplets dissolve in alcohol","B":"Mucinogen is lost during preparation","C":"Glycogen leaches out during fixation","D":"Apical vacuoles are filled with water","E":"Dying cells lose their organelles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"These are goblet cells, and their mucinogen is extracted during routine preparation, leaving an empty-looking cup. The narrow basal stem and pale apex are the usual appearance after that loss. Lipid droplets are not the material that normally fills these apical cups. Glycogen is not stored as a large apical cup in these cells. The apical cup is not a water-filled vacuole in the living cell. These pale cells are not dying cells that have lost their organelles.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in Barrett esophagus, the squamous epithelium is replaced by nonciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: the apical mucinogen mass in a goblet cell pushes on and distorts the shape of neighboring cells (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 2 — large-intestine absorptive and goblet cells","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"A":"Lipid droplets are not the material that normally fills","C":"Glycogen is not stored as a large apical cup","D":"The apical cup is not a water-filled vacuole in","E":"These pale cells are not dying cells that have"}},{"id":"sdl23_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman has episodic flushing and watery diarrhea that continues while she is fasting. A proton-pump inhibitor started for presumed reflux has not helped. The face is flushed and the abdomen is soft, without a mass. Chromogranin A is 280 ng/mL (reference range: <93 ng/mL). A duodenal biopsy shows nests of uniform cells with salt-and-pepper chromatin that stain for synaptophysin. From which epithelial cell type does this tumor most likely arise?","choices":{"A":"Enteroendocrine cells","B":"Absorptive enterocytes","C":"Intermediate goblet cells","D":"Microfold epithelial cells","E":"Myoepithelial basket cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Nests of uniform duodenal cells with salt-and-pepper chromatin and synaptophysin staining are enteroendocrine cells, and the elevated chromogranin A marks that same lineage. Flushing and fasting watery diarrhea fit hormone release from those cells. Absorptive enterocytes give rise to ordinary adenomas and carcinomas, not this neuroendocrine tumor. Goblet cells form mucin-producing tumors rather than a chromogranin-positive neuroendocrine tumor. Microfold cells sample antigen over lymphoid nodules and are not the source of this tumor. Myoepithelial basket cells are not a normal epithelial population of the duodenal mucosa.","boardPrep":"Step 1: motilin from the small intestine initiates gastric and intestinal motility (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: VIP, bombesin, and enkephalins are neurocrine peptides secreted by submucosal and myenteric nerve cells (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Absorptive enterocytes give rise to ordinary adenomas and carcinomas,","C":"Goblet cells form mucin-producing tumors rather than a chromogranin-positive neuroendocrine","D":"Microfold cells sample antigen over lymphoid nodules and are","E":"Myoepithelial basket cells are not a normal epithelial population"}},{"id":"sdl23_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman develops watery diarrhea five days after starting cytotoxic chemotherapy for lymphoma. She has had no sick contacts. She is afebrile, the abdomen is soft, and bowel sounds are active. The leukocyte count is 2,200/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL), and biopsy shows short, denuded villi. Why is the small-intestinal epithelium damaged so quickly?","choices":{"A":"Paneth cells migrating upward onto the villi","B":"Slow turnover of the mature villus enterocyte layer","C":"Continuous replacement from dividing crypt cells","D":"Stem cells concentrated near the outer villus tips","E":"Mature enterocytes dividing on the villus tips"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Villi are continuously replaced by cells born in the crypts, so killing those dividing cells strips the surface within days. Diarrhea a few days after cytotoxic chemotherapy, with short denuded villi, follows that rapid turnover. Paneth cells migrate downward, not upward onto the villi. Mature villus enterocytes turn over in days, so their replacement is not slow. The stem-cell niche sits in the crypt, not near the villus tips. Mature enterocytes on the villus tips do not divide.","boardPrep":"Step 1: intermediate cells in the niche usually undergo one or two divisions before committing to absorptive or goblet fates (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: cells shed at the villus tip are replaced by cells migrating upward from the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Paneth cells migrate downward, not upward onto the","B":"Mature villus enterocytes turn over in days, so their","D":"The stem-cell niche sits in the crypt, not near","E":"Mature enterocytes on the villus tips do not"}},{"id":"sdl23_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old healthy volunteer has crypt cells radiolabeled. He has no diarrhea. Days later most labeled cells are on the villi. One labeled population has moved down to the gland base, survives about four weeks, and no longer divides. Which cells are these?","choices":{"A":"Paneth cells","B":"Goblet cells","C":"Enterocytes","D":"Stem cells","E":"Plasma cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The cells that migrate downward, stop dividing, and live for weeks at the gland base are Paneth cells. The other labeled cohort has moved up onto the villi, which is the path of the shorter-lived lineages. Goblet cells migrate up the villus and are shed, rather than settling at the gland base for weeks. Enterocytes migrate onto the villus and are shed within days. Stem cells remain in the niche and continue to divide. Plasma cells reside in the lamina propria and are not labeled crypt epithelial cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: dead Paneth cells are replaced by differentiation of a nearby committed cell in the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: stem cells in the crypt base give rise to every mature cell type of the intestinal epithelium (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Goblet cells migrate up the villus and are shed,","C":"Enterocytes migrate onto the villus and are shed within","D":"Stem cells remain in the niche and continue to","E":"Plasma cells reside in the lamina propria and are"}},{"id":"sdl23_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy has had loose stools since infancy. A genetic variant blocks the transcription factor that commits intestinal stem cells to a secretory fate. Growth is normal and the abdomen is soft. A crypt-cell assay confirms failure of that secretory program. Which cells would differentiating crypt cells mainly become?","choices":{"A":"Mucus-secreting goblet cells","B":"Antimicrobial Paneth cells","C":"Hormone-secreting endocrine cells","D":"Absorptive enterocytes","E":"Antigen-sampling microfold cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"When the secretory-fate factor is blocked, crypt daughters default to absorptive enterocytes. Loose stools from infancy fit loss of the secretory lineages that this factor normally produces. Goblet cells are a secretory lineage and are not produced when that fate is blocked. Paneth cells are secretory cells and depend on the same blocked program. Enteroendocrine cells are part of the secretory lineage and are not the default. Microfold cells are a specialized secretory-related population and are not the default product.","boardPrep":"Step 1: intermediate cells committed to the absorptive fate lose their secretory granules and gather mitochondria, rER, and ribosomes (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: intermediate cells committed to the goblet fate develop a small rounded cluster of granules under the apical membrane (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Goblet cells are a secretory lineage and are not","B":"Paneth cells are secretory cells and depend on the","C":"Enteroendocrine cells are part of the secretory lineage and","E":"Microfold cells are a specialized secretory-related population and are"}},{"id":"sdl23_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":2,"stem":"A 23-year-old man develops fever and abdominal pain a week after returning from South Asia, where he did not take antibiotic prophylaxis. Temperature is 39.1°C, the pulse is 104/min, and the abdomen is tender. Blood culture grows Salmonella. The organisms crossed ileal epithelium where it covers large lymphatic nodules. Which route did they most likely use?","choices":{"A":"Microfold cell transcytosis","B":"Paracellular tight junctions","C":"Goblet cell mucus exocytosis","D":"Paneth cell phagocytosis","E":"Lacteal lymphatic uptake"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Salmonella crosses the epithelium over Peyer patches by transcytosis through microfold cells. Fever after travel and bacteremia from that ileal site match this entry path. Paracellular tight junctions are not the route these organisms use over the lymphoid nodules. Goblet-cell mucus release does not carry bacteria across the epithelium. Paneth cells secrete antimicrobials and do not phagocytose bacteria as the entry path. Lacteals receive chylomicrons and are not the epithelial crossing used here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peyer patches contain microfold cells that sample antigens and present them to immune cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Peyer patches). COMLEX: M cells have microfolds and a thin glycocalyx on their apical surface, not microvilli (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Paracellular tight junctions are not the route these organisms","C":"Goblet-cell mucus release does not carry bacteria across the","D":"Paneth cells secrete antimicrobials and do not phagocytose bacteria","E":"Lacteals receive chylomicrons and are not the epithelial crossing used"}},{"id":"sdl23_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman has had three episodes of giardiasis in two years. She is not taking immunosuppressants. She is afebrile and the abdomen is soft, without lymphadenopathy. Serum IgA is undetectable (reference range: 70–400 mg/dL), IgG is 1,100 mg/dL (reference range: 700–1,600 mg/dL), and IgM is normal. Which mucosal defense is mainly reduced?","choices":{"A":"Lysis of luminal bacteria by Paneth cell enzymes","B":"Sensitization of the lamina propria mast cells","C":"Opsonization of invading bacteria for neutrophils","D":"Blocking of microbial adhesion to the epithelium","E":"Activation of the classical complement pathway"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Secretory IgA blocks microbes from sticking to the epithelium, and its absence leaves that barrier down. Undetectable IgA with preserved IgG and IgM, plus repeated Giardia, localizes the defect to that adhesive block. Paneth-cell enzymes are intact in isolated IgA deficiency and are not the missing defense. Mast-cell sensitization is an IgE event and is not the function lost with absent IgA. Neutrophil opsonization is mainly an IgG function, and IgG is normal here. Classical complement activation depends on other isotypes, not on secretory IgA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretory IgA binds an HIV envelope glycoprotein and prevents viral attachment and internalization (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the GALT works as an immunologic barrier along the whole length of the gastrointestinal tract (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Paneth-cell enzymes are intact in isolated IgA deficiency and","B":"Mast-cell sensitization is an IgE event and is not","C":"Neutrophil opsonization is mainly an IgG function, and IgG","E":"Classical complement activation depends on other isotypes, not on secretory"}},{"id":"sdl23_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl has recurrent bacterial enteritis and grows poorly. She is not taking immunosuppressants. She is small for her age and the abdomen is soft. Lamina propria plasma cells secrete normal J-chain-linked IgA dimers, but intestinal secretions contain no IgA. Serum IgG is 900 mg/dL (reference range: 700–1,600 mg/dL). Which step most likely fails?","choices":{"A":"Class switching of mucosal B cells to the IgA isotype","B":"Linking of IgA monomers together by the J chain","C":"Homing of IgA plasma cells to the lamina propria","D":"Antigen uptake by the Peyer patch dendritic cells","E":"Receptor-mediated transcytosis across enterocytes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"IgA dimers are made normally but never reach the lumen, so receptor-mediated transcytosis across enterocytes has failed. Recurrent enteric infection with absent secretory IgA and intact plasma-cell dimers localizes the block to that transport step. Class switching has already produced IgA plasma cells, so that step succeeded. The J chain is linking the dimers, so monomer assembly is intact. IgA plasma cells are already in the lamina propria, so homing occurred. Antigen uptake by dendritic cells is upstream of plasma-cell formation, which is intact here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgM molecules use a similar receptor-mediated transcytosis pathway to reach the mucosal surface (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: dendritic cells and lymphocytes in the M-cell recess sample luminal antigen and can stimulate specific antibody production (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Class switching has already produced IgA plasma cells, so","B":"The J chain is linking the dimers, so monomer","C":"IgA plasma cells are already in the lamina propria,","D":"Antigen uptake by dendritic cells is upstream of plasma-cell"}},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old man has chronic diarrhea and a small-bowel stricture. He is thin and the abdomen is soft. A 60 cm resection shows, at the uninvolved margins, short villi, many goblet cells, no submucosal glands, and large lymphoid nodules opposite the mesenteric attachment. Loss of this segment most directly risks deficiency of which nutrient?","choices":{"A":"Folic acid","B":"Ferrous iron","C":"Dietary calcium","D":"Vitamin C","E":"Vitamin B12"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Short villi, abundant goblet cells, and lymphoid aggregates opposite the mesentery identify ileum, the site of vitamin B12 absorption. Resection of that segment removes the uptake of the intrinsic-factor complex. Folate is absorbed in the proximal small bowel, which this ileal pattern is not. Iron is absorbed in the duodenum, a segment that has submucosal glands this specimen lacks. Calcium absorption is not confined to the ileal segment marked by these lymphoid nodules. Vitamin C is not selectively dependent on this distal ileal segment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Iron is absorbed in the duodenum, folate in the duodenum and jejunum, and vitamin B12 with bile salts in the terminal ileum (First Aid 2025 GI physiology). COMLEX: Patients after ileal resection need lifelong vitamin B12 replacement that bypasses the gut.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Folate is absorbed in the proximal small bowel, which","B":"Iron is absorbed in the duodenum, a segment that","C":"Calcium absorption is not confined to the ileal segment","D":"Vitamin C is not selectively dependent on this distal ileal"}},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":3,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy has watery diarrhea, bloating, and gas after milk and sweetened juice, beginning three weeks after a viral gastroenteritis. He tolerated those drinks before the illness. He is afebrile, well hydrated, and the abdomen is soft and mildly distended. The stool osmotic gap is 140 mOsm/kg (values above 100 mOsm/kg indicate an osmotic diarrhea). Villi and crypts are normal in height, but electron microscopy shows short, sparse, irregular microvilli. Which loss best explains his symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Lacteal transport of chylomicrons","B":"Brush-border disaccharidase activity","C":"Paneth cell antimicrobial secretion","D":"Crypt chloride and water secretion","E":"Goblet cell release of surface mucus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Short sparse microvilli after a viral illness remove brush-border disaccharidases while the villus architecture stays normal. Osmotic diarrhea triggered by milk and sweetened juice follows that enzyme loss. Lacteal transport of chylomicrons is a fat pathway and does not explain sugar-triggered osmotic diarrhea. Paneth-cell secretion is antimicrobial and is not the function housed on these damaged microvilli. Crypt chloride secretion would cause secretory diarrhea, not an osmotic gap of 140 mOsm/kg. Goblet-cell mucus release does not digest milk sugar or juice sugars.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Brush-border disaccharidases and peptidases sit on the microvillus membrane, bringing digestion close to absorption (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: Transient sugar intolerance after viral gastroenteritis reflects brush-border injury and usually resolves.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — carbohydrate digestion and lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lacteal transport of chylomicrons is a fat pathway and","C":"Paneth-cell secretion is antimicrobial and is not the function","D":"Crypt chloride secretion would cause secretory diarrhea, not an","E":"Goblet-cell mucus release does not digest milk sugar or juice"}},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":3,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman has dyspepsia without weight loss. The abdomen is soft. Duodenal biopsies show scattered epithelial cells that reach the lumen, with receptors on a narrow apex and small granules packed under the nucleus against the basal lamina. After a fatty meal, release from some of these cells most directly produces which response?","choices":{"A":"Increased acid secretion and faster emptying","B":"Lysozyme discharge into the crypt lumen","C":"Mucus discharge onto the villus surface","D":"Gallbladder contraction and enzyme secretion","E":"Antigen transfer to dome lymphocytes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"These open enteroendocrine cells sense luminal fat and release a signal that contracts the gallbladder and drives pancreatic enzyme secretion. Their open apex and basal granules match that sensory cell. Increased acid secretion and faster emptying are not the response driven by these fat-sensing cells. Lysozyme discharge is a Paneth-cell action, and these cells are not confined to the crypt base. Mucus discharge is a goblet-cell action, and these cells have basal granules rather than an apical mucin cup. Antigen transfer to dome lymphocytes is a microfold-cell function over lymphoid tissue.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Duodenal I cells release cholecystokinin in response to fat and protein products, which contracts the gallbladder and stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: Fatty meals provoke biliary colic because cholecystokinin contracts a gallbladder holding stones.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 2 — large-intestine absorptive and goblet cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Increased acid secretion and faster emptying are not the","B":"Lysozyme discharge is a Paneth-cell action, and these cells","C":"Mucus discharge is a goblet-cell action, and these cells","E":"Antigen transfer to dome lymphocytes is a microfold-cell function"}},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":3,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman has bloating, loose stools, and fatigue. A cousin has type 1 diabetes, and she eats wheat daily. She is pale and the abdomen is soft. Hemoglobin is 10.1 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL), IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase is 140 U/mL (reference range: <4 U/mL), and anti-endomysial antibody is positive. Duodenal biopsy shows flat villi, many intraepithelial lymphocytes, and long crypts full of mitotic figures. Which mechanism best explains the crypt changes?","choices":{"A":"Arrested migration of cells out of crypts","B":"Neoplastic transformation of stem cells","C":"Conversion of crypt cells to Paneth cells","D":"Slower shedding of cells at the villus tips","E":"Faster villus loss driving crypt output"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Celiac disease destroys villus cells quickly, and the crypts increase their output to replace them. A high anti-tissue transglutaminase titer, endomysial antibody, flat villi, and intraepithelial lymphocytes define that injury. Cells are not arrested inside the crypts; mitoses are increased because output has risen. The crowded crypt mitoses are regenerative, not a neoplastic transformation of stem cells. The crypts are not converting into Paneth cells. Shedding at the villus tip is faster, not slower, in this injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In celiac disease, epithelial turnover is accelerated, villi shorten, and crypts become hyperplastic as a compensatory response (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Villous atrophy with crypt hyperplasia and intraepithelial lymphocytes on duodenal biopsy confirms the diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cells are not arrested inside the crypts; mitoses are","B":"The crowded crypt mitoses are regenerative, not a neoplastic","C":"The crypts are not converting into Paneth","D":"Shedding at the villus tip is faster, not slower,"}},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":3,"stem":"A 2-month-old girl, born at term, receives an oral live-attenuated rotavirus vaccine and continues to feed well. Examination is normal. Weeks later her stool contains rotavirus-specific antibody that blocks viral attachment to enterocytes, while serum antibody remains low. Stool IgA is present and serum IgG is 450 mg/dL (reference range for age: 200–600 mg/dL). Which sequence best describes how this luminal antibody was generated?","choices":{"A":"M cell uptake, follicle activation, dimeric IgA, pIgR transport","B":"Enterocyte uptake, splenic activation, IgG, paracellular leak","C":"Paneth cell uptake, follicle activation, IgM, lacteal transport","D":"Goblet cell uptake, thymic activation, IgE, mast cell release","E":"M cell uptake, nodal activation, IgG, Fc receptor transport"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Luminal IgA after an oral vaccine is generated by microfold-cell uptake, lymphoid-follicle activation, dimeric IgA, and polymeric-immunoglobulin-receptor transport. Low serum antibody with antibody in the stool fits that mucosal path. Enterocyte uptake with splenic IgG and a paracellular leak would raise serum antibody, which is low. Paneth cells do not take up vaccine virus, and lacteals do not deliver the luminal antibody. Goblet cells and thymic IgE are not the route that puts blocking antibody in the stool. Nodal IgG carried by Fc receptors would appear in serum, which stays low here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Intestinal plasma cells secrete dimeric IgA that is carried across enterocytes by the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor and released as secretory IgA (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: Oral vaccines work by inducing mucosal secretory IgA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Enterocyte uptake with splenic IgG and a paracellular leak","C":"Paneth cells do not take up vaccine virus, and","D":"Goblet cells and thymic IgE are not the route","E":"Nodal IgG carried by Fc receptors would appear in"}},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman remains anemic despite correctly taken oral ferrous sulfate. She has heavy menses. She is pale and the abdomen is soft. Hemoglobin is 9.1 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL), and serum folate and vitamin B12 are normal. Biopsies show disease confined to the single small-bowel segment where dietary iron is normally absorbed. Which histologic feature would the normal wall of that segment display?","choices":{"A":"Peyer patches opposite the mesentery","B":"Teniae coli in the muscularis","C":"Mucous glands in the submucosa","D":"Tall folds with no submucosal glands","E":"Parietal cells in deep glands"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dietary iron is absorbed in the duodenum, whose wall contains mucous glands in the submucosa. Disease limited to that segment, with folate and B12 spared, points to this proximal site. Peyer patches opposite the mesentery mark ileum, which is not the iron-absorbing segment. Teniae coli are a large-bowel muscle feature and are absent from small bowel. Tall folds without submucosal glands describe jejunum, distal to the iron-absorbing segment. Parietal cells belong to the stomach, not to the small-bowel segment that absorbs iron.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Brunner glands in the submucosa identify the duodenum, which is also the main site of iron absorption (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: Proximal mucosal disease such as celiac disease often presents with iron deficiency that resists oral iron.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Peyer patches opposite the mesentery mark ileum, which is","B":"Teniae coli are a large-bowel muscle feature and are","D":"Tall folds without submucosal glands describe jejunum, distal to","E":"Parietal cells belong to the stomach, not to the"}},{"id":"sdl23_q31","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":1,"stem":"The jejunal submucosa is distinguished by the absence of which pair of structures?","choices":{"A":"Villi and microvilli","B":"Plicae circulares and enterocytes","C":"Goblet cells and Paneth cells","D":"Brunner glands and Peyer patches","E":"Crypts of Lieberkühn and enteroendocrine cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Jejunal submucosa lacks both Brunner glands and aggregated lymphatic nodules. Villi and microvilli are prominent in the jejunum and are not the structures missing from its submucosa. Plicae circulares are tallest and most numerous in the jejunum, and enterocytes line them. Goblet cells and Paneth cells are epithelial, and Paneth cells sit in jejunal crypts. Crypts and enteroendocrine cells are part of jejunal mucosa.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Villi and microvilli are prominent in the jejunum and are not the structures missing from its submucosa","B":"Plicae circulares are tallest and most numerous in the jejunum, and enterocytes line them","C":"Goblet cells and Paneth cells are epithelial, and Paneth cells sit in jejunal crypts","E":"Crypts and enteroendocrine cells are part of jejunal mucosa"}},{"id":"sdl23_q32","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old man has right-lower-quadrant pain and a nonbloody loose stool for three months. He has lost 4 kg and has a draining perianal tract. Temperature is 37.8°C, and the right lower quadrant is tender without rebound. Hemoglobin is 12.4 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL) and albumin is 3.4 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Resection of the distal small bowel shows large lymphoid aggregates on the antimesenteric wall that reach the submucosa. Which segment was removed?","choices":{"A":"Ileum","B":"Proximal jejunum","C":"First portion of the duodenum","D":"Gastric antrum","E":"Appendix"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Peyer patches, aggregated lymphoid nodules on the antimesenteric wall that often enter the submucosa, mark the ileum. Proximal jejunum has the tallest plicae and no aggregated lymphatic nodules in the submucosa. The first portion of the duodenum is identified by submucosal Brunner glands, not Peyer patches. The gastric antrum has gastric pits and glands, not antimesenteric Peyer patches. The appendix is large intestine and is not the antimesenteric small-bowel segment that bears Peyer patches.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Proximal jejunum has the tallest plicae and no aggregated lymphatic nodules in the submucosa","C":"The first portion of the duodenum is identified by submucosal Brunner glands, not Peyer patches","D":"The gastric antrum has gastric pits and glands, not antimesenteric Peyer patches","E":"The appendix is large intestine and is not the antimesenteric small-bowel segment that bears Peyer patches"}},{"id":"sdl23_q33","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":1,"stem":"Which tissue forms the core of an intestinal villus?","choices":{"A":"Submucosa","B":"Muscularis externa","C":"Serosa","D":"A thick band of circular muscle","E":"Lamina propria"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A villus is a mucosal projection whose core is lamina propria covered by simple columnar epithelium. Submucosa is the core of a plica circularis, not of a villus. Muscularis externa is the deep muscle coat and does not form the villus core. Serosa is the outer peritoneal coat, far from the villus. Circular muscle belongs to the muscularis externa, not the villus core.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Submucosa is the core of a plica circularis, not of a villus","B":"Muscularis externa is the deep muscle coat and does not form the villus core","C":"Serosa is the outer peritoneal coat, far from the villus","D":"Circular muscle belongs to the muscularis externa, not the villus core"}},{"id":"sdl23_q34","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Which apical specialization do M cells have in place of a brush border?","choices":{"A":"Motile cilia","B":"Microfolds","C":"Stereocilia","D":"A thick keratin layer","E":"Kinocilia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"M cells overlying Peyer patches have apical microfolds instead of microvilli. Motile cilia are not the apical surface of intestinal M cells. Stereocilia are long actin processes of other epithelia, not the M-cell surface. A keratin layer is a feature of stratified squamous surfaces, not M cells. Kinocilia are microtubule-based processes and are not the M-cell apex.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Motile cilia are not the apical surface of intestinal M cells","C":"Stereocilia are long actin processes of other epithelia, not the M-cell surface","D":"A keratin layer is a feature of stratified squamous surfaces, not M cells","E":"Kinocilia are microtubule-based processes and are not the M-cell apex"}},{"id":"sdl23_q35","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy has fever and watery diarrhea two days after a preschool picnic. He is drinking, the abdomen is soft, and there is no blood in the stool. Temperature is 38.2°C. Sodium is 136 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L) and potassium is 3.8 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). A research biopsy over an ileal lymphoid nodule shows epithelial cells whose basolateral recess holds lymphocytes and dendritic cells. Which cell carried intact luminal antigen to those immune cells?","choices":{"A":"M cell","B":"Paneth cell","C":"Goblet cell","D":"Chief cell","E":"Parietal cell"},"correct":"A","explanation":"M cells endocytose intact luminal antigen and deliver it by transcytosis into a deep basolateral pocket that holds lymphocytes and dendritic cells. Paneth cells sit at the crypt base and release antimicrobial granules, and they do not ferry antigen into a lymphocyte pocket. Goblet cells secrete mucin and do not transcytose intact antigen to dendritic cells. Chief cells are gastric and secrete pepsinogen. Parietal cells are gastric and secrete acid and intrinsic factor.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Paneth cells sit at the crypt base and release antimicrobial granules, and they do not ferry antigen into a lymphocyte pocket","C":"Goblet cells secrete mucin and do not transcytose intact antigen to dendritic cells","D":"Chief cells are gastric and secrete pepsinogen","E":"Parietal cells are gastric and secrete acid and intrinsic factor"}}],"24":[{"id":"sdl24_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":1,"stem":"How many teniae coli run along the cecum and colon?","choices":{"A":"Two opposing bands","B":"Four paired bands","C":"Five irregular bands","D":"Three equal bands","E":"Six spiral bands"},"correct":"D","explanation":"There are three narrowed, thickened, equally spaced teniae coli, formed from the outer longitudinal layer of the muscularis externa. Two bands is too few; the outer longitudinal muscle condenses into three equally spaced teniae. There are three teniae coli, not four paired bands. The teniae are three equally spaced bands, not five irregular ones. The teniae are three straight, equally spaced bands, not six spiral bands. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; gross features, Fig. 17.28; Muscularis externa). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: between the teniae, the outer longitudinal layer of the colon is an extremely thin sheet (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: omental appendices are small fat-filled peritoneal projections on the outer colonic surface (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Two bands is too few.","B":"There are three teniae coli, not four paired bands.","C":"The teniae are three equally spaced bands, not five irregular ones.","E":"The teniae are three straight, equally spaced bands, not six spiral bands."}},{"id":"sdl24_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":1,"stem":"In which parts of the large intestine are teniae coli absent?","choices":{"A":"Cecum, ascending, and transverse colon","B":"Transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon","C":"Ascending colon, cecum, and appendix","D":"Descending colon, sigmoid, and rectum","E":"Rectum, anal canal, and appendix"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Teniae coli are seen mainly in the cecum and colon. In the rectum, anal canal, and vermiform appendix, the outer longitudinal muscle is a uniformly thick layer, as in the small intestine. The cecum and ascending and transverse colon are exactly where teniae coli are seen. The transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon all carry teniae coli. The cecum and ascending colon have teniae; only the appendix of this group lacks them. The descending and sigmoid colon have teniae; only the rectum of this group lacks them. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; gross features, Fig. 17.28; Muscularis externa). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the appendix differs from the cecum by having a uniform layer of longitudinal muscle in its muscularis externa (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the anal canal averages 4 cm and runs from the upper pelvic diaphragm to the anus (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The cecum and ascending and transverse colon are exactly where teniae coli are seen.","B":"The transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon all carry teniae coli.","C":"The cecum and ascending colon have teniae; only the appendix of this group lacks them.","D":"The descending and sigmoid colon have teniae; only the rectum of this group lacks them."}},{"id":"sdl24_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":1,"stem":"What are omental appendices of the colon?","choices":{"A":"Fatty projections of the serosa","B":"Mucosal folds over lymphoid nodules","C":"Submucosal outpouchings of the wall","D":"Bands of longitudinal smooth muscle","E":"Sacculations between muscle bands"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Omental appendices are small fatty projections of the serosa on the outer colonic surface. Teniae are muscle bands, and haustra are sacculations between them. Mucosal folds over lymphoid nodules are internal features, whereas omental appendices hang from the outer serosal surface. Submucosal outpouchings describe diverticula, not the fatty serosal tags called omental appendices. Bands of longitudinal smooth muscle are the teniae coli, thickenings of the outer muscularis externa, not omental appendices. Sacculations between the muscle bands are the haustra coli, not the fatty serosal omental appendices. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; gross features, Fig. 17.28; Muscularis externa). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: haustra coli are the visible sacculations between the teniae on the external surface of the cecum and colon (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the colon is subdivided into ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid parts by anatomic location (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mucosal folds over lymphoid nodules are internal features, whereas omental appendices hang from the outer serosal surface.","C":"Submucosal outpouchings describe diverticula, not the fatty serosal tags called omental appendices.","D":"Bands of longitudinal smooth muscle are the teniae coli, thickenings of the outer muscularis externa, not omental appendices.","E":"Sacculations between the muscle bands are the haustra coli, not the fatty serosal omental appendices."}},{"id":"sdl24_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the primary function of colonic columnar absorptive cells?","choices":{"A":"Absorbing most dietary amino acids","B":"Secreting brush-border disaccharidases","C":"Reabsorbing water and electrolytes","D":"Transporting dimeric IgA to the lumen","E":"Producing antimicrobial defensins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The primary function of colonic columnar absorptive cells is reabsorbing water and electrolytes. They use the same Na+/K+-ATPase-driven system as small intestinal enterocytes. Most dietary amino acids are absorbed in the small intestine, not by colonocytes, whose role is water and electrolyte reabsorption. Colonocytes do not express digestive enzymes in their glycocalyx, unlike small intestinal enterocytes. Defensins come from Paneth cells, which are normally absent from the human large intestine. IgA transcytosis is not the function of these absorptive cells. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic absorptive cells are essentially identical in morphology to small intestinal enterocytes (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the principal functions of the large intestine are electrolyte and water reabsorption and elimination of undigested waste (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Most dietary amino acids are absorbed in the small intestine, not by colonocytes, whose role is water and electrolyte reabsorption.","B":"Colonocytes do not express digestive enzymes in their glycocalyx, unlike small intestinal enterocytes.","E":"Defensins come from Paneth cells, which are normally absent from the human large intestine.","D":"IgA transcytosis is not the function of these absorptive cells."}},{"id":"sdl24_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which epithelial cell type of the small intestine is normally absent from the human colon?","choices":{"A":"Paneth cells","B":"Goblet cells","C":"Enteroendocrine cells","D":"Absorptive cells","E":"Stem cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The colonic epithelium has the same cell types as the small intestine except Paneth cells, which are normally absent in humans. Goblet cells are more numerous in the colon than in the small intestine and lubricate firm contents. Enteroendocrine cells are present in colonic glands and are renewed from the same stem cells. Absorptive cells are the predominant colonic cell and reabsorb water and electrolytes. Stem cells sit in the lower third of the colonic gland and renew every lineage. Sources: Pawlina ch 17, large intestine mucosa.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The colon has absorptive, goblet, enteroendocrine, and stem cells but no Paneth cells (Pawlina ch 17). COMLEX: Paneth cells in a left colon biopsy suggest metaplasia from chronic injury such as IBD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Goblet cells are more numerous in the","C":"Enteroendocrine cells are present in colonic glands","D":"Absorptive cells are the predominant colonic cell","E":"Stem cells sit in the lower third"}},{"id":"sdl24_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"How does the glycocalyx of colonic absorptive cells differ from that of small intestinal enterocytes?","choices":{"A":"It is absent from the cell surface","B":"It is renewed much more slowly","C":"It is made by the goblet cells","D":"It lacks carbohydrate side chains","E":"It has no proven digestive enzymes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Colonic absorptive cells secrete glycocalyx at a rapid rate, but unlike the small intestinal brush border it has not been shown to contain digestive enzymes, fitting a segment that absorbs water and salt rather than digesting nutrients. Colonic absorptive cells do have a glycocalyx, which they secrete rapidly. The colonic glycocalyx turns over rapidly, so it is not renewed more slowly. The glycocalyx is secreted by the absorptive cells themselves, while goblet cells make mucus. A glycocalyx is by definition a carbohydrate-rich surface coat, so it does not lack carbohydrate. Sources: Pawlina ch 17, large intestine absorptive cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Colonic absorptive cells reabsorb water and electrolytes using lateral Na/K-ATPase, and their glycocalyx lacks shown digestive enzymes (Pawlina ch 17). COMLEX: Nutrient digestion and absorption failures point to small bowel disease, not colonic disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Colonic absorptive cells do have a glycocalyx,","B":"The colonic glycocalyx turns over rapidly, so","C":"The glycocalyx is secreted by the absorptive","D":"A glycocalyx is by definition a carbohydrate-rich"}},{"id":"sdl24_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":1,"stem":"What part of the colonic intestinal gland forms the stem cell niche?","choices":{"A":"Upper third near the surface","B":"Lower third of the gland","C":"Middle third of the gland","D":"Entire length of the gland","E":"Surface cuff between glands"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Stem cells sit at the bottom of the intestinal gland, and the lower third of the gland is the stem cell niche. New cells then migrate up to the luminal surface. The upper gland near the surface is where cells migrate toward shedding, not the niche. The middle third holds migrating cells; the niche and amplifying zone are in the lower third. Stem cells are restricted to the gland base, not spread along its whole length. The surface cuff between glands is where senile cells are shed, not where they are generated. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: all mucosal epithelial cells of the large intestine come from stem cells at the bottom of the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the intermediate cell types in the lower third of the colonic gland are identical to those in the small intestine (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The upper gland near the surface is where cells migrate toward shedding, not the niche.","C":"The middle third holds migrating cells; the niche and amplifying zone are in the lower third.","D":"Stem cells are restricted to the gland base, not spread along its whole length.","E":"The surface cuff between glands is where senile cells are shed, not where they are generated."}},{"id":"sdl24_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":1,"stem":"From which source are colonic enteroendocrine cells renewed?","choices":{"A":"Migrating neural crest cells","B":"Pericryptal fibroblast sheath","C":"Submucosal plexus neurons","D":"Circulating marrow precursors","E":"Stem cells of the gland base"},"correct":"E","explanation":"All colonic epithelial cells, enteroendocrine cells included, arise from the single stem cell population at the base of the intestinal glands; enteroendocrine cells are not neural crest derivatives despite making amine and peptide products. Migrating neural crest cells give rise to APUD cells elsewhere, and Pawlina warns not to confuse them with gut enteroendocrine cells. The pericryptal fibroblast sheath migrates beside the epithelium and may become lamina propria macrophages. Submucosal plexus neurons are enteric nervous system cells and do not produce epithelial cells. Circulating marrow precursors supply blood and immune cells, not the epithelial lining of the glands. Sources: Pawlina ch 17, enteroendocrine cells and epithelial renewal in the large intestine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gut enteroendocrine cells are endodermal and come from crypt stem cells, not neural crest (Pawlina ch 17). COMLEX: Neuroendocrine tumors of the gut arise from these epithelial enteroendocrine cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Migrating neural crest cells give rise to","B":"The pericryptal fibroblast sheath migrates beside the","C":"Submucosal plexus neurons are enteric nervous system","D":"Circulating marrow precursors supply blood and immune"}},{"id":"sdl24_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Where on the colonic surface are senile epithelial cells shed?","choices":{"A":"Base of each intestinal gland","B":"Rim of each gland opening","C":"Crests of the semilunar folds","D":"Between two adjacent glands","E":"Tips of the colonic villi"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Senile colonic epithelial cells that reach the surface undergo apoptosis and are shed at the midpoint between two adjacent intestinal glands. The colon has no villi. The gland base is the stem cell niche, where cells are made rather than shed. Cells are shed midway between gland openings, not at the rim of each opening. Semilunar folds are contraction-dependent folds between haustra, not the defined shedding site. The colonic mucosa has a smooth surface with neither plicae circulares nor villi. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: on scanning EM, the colonic surface is divided by clefts into territories of gland openings (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: colonic gland openings on the luminal surface are arranged in an orderly pattern (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The gland base is the stem cell niche, where cells are made rather than shed.","B":"Cells are shed midway between gland openings, not at the rim of each opening.","C":"Semilunar folds are contraction-dependent folds between haustra, not the defined shedding site.","E":"The colonic mucosa has a smooth surface with neither plicae circulares nor villi."}},{"id":"sdl24_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":1,"stem":"Where does the subepithelial collagen–proteoglycan layer of the colon lie?","choices":{"A":"Between muscularis mucosae and submucosa","B":"Between the teniae and the circular muscle","C":"Under the epithelial basal lamina","D":"Within the core of each lymphatic nodule","E":"Beneath the serosal mesothelium layer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The collagen table is a thick layer of collagen and proteoglycans between the epithelial basal lamina and that of the fenestrated absorptive venous capillaries. The collagen table lies directly under the surface epithelium, not deep at the muscularis mucosae. The teniae and circular muscle are muscularis externa, far from the subepithelial collagen table. Lymphatic nodule cores are GALT, not the site of the subepithelial collagen–proteoglycan layer. The collagen table is subepithelial in the mucosa, not beneath the serosa. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the collagen table participates in regulating water and electrolyte transport from the epithelium to the vascular compartment (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the absorptive venous capillaries under the colonic epithelium are fenestrated (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The collagen table lies directly under the surface epithelium, not deep at the muscularis mucosae.","B":"The teniae and circular muscle are muscularis externa, far from the subepithelial collagen table.","D":"Lymphatic nodule cores are GALT, not the site of the subepithelial collagen–proteoglycan layer.","E":"The collagen table is subepithelial in the mucosa, not beneath the serosa."}},{"id":"sdl24_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the function of the subepithelial collagen table of the colon?","choices":{"A":"Regulating water and salt transport","B":"Anchoring the teniae coli to the mucosa","C":"Holding the lymphatic capillary network","D":"Producing the secretory component of IgA","E":"Storing mucinogen for the goblet cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The collagen table lies between the epithelial basal lamina and that of the fenestrated absorptive venous capillaries, and it helps regulate water and electrolyte transport from the epithelium to the vascular compartment. Anchoring the teniae coli is not its role, since the teniae are bands of the outer muscularis externa. The lamina propria near the surface has no lymphatic vessels, so the layer holds no lymphatic network. Secretory component comes from the polymeric Ig receptor of epithelial cells, not from this layer. Mucinogen is stored inside goblet cell granules, not in the subepithelial connective tissue. Sources: Pawlina ch 17, lamina propria of the large intestine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The colonic collagen table regulates water and electrolyte movement to the capillaries (Pawlina ch 17). COMLEX: A thickened subepithelial collagen band with watery diarrhea and normal colonoscopy suggests collagenous colitis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Anchoring the teniae coli is not its","C":"The lamina propria near the surface has","D":"Secretory component comes from the polymeric Ig","E":"Mucinogen is stored inside goblet cell granules,"}},{"id":"sdl24_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":1,"stem":"From what may macrophages in the core of the colonic lamina propria arise?","choices":{"A":"Circulating neutrophils","B":"Pericryptal fibroblasts","C":"Exhausted goblet cells","D":"Epithelial niche cells","E":"Muscularis mucosae cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pericryptal fibroblasts divide beneath the gland base and migrate upward with the epithelium. Near the surface most take on macrophage features, and lamina propria macrophages may be their terminal stage. Exhausted goblet cells are epithelial cells shed at the surface, not macrophage precursors. Epithelial niche cells generate epithelium, whereas lamina propria macrophages come from pericryptal fibroblasts. Pawlina notes that pericryptal fibroblasts migrate upward and some evidence suggests the macrophages of the colonic lamina propria core arise as their terminal differentiation, not from circulating neutrophils. Pawlina notes that pericryptal fibroblasts migrate upward and some evidence suggests the macrophages of the colonic lamina propria core arise as their terminal differentiation, not from muscularis mucosae cells. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pericryptal fibroblast sheath exists in both the large and small intestines (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: pericryptal fibroblasts divide adjacent to the epithelial stem cells at the bottom of the gland (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Exhausted goblet cells are epithelial cells shed at the surface, not macrophage precursors.","D":"Epithelial niche cells generate epithelium, whereas lamina propria macrophages come from pericryptal fibroblasts.","A":"Pawlina notes that pericryptal fibroblasts migrate upward and some evidence suggests the macrophages of the colonic lamina propria core arise as their terminal differentiation, not from circulating neutrophils.","E":"Pawlina notes that pericryptal fibroblasts migrate upward and some evidence suggests the macrophages of the colonic lamina propria core arise as their terminal differentiation, not from muscularis mucosae cells."}},{"id":"sdl24_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":1,"stem":"Where do colonic lymphatics from the gland bases first drain?","choices":{"A":"Lacteals in the lamina propria core","B":"Vessels reaching the luminal surface","C":"Plexus beneath the serosal surface","D":"Network within the muscularis mucosae","E":"Sinusoids of the regional lymph nodes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lymphatics are generally absent from the lamina propria core, but occasional small vessels at the gland bases drain into a network in the muscularis mucosae. Lymph then passes to submucosal and muscularis externa plexuses and on to regional nodes. The colon has no villi and no lacteals; lamina propria lymphatics are absent near the surface. Lymphatics do not reach the luminal surface; they are confined to gland bases. Gland-base lymphatics drain first to the muscularis mucosae, not directly to a subserosal plexus. Regional nodes come last, after the muscularis mucosae, submucosal, and muscularis externa plexuses. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels; Folder 17.6). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: no lymphatic vessels extend toward the luminal surface of the large intestine (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: lymph leaves the colonic wall only after passing through plexuses in the submucosa and muscularis externa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The colon has no villi and no lacteals; lamina propria lymphatics are absent near the surface.","B":"Lymphatics do not reach the luminal surface; they are confined to gland bases.","C":"Gland-base lymphatics drain first to the muscularis mucosae, not directly to a subserosal plexus.","E":"Regional nodes come last, after the muscularis mucosae, submucosal, and muscularis externa plexuses."}},{"id":"sdl24_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibody marker is used to identify lymphatic endothelium in colonic lamina propria?","choices":{"A":"CD117","B":"CD34","C":"D2-40","D":"CK20","E":"S-100"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The monoclonal antibody D2-40 reacts with a 40-kDa O-linked sialoglycoprotein on lymphatic endothelium and is used to map lamina propria lymphatics. CD117 (KIT) marks normal interstitial cells of Cajal and most gastrointestinal stromal tumors, not lymphatic endothelium. CD34 is a marker of hematopoietic progenitors such as myeloid blasts, not a specific lymphatic endothelial marker. S-100 is a Schwann cell calcium-binding protein marker, not a lymphatic endothelial marker. Pawlina describes D2-40 as the monoclonal antibody that reacts with a 40-kDa sialoglycoprotein on lymphatic endothelium, which CK20 does not mark. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels; Folder 17.6). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the absence of lamina propria lymphatics was first found with light and electron microscopy of biopsy samples (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: D2-40 recognizes an O-linked sialoglycoprotein of about 40 kDa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CD117 (KIT) marks normal interstitial cells of Cajal and most gastrointestinal stromal tumors, not lymphatic endothelium.","B":"CD34 is a marker of hematopoietic progenitors such as myeloid blasts, not a specific lymphatic endothelial marker.","E":"S-100 is a Schwann cell calcium-binding protein marker, not a lymphatic endothelial marker.","D":"Pawlina describes D2-40 as the monoclonal antibody that reacts with a 40-kDa sialoglycoprotein on lymphatic endothelium, which CK20 does not mark."}},{"id":"sdl24_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":1,"stem":"Many adenomatous polyps of the large intestine lie in the rectum and sigmoid colon. Which procedure can therefore detect a large share of them?","choices":{"A":"Capsule endoscopy","B":"Rectosigmoidoscopy","C":"Upper endoscopy","D":"Barium swallow study","E":"Abdominal ultrasound"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because almost half of colonic adenomas sit in the rectum and sigmoid colon, rectosigmoidoscopy can find many of them, and removal is adequate while the lesion stays in the mucosa above the lymphatics of the muscularis mucosae. Capsule endoscopy is mainly used to image the small intestine, not to sample the rectosigmoid. Upper endoscopy examines the esophagus, stomach, and duodenum, not the distal colon. A barium swallow study outlines the esophagus and cannot show rectal or sigmoid polyps. Abdominal ultrasound cannot reliably image small mucosal polyps inside the colon. Sources: Pawlina ch 17, Folder 17.6, lymph vessel distribution and diseases of the large intestine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Colonic adenomas confined to the mucosa rarely metastasize because lamina propria lymphatics are absent (Pawlina ch 17). COMLEX: Flexible sigmoidoscopy is an accepted alternative screening test, though full colonoscopy reaches the proximal colon.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Capsule endoscopy is mainly used to image","C":"Upper endoscopy examines the esophagus, stomach, and","D":"A barium swallow study outlines the esophagus","E":"Abdominal ultrasound cannot reliably image small mucosal"}},{"id":"sdl24_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man is having his first screening colonoscopy. He has never had rectal bleeding or a change in stool caliber. The examination is unremarkable. A transverse-colon biopsy is histologically normal, and an ileal biopsy taken at the same procedure shows villi. Compared with the ileal biopsy, which mucosal feature distinguishes the colonic sample?","choices":{"A":"Glands lined by simple columnar cells","B":"Lamina propria rich in lymphocytes","C":"Muscularis mucosae deep to the glands","D":"Smooth surface without villi","E":"Goblet cells among absorptive cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Normal colonic mucosa has a smooth surface without villi, unlike the villous ileal mucosa sampled at the same procedure. Both sites have glands, lymphocytes, a muscularis mucosae, and goblet cells, so those features do not separate them. Glands lined by simple columnar cells are present in both the colon and the ileum. A lymphocyte-rich lamina propria is found in both biopsies. A muscularis mucosae lies under the glands in both the colon and the ileum. Goblet cells sit among absorptive cells in both the colon and the ileum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the colon has crypts of Lieberkühn with abundant goblet cells but no villi (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: the smooth colonic mucosa shows semilunar folds formed by contractions of the muscularis externa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Glands lined by simple columnar cells are present in","B":"A lymphocyte-rich lamina propria is found in both","C":"A muscularis mucosae lies under the glands in both","E":"Goblet cells sit among absorptive cells in both the"}},{"id":"sdl24_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has had bloody diarrhea for eight years and takes mesalamine. A cousin has autoimmune thyroid disease. The abdomen is soft and nontender. Barium enema shows a featureless lead-pipe colon. p-ANCA is positive, ASCA is negative, and hemoglobin is 10.4 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL). Which normal arrangement produces the sacculations she has lost?","choices":{"A":"Plicae circulares anchored in the submucosa","B":"Teniae bundles crossing circular muscle","C":"Fat lobules pushing out the serosal surface","D":"Lymphoid nodules distorting the gland rows","E":"Uniform longitudinal muscle around the wall"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Haustra are made where teniae cross the circular muscle, and their loss produces the lead-pipe contour. Years of bloody diarrhea with p-ANCA and a negative ASCA identify the colitis in which those sacculations disappear. Plicae circulares are small-bowel folds anchored in submucosa and do not form colonic haustra. Fat lobules on the serosa are the omental appendices and do not create the haustra. Lymphoid nodules distort glands locally and do not form the regular sacculations. A uniform longitudinal muscle coat would not pinch the wall into haustra the way teniae do.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis is continuous colonic inflammation that involves the rectum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: ulcerative colitis causes friable mucosa with superficial or deep ulcerations and crypt abscesses (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Plicae circulares are small-bowel folds anchored in submucosa and","C":"Fat lobules on the serosa are the omental appendices","D":"Lymphoid nodules distort glands locally and do not form","E":"A uniform longitudinal muscle coat would not pinch the"}},{"id":"sdl24_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":5,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has a screening flexible sigmoidoscopy. He has no bleeding, pain, or history of colitis. In the upper rectum the endoscopist sees several shelf-like mucosal folds. Which structures are these?","choices":{"A":"Colonic semilunar folds","B":"Longitudinal anal columns","C":"Transverse rectal folds","D":"Plicae circulares folds","E":"Gastric rugae-type folds"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The shelf-like folds in the upper rectum are the transverse rectal folds. They are a normal feature of that segment and are not the semilunar folds of the colon or the columns of the anal canal. Semilunar folds are the haustral shelves of the colon, not these rectal shelves. Longitudinal anal columns lie in the anal canal, distal to the upper rectum. Plicae circulares are circular folds of the small intestine. Rugae are gastric folds and are not seen in the rectum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pectinate line forms where hindgut endoderm meets ectoderm (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: the depressions between the anal columns are called anal sinuses (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and the anal sphincters","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"A":"Semilunar folds are the haustral shelves of the colon,","B":"Longitudinal anal columns lie in the anal canal, distal","D":"Plicae circulares are circular folds of the small","E":"Rugae are gastric folds and are not seen in the"}},{"id":"sdl24_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman has a colonic biopsy during screening colonoscopy. She does not use laxatives and has no diarrhea. At the luminal surface many tall, thin goblet cells hold only a few mucinogen granules in the central apical cytoplasm. What explains their appearance?","choices":{"A":"Secretion outpacing mucin synthesis","B":"Mucin loss during tissue processing","C":"Immature cells still in the niche","D":"Apoptosis of aging goblet cells","E":"Conversion into absorptive cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Surface goblet cells look depleted because secretion of mucus has outrun new mucin synthesis. Their tall thin shape and few remaining central granules fit cells that have recently discharged. Mucin loss during processing empties the whole cup, which is not the picture of a few retained central granules. Cells still in the stem niche sit deep in the gland, not on the luminal surface. Apoptosis of aging goblet cells sheds them rather than leaving a few apical granules. These cells have not converted into absorptive cells, which lack mucinogen granules.","boardPrep":"Step 1: caveolated tuft cells described in the colonic epithelium may be a form of exhausted goblet cell (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: colonic goblet cells may mature deep in the gland, even within the replicative zone (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 3 — small-intestine epithelial cell types","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mucin loss during processing empties the whole cup, which","C":"Cells still in the stem niche sit deep in","D":"Apoptosis of aging goblet cells sheds them rather than","E":"These cells have not converted into absorptive cells, which"}},{"id":"sdl24_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man has biopsies from several sites at colonoscopy. He has no bleeding or chronic diarrhea. One site shows absorptive cells and goblet cells in a ratio close to 1 to 1. Where was that biopsy most likely taken?","choices":{"A":"Proximal cecum","B":"Lower rectum","C":"Ascending colon","D":"Transverse colon","E":"Descending colon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A near-equal mix of absorptive cells and goblet cells is reached in the lower rectum, where goblet cells increase. Elsewhere absorptive cells outnumber goblet cells about four to one. The proximal cecum keeps the usual excess of absorptive cells, about four for each goblet cell. The ascending colon keeps that absorptive majority rather than an even mix. The transverse colon has the usual excess of absorptive cells. The descending colon still has more absorptive cells than goblet cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rectal mucosa resembles distal colonic mucosa, with straight tubular glands rich in goblet cells (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the rectum is the dilated distal portion of the alimentary canal (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and the anal sphincters","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The proximal cecum keeps the usual excess of absorptive","C":"The ascending colon keeps that absorptive majority rather than","D":"The transverse colon has the usual excess of absorptive","E":"The descending colon still has more absorptive cells than goblet"}},{"id":"sdl24_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman has passed hard stools only twice a week for years. The abdomen is soft, and stool is palpable in the left colon. A biopsy shows abundant goblet cells packing the colonic glands. What is the main role of their continuous secretion?","choices":{"A":"Neutralizing gastric acid in chyme","B":"Digesting the residual disaccharides","C":"Killing bacteria with lysozyme","D":"Absorbing short-chain fatty acids","E":"Easing the passage of firm contents"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Colonic goblet cells continuously secrete mucus that lubricates the mucosa and eases passage of firm contents. Constipation with a goblet-cell-rich biopsy asks for that lubricating function. Neutralizing gastric acid is a duodenal task and is not the role of colonic goblet mucus. Digesting residual disaccharides is a brush-border enzyme function, not a mucus function. Killing bacteria with lysozyme is a Paneth-cell role, and the colon lacks those cells. Absorbing short-chain fatty acids is the work of colonocytes, not of goblet mucus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diverticulosis is associated with low-fiber diets and focal weakness in the colonic wall (First Aid Gastrointestinal, diverticulosis). COMLEX: elimination of semisolid to solid waste is aided by the large amount of mucus in the colonic glands (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Neutralizing gastric acid is a duodenal task and is","B":"Digesting residual disaccharides is a brush-border enzyme function, not","C":"Killing bacteria with lysozyme is a Paneth-cell role, and","D":"Absorbing short-chain fatty acids is the work of colonocytes,"}},{"id":"sdl24_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old healthy man volunteers for a labeling study of colonic epithelium. He has one formed stool each day. Newly generated crypt cells are tagged as they leave the stem-cell zone and are followed toward the lumen. The labeled cells later appear in shed surface epithelium. About how long after they begin migrating are these cells shed?","choices":{"A":"About 5 hours","B":"About 5 weeks","C":"About 5 days","D":"About 5 months","E":"About 1 year"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Colonic epithelial cells migrate from the crypt and are shed at the surface after about 5 days. The labeling study times that interval directly. Five hours is far shorter than the colonic epithelial lifespan. Five weeks would leave cells in place long after they have been shed. Five months greatly exceeds the turnover of this epithelium. A year is not the survival time of a colonocyte.","boardPrep":"Step 1: as new colonic cells begin their upward migration, they continue dividing (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: in the stomach, surface and pit mucous cells turn over in 3–5 days (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Five hours is far shorter than the colonic epithelial","B":"Five weeks would leave cells in place long after","D":"Five months greatly exceeds the turnover of this","E":"A year is not the survival time of a"}},{"id":"sdl24_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":5,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman has a colonic biopsy examined by electron microscopy during a research protocol. She has no diarrhea or family history of polyposis. Deep in a gland, two cells that still hold apical mucinogen granules are in mitosis. What does this show?","choices":{"A":"Mature goblet cells can still divide","B":"Mucinogen granules form during mitosis","C":"Exhausted surface cells reenter the cycle","D":"Paneth cells give rise to new goblets","E":"Absorptive cells convert into goblets"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Goblet cells that still contain mucinogen granules can divide, so differentiation and proliferation overlap in the gland. Mitoses in deep cells that already hold granules demonstrate that overlap. The granules are not formed by the mitotic apparatus itself. Exhausted surface cells are shed and do not reenter the cell cycle deep in the gland. Paneth cells are absent from the human colon and do not give rise to these goblet cells. Absorptive cells do not convert into goblet cells during these mitoses.","boardPrep":"Step 1: dividing intestinal cells typically move away from the basal lamina toward the gland lumen (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: chromosomes of dividing goblet cells lack a surrounding nuclear membrane on EM (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"B":"The granules are not formed by the mitotic apparatus","C":"Exhausted surface cells are shed and do not reenter","D":"Paneth cells are absent from the human colon and","E":"Absorptive cells do not convert into goblet cells during these"}},{"id":"sdl24_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":5,"stem":"A 24-year-old man is found to have hundreds of colonic adenomas at colonoscopy. His father had a colectomy at age 35 for the same problem. The abdomen is soft and there is no mass. Which initial epithelial event leads to these small polyps?","choices":{"A":"Mutation of the KRAS proto-oncogene","B":"Deletion of the TP53 suppressor gene","C":"Methylation of the MLH1 repair gene","D":"Loss of the APC suppressor gene","E":"Gain of the RET tyrosine kinase gene"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The first epithelial event in these familial adenomas is loss of the APC suppressor gene. Hundreds of polyps at a young age, with an affected father, match that earliest step rather than a later mutation. KRAS mutation occurs later in the adenoma sequence and does not initiate the small polyp. TP53 deletion is a late event associated with carcinoma, not the first step. MLH1 methylation is a mismatch-repair pathway and is not the start of this polyposis. Gain of RET is related to enteric neurons, not to the start of an adenoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: familial adenomatous polyposis is an autosomal dominant APC mutation on chromosome 5q21-q22 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, familial adenomatous polyposis). COMLEX: about 98% of colorectal cancers are adenocarcinomas arising from glandular epithelium (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"A":"KRAS mutation occurs later in the adenoma sequence and","B":"TP53 deletion is a late event associated with carcinoma,","C":"MLH1 methylation is a mismatch-repair pathway and is not","E":"Gain of RET is related to enteric neurons, not"}},{"id":"sdl24_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has a small hyperplastic polyp removed at colonoscopy. He has no chronic diarrhea or weight loss. Under the surface epithelium a layer of fibrillar protein and proteoglycans measures about 15 µm, several times the usual few micrometers. Which structure is thickened?","choices":{"A":"Basal lamina","B":"Muscularis mucosae","C":"Pericryptal sheath","D":"Submucosa","E":"Collagen table"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The fibrillar layer immediately under the surface epithelium is the collagen table, and 15 µm is thicker than its usual few micrometers. The polyp finding asks which named layer has that thickness. The basal lamina is a thin sheet and is not this 15-µm fibrillar layer. The muscularis mucosae is a muscle band deep to the glands, not a subepithelial fibrillar layer. The pericryptal sheath surrounds glands and is not the subepithelial table. The submucosa lies below the muscularis mucosae and is far thicker than 15 µm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mucosal polyps are usually small, look like normal mucosa, and are clinically insignificant (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic polyps). COMLEX: submucosal polyps may include lipomas, leiomyomas, and fibromas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic polyps).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The basal lamina is a thin sheet and is","B":"The muscularis mucosae is a muscle band deep to","C":"The pericryptal sheath surrounds glands and is not the subepithelial","D":"The submucosa lies below the muscularis mucosae and is"}},{"id":"sdl24_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman has mild cramping and a colonic biopsy. She is afebrile and the abdomen is soft. Large lymphatic nodules distort the spacing of the glands and extend into the submucosa. What best explains this extensive lymphoid development?","choices":{"A":"Absorption of dietary antigens","B":"Thin goblet-cell mucus layer","C":"Absence of epithelial renewal","D":"Abundant luminal microbes","E":"Drainage of lacteal lymph flow"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Colonic lymphoid tissue is extensive because the lumen holds abundant microbes. Nodules that reach the submucosa are the expected response to that microbial load. Absorption of dietary antigens is a small-bowel function and does not drive these colonic nodules. A thin mucus layer is not the reason the colon develops large lymphoid nodules. The epithelium renews continuously, so absence of renewal is not the explanation. Lacteal lymph flow belongs to small-bowel villi, which the colon does not have.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease microscopically shows noncaseating granulomas and lymphoid aggregates (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: large colonic lymphatic nodules extend from the lamina propria into the submucosa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Absorption of dietary antigens is a small-bowel function and","B":"A thin mucus layer is not the reason the","C":"The epithelium renews continuously, so absence of renewal is","E":"Lacteal lymph flow belongs to small-bowel villi, which the"}},{"id":"sdl24_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":5,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy has fever and right lower quadrant pain. He has never had abdominal surgery, and there is no family history of cystic fibrosis. Temperature is 38.3°C and the right lower quadrant is tender. The leukocyte count is 16,000/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL). No fecalith is found, and the appendix shows markedly enlarged lymphatic nodules with germinal centers. What is the most likely cause of the obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Fibrous scar tissue","B":"Lymphoid hyperplasia","C":"Carcinoid tumor mass","D":"Inspissated mucus plug","E":"Spasm of the teniae"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Without a fecalith, the lumen is closed by lymphoid hyperplasia of the enlarged nodules. Fever, leukocytosis, and germinal centers in the appendix wall support that swelling as the obstruction. Fibrous scar is not the acute obstructing lesion in a first attack without prior surgery. A carcinoid mass is not the typical obstructing lesion of acute appendicitis in a child. An inspissated mucus plug is the cystic-fibrosis pattern, and there is no such history. Spasm of the teniae is not how an appendix without a fecalith becomes obstructed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: appendiceal obstruction causes early diffuse periumbilical pain through T8-T10 visceral afferents (First Aid Gastrointestinal, appendicitis). COMLEX: the appendix is a common site of carcinoid, a tumor of enteroendocrine cells (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"A":"Fibrous scar is not the acute obstructing lesion in","C":"A carcinoid mass is not the typical obstructing lesion","D":"An inspissated mucus plug is the cystic-fibrosis pattern, and","E":"Spasm of the teniae is not how an appendix"}},{"id":"sdl24_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has a pedunculated sigmoid polyp removed at his first screening colonoscopy. The abdomen is soft. The polyp contains carcinoma confined to the mucosa above the muscularis mucosae. Why is endoscopic removal considered adequate?","choices":{"A":"Polyp stalks contain no blood vessels","B":"Basal lamina halts carcinoma cell spread","C":"Serosa blocks spread to the regional nodes","D":"Mucosal cancer cells stop dividing early","E":"No lymphatics in the lamina propria"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lymphatics are absent from the colonic lamina propria, so a carcinoma that has not crossed the muscularis mucosae cannot yet reach nodes. Confinement above that line is why polypectomy is enough. Polyp stalks do contain blood vessels, so absence of vessels is not the reason. The basal lamina is not the barrier that keeps this carcinoma from nodal spread. The serosa is far deep to a mucosal carcinoma and is not what makes polypectomy adequate. Mucosal cancer cells do not stop dividing early as the reason for cure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: villous adenomas have more malignant potential than tubular ones ('villous histology is villainous') (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic polyps). COMLEX: the polyp stalk is continuous with the colonic submucosa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Polyp stalks do contain blood vessels, so absence of","B":"The basal lamina is not the barrier that keeps","C":"The serosa is far deep to a mucosal carcinoma","D":"Mucosal cancer cells do not stop dividing early as"}},{"id":"sdl24_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has bloody diarrhea. She has not had radiation. The abdomen is mildly tender. p-ANCA is positive, ASCA is negative, and hemoglobin is 10.2 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL). A colonic biopsy shows many lymphatic vessels in the lamina propria between the glands. What does this finding indicate?","choices":{"A":"Active inflammation","B":"Healed remission phase","C":"Normal colonic mucosa","D":"Early adenoma growth","E":"Ischemic mucosal injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Numerous lymphatics between the glands indicate active inflammation, which induces vessels the normal lamina propria lacks. Bloody diarrhea with p-ANCA and a negative ASCA is the colitis in which this appears. Healed mucosa does not show this new lymphatic network in the lamina propria. Normal colonic mucosa has essentially no lymphatics in the lamina propria. Early adenoma growth does not fill the lamina propria with new lymphatics. Ischemic injury thins the mucosa and does not produce this lymphatic proliferation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis complications include toxic megacolon and colorectal cancer, with risk rising in pancolitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: treatment progress in ulcerative colitis can be monitored by biopsies showing lymphatics disappearing (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Healed mucosa does not show this new lymphatic network","C":"Normal colonic mucosa has essentially no lymphatics in the lamina","D":"Early adenoma growth does not fill the lamina propria","E":"Ischemic injury thins the mucosa and does not produce"}},{"id":"sdl24_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man has bloody diarrhea, and biopsies show new lymphatic vessels forming in the colonic lamina propria. He has not had radiation. The abdomen is mildly tender. p-ANCA is positive and ASCA is negative. Expression of which factor is linked to this lymphangiogenesis?","choices":{"A":"Transforming growth factor alpha","B":"Hepatocyte growth factor and c-Met","C":"Vascular endothelial growth factor","D":"Fibroblast growth factor 10 signaling","E":"Insulin-like growth factor 1 axis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The new lamina-propria lymphatics of active colitis are linked to vascular endothelial growth factor. Bloody diarrhea with p-ANCA and a negative ASCA is the setting in which that factor drives the vessels. Transforming growth factor alpha is not the factor tied to these new lymphatics. Hepatocyte growth factor and c-Met are not the drivers named for this lymphangiogenesis. Fibroblast growth factor 10 is a developmental signal and is not the factor linked here. Insulin-like growth factor 1 is not the factor linked to these new mucosal lymphatics.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis is associated with primary sclerosing cholangitis and MPO-ANCA (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: in ulcerative colitis, granulation tissue forms with proliferating blood and lymphatic vessels (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Transforming growth factor alpha is not the factor tied","B":"Hepatocyte growth factor and c-Met are not the drivers","D":"Fibroblast growth factor 10 is a developmental signal and","E":"Insulin-like growth factor 1 is not the factor linked"}},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has mild left lower quadrant discomfort. He has eaten a low-fiber diet for decades and is not taking antibiotics. The abdomen is soft, without peritonitis. CT shows many small outpouchings of the sigmoid wall without inflammation. In a resection specimen each outpouching is mucosa and submucosa only partly covered by muscle. Where in the wall do these lesions form?","choices":{"A":"Directly beneath a tenia coli band","B":"At gaps in the muscularis mucosae","C":"At vessel entry gaps in circular muscle","D":"Through every layer of the bowel wall","E":"Where lymphoid nodules enlarge the wall"},"correct":"C","explanation":"These are false diverticula that herniate where vessels pierce gaps in the circular muscle. A low-fiber diet, sigmoid outpouchings, and a wall of mucosa plus submucosa without a full muscle coat mark that site. They do not form directly beneath a tenia, which is where the muscle is reinforced. Gaps in the muscularis mucosae are not the penetration site of these outpouchings. A through-and-through outpouching of every layer would be a true diverticulum, which this is not. Lymphoid nodules are not the point where these vascular gaps let mucosa herniate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Colonic diverticula are pseudodiverticula of mucosa and submucosa at sites where vasa recta penetrate the circular muscle between the teniae (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Diverticulosis is common in older adults on low-fiber diets and can bleed or become inflamed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"They do not form directly beneath a tenia, which","B":"Gaps in the muscularis mucosae are not the penetration","D":"A through-and-through outpouching of every layer would be a","E":"Lymphoid nodules are not the point where these vascular"}},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old man had his colon removed and an end ileostomy after years of bloody diarrhea. p-ANCA had been positive and ASCA negative. Months later the stoma puts out a large volume of loose fluid, and on hot days he becomes lightheaded. Serum sodium is 136 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Loss of which colonic epithelial function best explains the larger fluid losses?","choices":{"A":"Goblet cells secreting lubricating mucus","B":"Absorptive cells reclaiming water and salt","C":"Enteroendocrine cells releasing hormones","D":"Paneth cells releasing antibacterial enzymes","E":"M cells moving antigens to lymphoid tissue"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Colonocytes reclaim water and salt, so their removal leaves a high-volume ileostomy effluent and volume loss in the heat. The prior bloody diarrhea with p-ANCA was the reason the colon was removed. Goblet-cell mucus lubricates stool and does not account for the liters of fluid now lost. Enteroendocrine hormone release does not reclaim the water the stoma is now putting out. Paneth cells are not a normal colonic population and do not reclaim salt and water. M cells move antigen and do not absorb the fluid lost after colectomy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Colonic columnar absorptive cells reabsorb water and electrolytes, while goblet cells secrete lubricating mucus (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: Patients with an ileostomy are prone to dehydration and need higher fluid and salt intake.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Goblet-cell mucus lubricates stool and does not account for","C":"Enteroendocrine hormone release does not reclaim the water the","D":"Paneth cells are not a normal colonic population and","E":"M cells move antigen and do not absorb the"}},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":3,"stem":"A 62-year-old man has a 4 mm rectosigmoid polyp removed at screening colonoscopy, which is otherwise normal. He has had no bleeding or change in bowel habit. Crypts and surface epithelium have a saw-tooth outline of crowded goblet and absorptive cells. The nuclei are normal and there is no dysplasia. Which alteration in epithelial renewal most likely produced this architecture?","choices":{"A":"Stem cells moved to the gland surface","B":"Loss of the APC suppressor gene","C":"Faster migration of cells off the surface","D":"Arrest of division in the crypt base","E":"Delayed shedding of surface cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A hyperplastic polyp with a saw-tooth surface and no dysplasia comes from delayed shedding of surface cells. Normal nuclei exclude the dysplastic sequence that begins with loss of a suppressor gene. Stem cells have not moved to the surface; the crowded cells are the ones that failed to shed. Loss of APC starts an adenoma, and this polyp has no dysplasia. Faster migration off the surface would thin the epithelium rather than crowd it. Division in the crypt base is not arrested in this lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hyperplastic polyps arise from decreased turnover and delayed shedding of surface cells and have no malignant potential (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: They must be distinguished from sessile serrated lesions, which look similar but can progress.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Stem cells have not moved to the surface; the","B":"Loss of APC starts an adenoma, and this polyp","C":"Faster migration off the surface would thin the epithelium","D":"Division in the crypt base is not arrested in this"}},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman has four months of watery diarrhea without blood or weight loss. She does not use laxatives. The abdomen is soft and colonoscopy looks normal. Biopsies show a collagen band 25 µm thick, directly under the surface basal lamina, where this layer is normally only about 5 µm, plus increased intraepithelial lymphocytes. Which normal function of this layer is most likely disturbed?","choices":{"A":"Anchoring the teniae coli muscle","B":"Supplying macrophages to the core","C":"Moving absorbed fluid to capillaries","D":"Housing the crypt stem cell niche","E":"Draining lymph away from the gland bases"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The thickened subepithelial collagen table normally moves absorbed fluid toward capillaries, and a 25-µm band blocks that path. Chronic watery diarrhea with a normal-looking colonoscopy fits that disturbed function. Anchoring the teniae is not the job of this thin subepithelial layer. Supplying macrophages is not the role of the collagen table. The crypt stem-cell niche sits at the gland base, not in this subepithelial band. Lymph leaves from deeper vessels, and this layer is not the lymphatic drain of the gland base.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Collagenous colitis shows a subepithelial collagen layer thicker than 10 µm with intraepithelial lymphocytes and causes chronic watery diarrhea with a normal endoscopy (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Older women with chronic watery diarrhea and normal colonoscopy need random biopsies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Anchoring the teniae is not the job of this","B":"Supplying macrophages is not the role of the collagen","D":"The crypt stem-cell niche sits at the gland base,","E":"Lymph leaves from deeper vessels, and this layer is"}},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 69-year-old man has lost 4 kg and has a sigmoid mass that has grown through the muscularis mucosae into the submucosa and muscularis. He has no history of inflammatory bowel disease. The abdomen is soft. Hemoglobin is 10.0 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and carcinoembryonic antigen is 16 ng/mL (reference range: <3.0 ng/mL). The surgeon removes the regional lymph nodes with the specimen. Which named node group receives lymph from this segment?","choices":{"A":"Superior mesenteric nodes","B":"Superficial inguinal nodes","C":"Internal iliac nodes","D":"Inferior mesenteric nodes","E":"Para-aortic nodes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The sigmoid colon drains to inferior mesenteric nodes. A carcinoma that has entered the submucosa, with an elevated carcinoembryonic antigen, is the lesion whose regional nodes are that group. Superior mesenteric nodes drain midgut colon, not the sigmoid. Superficial inguinal nodes drain the anal canal below the pectinate line, not the sigmoid. Internal iliac nodes are not the primary group for the sigmoid colon. Para-aortic nodes are a later station, not the named regional group removed with this segment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The colon from the splenic flexure to the upper rectum drains to inferior mesenteric nodes, and the lower rectum to internal iliac nodes (First Aid 2025 Anatomy). COMLEX: Colon cancer staging depends on whether the tumor has reached the lymphatic-bearing layers and regional nodes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Superior mesenteric nodes drain midgut colon, not the","B":"Superficial inguinal nodes drain the anal canal below the","C":"Internal iliac nodes are not the primary group for","E":"Para-aortic nodes are a later station, not the named"}},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has a 3 cm sessile rectosigmoid mass. She has no history of colitis or polyposis. Biopsy shows long, slender, finger-like projections lined by dysplastic columnar epithelium, a surface pattern never seen in normal colonic mucosa. Compared with a polyp built of dysplastic tubular glands, which feature is this lesion most likely to have?","choices":{"A":"No malignant potential","B":"Origin from submucosal fat","C":"Hamartomatous growth of normal tissue","D":"Greater malignant potential","E":"Regenerating mucosa after colitis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Finger-like dysplastic projections are a villous adenoma, which has greater malignant potential than a tubular adenoma. The sessile rectosigmoid mass with that surface pattern is the higher-risk lesion. This dysplastic villous architecture is not free of malignant potential. The projections are mucosal epithelium, not growth from submucosal fat. Hamartomatous polyps are disorganized normal tissue and are not lined by dysplastic epithelium. Regenerating mucosa after colitis is not a mass of dysplastic finger-like projections.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Normal colonic mucosa has no villi, and villous adenomas have the greatest malignant potential of the adenomatous polyps (Bootcamp Gastroenterology). COMLEX: Large sessile villous lesions need complete removal and close surveillance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"This dysplastic villous architecture is not free of malignant","B":"The projections are mucosal epithelium, not growth from submucosal","C":"Hamartomatous polyps are disorganized normal tissue and are not","E":"Regenerating mucosa after colitis is not a mass of"}},{"id":"sdl24_q31","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man is having a screening colonoscopy. He has never had bleeding or a change in stool caliber. The examination is easy, the mucosa is pale pink, and there is no mass. Hemoglobin is 14.8 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). Between the outward sacculations of the colon, the endoscopist sees crescent-shaped internal folds. Which structures are these?","choices":{"A":"Plicae circulares","B":"Semilunar folds","C":"Rugae of the stomach","D":"Spiral valves of the cystic duct","E":"Circular folds of the jejunum only"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Semilunar folds are the internal shelves that form between haustra when the muscularis externa contracts. Plicae circulares are permanent submucosal folds of the small intestine, which this colon does not have. Gastric rugae are folds of the stomach, not shelves between colonic haustra. Spiral valves are mucosal folds of the cystic duct. Circular folds of the jejunum are plicae circulares, absent from colonic mucosa.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Plicae circulares are permanent submucosal folds of the small intestine, which this colon does not have","C":"Gastric rugae are folds of the stomach, not shelves between colonic haustra","D":"Spiral valves are mucosal folds of the cystic duct","E":"Circular folds of the jejunum are plicae circulares, absent from colonic mucosa"}},{"id":"sdl24_q32","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old healthy man volunteers for a colonic epithelial labeling study. He has one formed stool a day and takes no laxatives. The abdomen is soft. Hemoglobin is 15.1 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). Three weeks later a few labeled cells are still on the surface, long after the absorptive cells have been shed. Which cell type accounts for those persistent labels?","choices":{"A":"Enteroendocrine cells","B":"Surface absorptive cells that normally live for months","C":"Mature goblet cells that never leave the surface","D":"Parietal cells that migrated from the stomach","E":"Hepatocytes shed into the crypt"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Colonic enteroendocrine cells migrate more slowly than absorptive and goblet cells and can live up to four weeks. Surface absorptive cells turn over in about six days, so a three-week label is not an ordinary colonocyte. Goblet cells are shed on the same short timetable as absorptive cells, not after three weeks. Parietal cells are gastric and do not colonize colonic crypts. Hepatocytes are liver cells and are not the long-lived labeled cells of colonic epithelium.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Surface absorptive cells turn over in about six days, so a three-week label is not an ordinary colonocyte","C":"Goblet cells are shed on the same short timetable as absorptive cells, not after three weeks","D":"Parietal cells are gastric and do not colonize colonic crypts","E":"Hepatocytes are liver cells and are not the long-lived labeled cells of colonic epithelium"}},{"id":"sdl24_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman joins a study of colonic cell division. She has regular bowel habits and no family history of polyposis. The abdomen is soft and colonoscopy is normal. Hemoglobin is 13.2 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL). Newly generated crypt cells are seen dividing two or three more times as they move up the gland. What does that finding show?","choices":{"A":"Surface cells re-enter the cell cycle after they are shed","B":"Paneth cells are the only dividing cells in the colon","C":"Stem cells are confined to the luminal surface","D":"The amplifying compartment keeps dividing during upward migration","E":"Enteroendocrine cells divide every few hours for months"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Intermediate cells in the lower third of the colonic gland undergo two or three further divisions as they migrate upward. Cells that reach the surface undergo apoptosis and are shed, and they are not the population still dividing in the gland. Paneth cells are normally absent from human colonic epithelium. The stem-cell niche is at the base of the gland, not on the luminal surface. Enteroendocrine cells migrate slowly and are not the cells adding two or three divisions on the way up the gland.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cells that reach the surface undergo apoptosis and are shed, and they are not the population still dividing in the gland","B":"Paneth cells are normally absent from human colonic epithelium","C":"The stem-cell niche is at the base of the gland, not on the luminal surface","E":"Enteroendocrine cells migrate slowly and are not the cells adding two or three divisions on the way up the gland"}},{"id":"sdl24_q34","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman has mild cramping, and a colonic biopsy is taken. She is afebrile and has no weight loss. The abdomen is soft. Hemoglobin is 13.6 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL) and the leukocyte count is 7,200/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL). The biopsy shows large lymphoid nodules that push glands apart and cross the muscularis mucosae. How far do these nodules normally extend?","choices":{"A":"They stay inside a single surface enterocyte","B":"They are limited to the serosa and never reach mucosa","C":"They replace the teniae coli","D":"They fill the lumen of the inferior mesenteric artery","E":"Through the muscularis mucosae into the submucosa"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Large colonic lymphoid nodules distort gland spacing and extend through the muscularis mucosae into the submucosa. These nodules are lymphoid tissue in the wall, not inclusions inside one enterocyte. The nodules begin in the mucosa and lamina propria, not as a serosal-only collection. Teniae coli are bands of the outer longitudinal muscle and are not lymphoid nodules. The nodules are intramural lymphoid tissue and do not occupy the inferior mesenteric artery.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"These nodules are lymphoid tissue in the wall, not inclusions inside one enterocyte","B":"The nodules begin in the mucosa and lamina propria, not as a serosal-only collection","C":"Teniae coli are bands of the outer longitudinal muscle and are not lymphoid nodules","D":"The nodules are intramural lymphoid tissue and do not occupy the inferior mesenteric artery"}}],"25":[{"id":"sdl25_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"Slow waves in intestinal smooth muscle originate in which cells?","choices":{"A":"Interstitial cells of Cajal","B":"Myenteric ganglion neurons","C":"Submucosal plexus neurons","D":"Enterochromaffin-like cells","E":"Longitudinal smooth muscle cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Costanzo describes the interstitial cells of Cajal, abundant in the myenteric plexus, as the pacemaker of gastrointestinal smooth muscle; their spontaneous depolarizations spread to smooth muscle through gap junctions. Myenteric and submucosal neurons modulate action potentials and force but do not set slow wave rhythm. Smooth muscle cells follow the pacemaker rather than generate it. Enterochromaffin-like cells are gastric mucosal cells that release histamine to stimulate parietal cell acid secretion. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Slow waves, origin of slow waves); Guyton Ch. 63 (slow waves). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: interstitial cells of Cajal act like the sinoatrial node of the gut and couple to smooth muscle by gap junctions (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: unitary smooth muscle lets action potentials spread rapidly from cell to cell (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic effects on GI function","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Myenteric neurons modulate action potentials and force; they do not set slow-wave rhythm.","E":"Smooth muscle cells follow the pacemaker rather than generate it.","C":"Submucosal plexus neurons do not set slow-wave rhythm.","D":"Enterochromaffin-like cells release histamine to stimulate parietal cell acid secretion."}},{"id":"sdl25_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the intrinsic slow wave frequency of the duodenum?","choices":{"A":"3 per minute","B":"6 per minute","C":"9 per minute","D":"12 per minute","E":"20 per minute"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Costanzo gives slow wave frequencies from 3 to 12 per minute along the tract, with the duodenum highest at 12 per minute. Three per minute is the stomach, the lowest rate. Nine per minute is the ileum. Six per minute is not a characteristic value given, and 20 per minute exceeds the stated range. Three slow waves per minute is the stomach rate, the lowest in the GI tract. Nine slow waves per minute is the ileal rate. Six slow waves per minute is not a characteristic gut rate. Twenty per minute exceeds the 3 to 12 per minute range of gastrointestinal slow waves, whose maximum is the duodenal rate of 12. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Frequency of slow waves; Small intestinal motility); Guyton Ch. 64 (segmentation maximum near 12 per minute in duodenum, 8 to 9 in terminal ileum). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: slow wave frequency sets the maximum contraction frequency, so segmentation in the proximal small intestine reaches at most about 12 per minute (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: slow wave frequency falls from duodenum to terminal ileum (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic effects on GI function","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Three slow waves per minute is the stomach rate, the lowest in the GI tract.","C":"Nine slow waves per minute is the ileal rate.","B":"Six slow waves per minute is not a characteristic gut rate.","E":"Twenty per minute exceeds the 3 to 12 per minute range of gastrointestinal slow waves, whose maximum is the duodenal rate of 12."}},{"id":"sdl25_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"How does parasympathetic stimulation change the electrical activity of intestinal smooth muscle?","choices":{"A":"Higher slow wave frequency","B":"More spikes per slow wave","C":"Larger slow wave amplitude","D":"Shorter slow wave plateau","E":"Longer repolarization phase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Neural and hormonal inputs do not alter the intrinsic slow wave frequency; parasympathetic stimulation increases the number of action potentials on the slow wave plateau and so strengthens contraction. A higher slow wave frequency is the classic trap because that rhythm is set by the pacemaker. Slow wave amplitude, plateau, and repolarization are set by pacemaker calcium and potassium currents and are not described as vagal targets; sympathetic input instead decreases spikes. Neural input does not change the intrinsic slow wave frequency; parasympathetic stimulation adds action potentials on top of each slow wave. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Frequency of slow waves; gastric and small intestinal motility). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the more action potentials ride on a slow wave, the larger the phasic contraction (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: acetylcholine depolarizes gut smooth muscle and norepinephrine hyperpolarizes it (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic effects on GI function","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Neural input does not change the intrinsic slow wave frequency."}},{"id":"sdl25_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the main role of segmentation contractions in the small intestine?","choices":{"A":"Propelling chyme toward the cecum","B":"Clearing residue during fasting","C":"Emptying stomach into duodenum","D":"Moving feces into the rectum","E":"Mixing chyme with secretions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Segmentation splits a bolus orad and caudad and then lets it recombine, mixing chyme with pancreatic enzymes and exposing it to the mucosa with no forward propulsion. Propulsion toward the cecum is peristalsis. Fasting clearance is the migrating complex. Gastric emptying and rectal filling by mass movements are other patterns. Propulsion toward the large intestine is the job of peristaltic contractions; segmentation produces no forward movement. Clearing residual chyme during fasting is done by the migrating myoelectric complexes every 90 minutes. Moving feces into the rectum is done by colonic mass movements, not small intestinal segmentation. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Segmentation contractions, Fig. 8.12). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: segmentation mixes and peristalsis propels, both coordinated by the enteric nervous system (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: segmentation movements may still travel about 1 cm anally, so the two patterns overlap (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Propulsion toward the large intestine is the job of peristaltic contractions; segmentation produces no forward movement.","B":"Clearing residual chyme during fasting is done by the migrating myoelectric complexes every 90 minutes.","D":"Moving feces into the rectum is done by colonic mass movements, not small intestinal segmentation."}},{"id":"sdl25_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":1,"stem":"During small intestinal peristalsis, what happens to the segment orad to the bolus?","choices":{"A":"It narrows and lengthens","B":"It widens and shortens","C":"It narrows and shortens","D":"It widens and lengthens","E":"It keeps a fixed diameter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Behind the bolus, circular muscle contracts while longitudinal muscle relaxes, so the segment narrows and lengthens. Widening and shortening is the caudad segment, where circular muscle relaxes and longitudinal muscle contracts. Narrowing with shortening or widening with lengthening would need both layers to contract or relax together, which reciprocal innervation prevents. A fixed diameter does not propel the bolus. Widening and shortening happens in front of (caudad to) the bolus, where circular muscle relaxes and longitudinal muscle contracts. Behind the bolus, longitudinal muscle relaxes while circular muscle contracts, so the segment lengthens rather than shortens. Behind the bolus, circular muscle contracts, so the segment narrows rather than widens. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Peristaltic contractions, Fig. 8.12). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: excitatory transmitters such as ACh and substance P act behind the bolus, inhibitory VIP and NO ahead of it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: contraction of circular muscle narrows a segment and longitudinal contraction shortens it (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Widening and shortening happens in front of (caudad to) the bolus, where circular muscle relaxes and longitudinal muscle contracts.","C":"Behind the bolus, longitudinal muscle relaxes while circular muscle contracts, so the segment lengthens rather than shortens.","D":"Behind the bolus, circular muscle contracts, so the segment narrows rather than widens."}},{"id":"sdl25_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":1,"stem":"Which mucosal mediator activates intrinsic primary afferent neurons to start the peristaltic reflex?","choices":{"A":"Somatostatin","B":"Serotonin","C":"Histamine","D":"Glucagon","E":"Secretin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo states that the bolus is sensed by mucosal enterochromaffin (EC) cells, which release serotonin onto intrinsic primary afferent neurons to start the peristaltic reflex. Somatostatin is an inhibitory paracrine. Histamine is a gastric acid secretagogue. Glucagon and secretin inhibit small intestinal motility. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Peristaltic contractions). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-HT from mucosal cells activates IPANs that initiate peristalsis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: gastrin, CCK, insulin, motilin, and serotonin enhance small intestinal motility, while secretin and glucagon inhibit it (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":1,"stem":"At what interval do migrating myoelectric complexes recur during fasting?","choices":{"A":"At 5 minute intervals","B":"At 30 minute intervals","C":"At 90 minute intervals","D":"At 4 hour intervals","E":"At 12 hour intervals"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Costanzo states that migrating myoelectric complexes recur about every 90 minutes during fasting to clear residual chyme from the stomach and small intestine. The shorter and longer intervals do not match that fasting cycle. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastric motility; Small intestinal motility; motilin). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the interdigestive complex sweeps from stomach through small intestine every 90 minutes while fasting (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: fasting complexes clear the stomach of residual chyme from the previous meal (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — gastric mixing and propulsion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Every 5 minutes is far too frequent.","B":"A 30-minute interval is too short.","D":"A 4-hour interval is too long.","E":"A 12-hour interval would allow only one or two complexes per fast."}},{"id":"sdl25_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":1,"stem":"Which hormone initiates the interdigestive migrating complexes?","choices":{"A":"Cholecystokinin","B":"Secretin","C":"Ghrelin","D":"Motilin","E":"Peptide YY"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Motilin, secreted from the upper duodenum during fasting, initiates the interdigestive myoelectric complexes. CCK and secretin are fed-state hormones that slow gastric emptying. Ghrelin is a gastric hunger hormone, not the initiator of fasting complexes. Peptide YY from ileum and colon inhibits secretion and appetite. CCK, with gastrin, mediates the efferent limb of the gastrocolic reflex; motilin initiates the interdigestive complexes. Secretin decreases action potential frequency and contraction force, so it does not trigger the fasting complexes. Peptide YY from the ileum and colon inhibits gastric acid and pancreatic secretion and decreases appetite; it does not start the complexes. Ghrelin is secreted by gastric cells just before a meal to increase appetite. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (candidate hormones, motilin). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: motilin is a 22 amino acid candidate hormone outside the gastrin-CCK and secretin families (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: motilin receptor agonists such as erythromycin are used as prokinetics (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI hormones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — gastric mixing and propulsion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CCK, with gastrin, mediates the efferent limb of the gastrocolic reflex; motilin initiates the interdigestive complexes.","B":"Secretin decreases action potential frequency and contraction force, so it does not trigger the fasting complexes.","E":"Peptide YY from the ileum and colon inhibits gastric acid and pancreatic secretion and decreases appetite; it does not start the complexes.","C":"Ghrelin is secreted by gastric cells just before a meal to increase appetite."}},{"id":"sdl25_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":1,"stem":"How does eating a meal affect motilin secretion?","choices":{"A":"It inhibits secretion","B":"It stimulates secretion","C":"It doubles the cycle length","D":"It shifts release to the ileum","E":"It has no measurable effect"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton states that motilin is released cyclically during fasting and that its secretion is inhibited after ingestion of food, which is why the fasting complexes stop in the fed state. Stimulation is the opposite effect. Cycle length doubling and an ileal shift are not described; motilin comes from stomach and upper duodenum. Motilin is secreted from the upper duodenum during fasting, so a meal does not stimulate its release. Costanzo states motilin is secreted from the upper duodenum during fasting, not shifted to the ileum by a meal. Sources: Guyton Ch. 63 (motilin); Costanzo Ch. 8 (motilin secreted during fasting). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: feeding replaces fasting complexes with segmentation and peristalsis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: motilin is secreted by the stomach and upper duodenum, not the ileum (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — gastric mixing and propulsion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Motilin is secreted from the upper duodenum during fasting, so a meal does not stimulate its release.","D":"Costanzo states motilin is secreted from the upper duodenum during fasting, not shifted to the ileum by a meal."}},{"id":"sdl25_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":1,"stem":"How often do colonic mass movements occur?","choices":{"A":"Ten to twelve times per minute","B":"Three to five times per hour","C":"Twenty to thirty times per day","D":"Once or twice per week","E":"One to three times per day"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Costanzo states that mass movements move colonic contents long distances, such as transverse to sigmoid colon, one to three times per day. Ten to twelve per minute resembles duodenal slow wave rhythm. The hourly and frequent daily options are too often, and once or twice per week too rare. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Mass movements); Guyton Ch. 64 (series of mass movements lasting 10 to 30 minutes). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a final mass movement propels feces into the rectum and produces the urge to defecate (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: during a mass movement the colon loses haustrations and contracts as a unit (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — teniae coli and haustra coli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ten to twelve per minute resembles duodenal slow wave rhythm.","B":"Three to five times per hour is far more often than colonic mass movements.","C":"Twenty to thirty times per day is far more often than colonic mass movements.","D":"Once or twice per week is less often than colonic mass movements."}},{"id":"sdl25_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":1,"stem":"The efferent limb of the gastrocolic reflex is mediated by which hormones?","choices":{"A":"Secretin and GIP","B":"Motilin and ghrelin","C":"CCK and gastrin","D":"Somatostatin and PYY","E":"VIP and nitric oxide"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Costanzo describes gastric distention as the afferent limb, carried by the parasympathetic nervous system, and CCK and gastrin as the efferent limb that increases colonic motility and mass movements. Secretin and GIP decrease contraction force. Motilin and ghrelin are fasting-state peptides; motilin drives migrating complexes and ghrelin stimulates appetite. Somatostatin inhibits release of the other GI hormones, and peptide YY inhibits gastric and pancreatic secretion. VIP and nitric oxide are inhibitory neurocrines that relax gastrointestinal smooth muscle. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Gastrocolic reflex). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastrocolic reflex explains the urge to defecate soon after a meal (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: gastrocolic and duodenocolic reflexes nearly disappear when extrinsic autonomic nerves to the colon are removed (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — teniae coli and haustra coli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Secretin and GIP decrease contraction force.","B":"Motilin and ghrelin are fasting-state peptides; motilin drives migrating complexes and ghrelin stimulates appetite.","D":"Somatostatin inhibits release of the other GI hormones, and peptide YY inhibits gastric and pancreatic secretion.","E":"VIP and nitric oxide are inhibitory neurocrines that relax gastrointestinal smooth muscle."}},{"id":"sdl25_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":1,"stem":"Which combined contraction forms colonic haustrations?","choices":{"A":"Circular and teniae coli relaxation","B":"Muscularis mucosae fold contraction","C":"External anal sphincter contraction","D":"Circular and teniae coli contraction","E":"Ileocecal sphincter tonic contraction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Guyton describes about 2.5 cm of circular muscle constricting while the teniae coli also contract, so the unstimulated wall bulges into baglike haustrations. Relaxation of both layers would not form sacs. The muscularis mucosae folds the mucosa, and the anal and ileocecal sphincters are not involved. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Mixing movements, haustrations); Costanzo Ch. 8 (segmentation in the cecum and proximal colon). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: haustral contractions peak in about 30 seconds and fade over the next 60 seconds (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: slow haustral mixing takes 8 to 15 hours to move chyme from the ileocecal valve through the colon (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — teniae coli and haustra coli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The external anal sphincter is striated muscle for continence.","A":"Relaxation of both layers would not form sacs.","E":"The ileocecal sphincter contracts to prevent reflux into the ileum.","B":"The muscularis mucosae folds the mucosa."}},{"id":"sdl25_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":1,"stem":"What does rectal distention cause in the rectosphincteric reflex?","choices":{"A":"Relaxation of the external anal sphincter","B":"Contraction of the internal anal sphincter","C":"Relaxation of the internal anal sphincter","D":"Relaxation of the ileocecal sphincter","E":"Contraction of the ileocecal sphincter"},"correct":"C","explanation":"As the rectum fills, its smooth muscle contracts and the internal anal sphincter relaxes in the rectosphincteric reflex. The external sphincter stays tonically contracted until it is relaxed voluntarily. Internal sphincter contraction is the opposite response. Ileocecal sphincter contraction follows entry of chyme into the cecum and is unrelated to rectal filling. The external anal sphincter stays tonically contracted during the rectosphincteric reflex and relaxes only voluntarily. Rectal distention relaxes, not contracts, the internal anal sphincter in the rectosphincteric reflex. Rectal distention relaxes the internal anal sphincter, not the ileocecal sphincter, which acts at the junction of ileum and cecum. The ileocecal sphincter contracts after chyme enters the cecum to prevent reflux into the ileum, a response unrelated to rectal distention. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Defecation). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: defecation is delayed after internal sphincter relaxation because the striated external sphincter remains contracted (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the internal anal sphincter is a tonic smooth muscle sphincter (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — vagus and pelvic nerve distribution","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The external anal sphincter stays tonically contracted during the rectosphincteric reflex and relaxes only voluntarily.","B":"Rectal distention relaxes, not contracts, the internal anal sphincter in the rectosphincteric reflex.","D":"Rectal distention relaxes the internal anal sphincter, not the ileocecal sphincter, which acts at the junction of ileum and cecum.","E":"The ileocecal sphincter contracts after chyme enters the cecum to prevent reflux into the ileum, a response unrelated to rectal distention."}},{"id":"sdl25_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does defecation not occur as soon as rectal filling relaxes the internal anal sphincter?","choices":{"A":"Rectal smooth muscle stays fully relaxed","B":"External sphincter stays contracted","C":"Pelvic nerve activity stops completely","D":"Internal sphincter recontracts at once","E":"Gastrocolic reflex input is suppressed"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The rectosphincteric reflex relaxes the internal anal sphincter, but the striated external anal sphincter is under voluntary control and remains tonically contracted until it is relaxed voluntarily when defecation is appropriate. The rectal smooth muscle actually contracts as the rectum fills, so it does not stay relaxed. Pelvic parasympathetic nerves carry the rectal reflex; their activity does not stop with filling. The internal sphincter relaxes in the reflex, and its recontraction is not what holds stool back. The gastrocolic reflex speeds colonic mass movements and is not what keeps the anus closed. Sources: Costanzo ch 8, defecation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Defecation needs internal sphincter relaxation by reflex plus voluntary external sphincter relaxation, with rectal contraction and Valsalva (Costanzo ch 8). COMLEX: External sphincter or pudendal injury causes urge incontinence despite normal rectal sensation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — vagus and pelvic nerve distribution","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The rectal smooth muscle actually contracts as","C":"Pelvic parasympathetic nerves carry the rectal reflex;","D":"The internal sphincter relaxes in the reflex,","E":"The gastrocolic reflex speeds colonic mass movements"}},{"id":"sdl25_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":1,"stem":"Which nerve controls the external anal sphincter?","choices":{"A":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves","B":"Hypogastric nerves","C":"Vagus nerve","D":"Inferior mesenteric nerves","E":"Pudendal nerve"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Guyton states that the external anal sphincter, striated voluntary muscle, is controlled by the somatic pudendal nerve. Pelvic splanchnic nerves carry sacral parasympathetic fibers that reinforce the defecation reflex. Hypogastric and inferior mesenteric nerves are sympathetic. The vagus does not reach the anal canal. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Defecation); Costanzo Ch. 8 (external anal sphincter is striated). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the external anal sphincter is striated voluntary muscle, unlike the smooth muscle internal sphincter (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: sacral parasympathetic outflow comes from cord segments S2 to S4 (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — vagus and pelvic nerve distribution","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has abdominal distention and has passed no flatus for two days after an open colectomy. Opioids were stopped yesterday, and imaging shows an intact anastomosis without peritonitis. Temperature is 37.2°C, the pulse is 96/min, blood pressure is 134/82 mm Hg, and bowel sounds are absent. Serum potassium is 4.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Heightened sympathetic outflow is suspected. Which change in intestinal smooth-muscle electrical activity is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Faster pacemaker slow wave rhythm","B":"Slower pacemaker slow wave rhythm","C":"Fewer spikes on each slow wave","D":"More spikes on each slow wave","E":"Loss of gap junction coupling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sympathetic outflow reduces the spikes that ride on each slow wave, so contractions fade after surgery. A soft ileus with normal potassium and absent bowel sounds fits fewer spikes rather than a change in pacemaker rate. Sympathetic input does not speed the pacemaker slow-wave rhythm. The slow-wave rhythm itself is not slowed by this sympathetic surge. More spikes would increase contractions, the opposite of this silent abdomen. Gap-junction coupling of the muscle is not what sympathetic outflow removes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: peritoneal irritation inhibits excitatory enteric nerves and can cause intestinal paralysis (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: sympathetic fibers to the small intestine arise from the celiac and superior mesenteric ganglia (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — sympathetic and parasympathetic effects on GI function","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Sympathetic input does not speed the pacemaker slow-wave","B":"The slow-wave rhythm itself is not slowed by this sympathetic","D":"More spikes would increase contractions, the opposite of this silent","E":"Gap-junction coupling of the muscle is not what sympathetic outflow"}},{"id":"sdl25_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman develops constipation and a dry mouth three weeks after starting oxybutynin for urge incontinence. She is not taking opioids. The abdomen is soft and bowel sounds are present. Serum calcium is 9.4 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.5 mg/dL). Reduced action of which transmitter on intestinal smooth muscle best explains the constipation?","choices":{"A":"Norepinephrine","B":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide","C":"Nitric oxide","D":"Somatostatin","E":"Acetylcholine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A drug that blocks muscarinic receptors for urge incontinence also blocks acetylcholine on gut smooth muscle, slowing motility. Dry mouth with new constipation is the cholinergic pair of effects. Norepinephrine is a sympathetic transmitter and is not the signal this bladder drug blocks. Vasoactive intestinal peptide relaxes muscle and is not the transmitter removed here. Nitric oxide mediates inhibitory relaxation and is not the blocked excitatory transmitter. Somatostatin is not the muscarinic transmitter this incontinence drug interrupts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: anticholinergic adverse effects include constipation, dry mouth, urinary retention, and mydriasis (First Aid Pharmacology, muscarinic antagonists). COMLEX: postganglionic parasympathetic neurons sit in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic neurotransmitters and effects on GI function","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Norepinephrine is a sympathetic transmitter and is not the","B":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide relaxes muscle and is not the","C":"Nitric oxide mediates inhibitory relaxation and is not the","D":"Somatostatin is not the muscarinic transmitter this incontinence drug"}},{"id":"sdl25_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has had bloating, vomiting, and distention for two years, and imaging shows no mechanical blockage. She does not have diabetes and is not taking anticholinergic drugs. The abdomen is distended and tympanitic, with quiet bowel sounds. A full-thickness jejunal biopsy shows loss of the pacemaker cells around the myenteric plexus, and serum TSH is 2.1 µU/mL (reference range: 0.4–4.5 µU/mL). Loss of which function most likely explains her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Mucosal serotonin release","B":"Pacemaker slow wave generation","C":"Inhibitory nitrergic signaling","D":"Sympathetic ganglion output","E":"Somatic sphincter control"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The missing cells around the myenteric plexus are the pacemakers that generate slow waves. Without them the bowel cannot organize contractions, which matches a dilated gut with no mechanical obstruction. Mucosal serotonin release starts reflexes but is not the pacemaker function that was lost. Inhibitory nitrergic signaling is a nerve function, not the role of these pacemaker cells. Sympathetic ganglion output is outside the gut wall and is not the missing cell population. Somatic sphincter control uses skeletal muscle and is unrelated to these jejunal pacemakers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: slow waves are not action potentials but oscillations in membrane potential set by pacemaker cells (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: slow wave depolarization is due to inward calcium current and repolarization to outward potassium current (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system regulation of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mucosal serotonin release starts reflexes but is not the","C":"Inhibitory nitrergic signaling is a nerve function, not the","D":"Sympathetic ganglion output is outside the gut wall and","E":"Somatic sphincter control uses skeletal muscle and is unrelated"}},{"id":"sdl25_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":5,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has profuse watery diarrhea and cramps 12 hours after eating undercooked chicken. Temperature is 38.3°C, the pulse is 104/min, and the abdomen is soft with hyperactive bowel sounds. Barium given earlier for another study reached the colon from the jejunum within minutes. Which motility pattern best explains this rapid transit?","choices":{"A":"Peristaltic rush","B":"Segmentation contractions","C":"Migrating motor complex","D":"Haustral contractions","E":"Gastroileal reflex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A powerful peristaltic rush sweeps contents from jejunum to colon within minutes during this infectious diarrhea. Cramping, fever, and that rapid barium transit distinguish it from mixing or a fasting complex. Segmentation mixes contents and does not race barium to the colon in minutes. A migrating motor complex is a fasting housekeeping wave, not this fed infectious rush. Haustral contractions are slow colonic mixing movements. The gastroileal reflex empties the ileum after a meal and does not flush the whole small bowel.","boardPrep":"Step 1: normal small intestinal peristalsis moves chyme only about 1 cm/min, taking 3 to 5 hours from pylorus to ileocecal valve (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: peristaltic rush is driven by autonomic and brainstem reflexes plus intrinsic myenteric reflexes (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton","whyNot":{"B":"Segmentation mixes contents and does not race barium to","C":"A migrating motor complex is a fasting housekeeping wave,","D":"Haustral contractions are slow colonic mixing","E":"The gastroileal reflex empties the ileum after a meal"}},{"id":"sdl25_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman in a drug trial receives an agent that inhibits neuronal nitric oxide synthase in the gut plexuses. Over the next week she has cramps. The abdomen is mildly tender and blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg. Transit of a radiopaque marker is slowed. Failure of which step of the peristaltic reflex best explains the slowing?","choices":{"A":"Contraction of circular muscle behind the bolus","B":"Contraction of longitudinal muscle ahead of the bolus","C":"Relaxation of circular muscle ahead of the bolus","D":"Release of serotonin from mucosal cells","E":"Slow wave generation in pacemaker cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"VIP and nitric oxide relax circular muscle ahead of a bolus, and blocking nitric oxide synthase removes that receiving relaxation. Cramping with slowed transit follows when the bowel cannot open in front of the contents. Contraction behind the bolus is the excitatory cholinergic step, not the nitric-oxide step that was blocked. Longitudinal muscle ahead of the bolus is not the nitric-oxide relaxation that failed. Serotonin release from mucosal cells starts the reflex and is not the inhibited enzyme. Slow-wave generation by pacemaker cells continues and is not nitric-oxide synthase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIP and NO are inhibitory enteric neurocrines that relax gut smooth muscle (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: reciprocal innervation keeps circular and longitudinal layers from contracting together (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — esophageal peristalsis and its neural control","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Contraction behind the bolus is the excitatory cholinergic step,","B":"Longitudinal muscle ahead of the bolus is not the","D":"Serotonin release from mucosal cells starts the reflex and","E":"Slow-wave generation by pacemaker cells continues and is not nitric-oxide"}},{"id":"sdl25_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with abdominal cramps takes a drug that blocks muscarinic receptors. Imaging shows no mechanical obstruction. Jejunal manometry before the dose shows ring contractions about 11 times per minute; afterward the rings fade while the slow-wave frequency is unchanged. Serum magnesium is 2.0 mg/dL (reference range: 1.7–2.2 mg/dL). This change shows that his mixing contractions depend on which input?","choices":{"A":"Circulating motilin from the duodenum","B":"Excitation from the myenteric plexus","C":"Sympathetic outflow from the celiac ganglion","D":"Voluntary outflow from the sacral cord","E":"Stretch afferents of the colonic wall"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The mixing rings fade when muscarinic excitation from the myenteric plexus is blocked, while the slow-wave frequency stays the same. That split shows the contractions need neural excitation on top of the pacemaker rhythm. Circulating motilin drives fasting complexes, not these fed mixing rings. Sympathetic outflow from the celiac ganglion inhibits the gut and is not the muscarinic drive that was blocked. Voluntary sacral outflow does not generate jejunal mixing rings. Stretch afferents of the colon are not the muscarinic input removed by this drug.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the terminal ileum the maximum segmentation rate is about 8 to 9 per minute (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: segmentation exposes chyme to pancreatic enzymes and the mucosa for absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system and extrinsic modulation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Circulating motilin drives fasting complexes, not these fed mixing","C":"Sympathetic outflow from the celiac ganglion inhibits the gut","D":"Voluntary sacral outflow does not generate jejunal mixing","E":"Stretch afferents of the colon are not the muscarinic"}},{"id":"sdl25_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":5,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with long-standing type 1 diabetes has early satiety and vomiting after meals. He takes insulin and has never had gastric surgery. The abdomen is soft. A gastric-emptying study shows marked delay, and a low dose of a macrolide antibiotic improves the symptoms. Hemoglobin A1c is 8.2% (reference range: <5.7%). Which drug action most likely explains the improvement?","choices":{"A":"Dopamine D2 antagonism","B":"Muscarinic receptor agonism","C":"Serotonin 5-HT3 antagonism","D":"Mu opioid receptor agonism","E":"Motilin receptor agonism"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Low-dose erythromycin speeds emptying by agonism at motilin receptors. Delayed emptying in long-standing diabetes that improves on that macrolide fits this prokinetic action. Dopamine D2 antagonism is the action of a different prokinetic, not of this macrolide. Direct muscarinic agonism is not how a low-dose macrolide improves emptying. Serotonin 5-HT3 antagonism reduces nausea and does not provide this prokinetic effect. Mu opioid agonism slows the gut and would worsen the delay.","boardPrep":"Step 1: metoclopramide is a D2 antagonist prokinetic that can cause tardive dyskinesia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, metoclopramide). COMLEX: motilin is released cyclically during fasting and suppressed by eating (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — gastric mixing and propulsion","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton","whyNot":{"A":"Dopamine D2 antagonism is the action of a different","B":"Direct muscarinic agonism is not how a low-dose macrolide improves","C":"Serotonin 5-HT3 antagonism reduces nausea and does not provide","D":"Mu opioid agonism slows the gut and would worsen the"}},{"id":"sdl25_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with tight skin over the fingers has chronic bloating and loose stools. She is not taking opioids and has had no intestinal surgery. The abdomen is soft and mildly distended. A lactulose breath test shows an early hydrogen rise, and fasting antroduodenal recordings show no phase III activity over six hours. Serum albumin is 3.6 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Loss of which function most likely contributed?","choices":{"A":"Postprandial mixing of jejunal chyme","B":"Rectal storage of formed feces","C":"Colonic absorption of luminal water","D":"Interdigestive clearance of residue","E":"Gastrocolic reflex after meals"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Absent phase III activity means the migrating motor complex is lost, so residue is no longer cleared between meals. An early hydrogen rise and loose stools follow bacterial overgrowth when that sweep is gone. Postprandial mixing is a fed pattern and is not the missing fasting phase III. Rectal storage does not clear small-bowel residue during fasting. Colonic water absorption does not produce the absent phase III complexes. The gastrocolic reflex follows meals and is not the fasting sweep that failed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: small intestinal bacterial overgrowth causes bloating, diarrhea, and a positive hydrogen breath test (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption). COMLEX: the interdigestive complex moves through the stomach and small intestine in a fasting person (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — pathogenesis of malabsorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Postprandial mixing is a fed pattern and is not","B":"Rectal storage does not clear small-bowel residue during","C":"Colonic water absorption does not produce the absent phase III","E":"The gastrocolic reflex follows meals and is not the"}},{"id":"sdl25_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old healthy woman in a fasting motility study has bands of intense contractions that start in the stomach and travel down the small intestine. She is not taking prokinetic drugs and does not have diabetes. Blood glucose is 92 mg/dL (reference range: 70–99 mg/dL) before breakfast, and the complexes stop when she eats. Which change in the recording is most likely after the meal?","choices":{"A":"Shortening of the complex interval","B":"Spread of complexes to the colon","C":"Interruption of the complex cycle","D":"Increase in phase III force","E":"Doubling of slow wave frequency"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Food inhibits motilin secretion and interrupts the migrating complex cycle, so the fasting bands stop. A normal fasting glucose and cessation when she eats show that interruption rather than a change in slow-wave rate. The interval is not merely shortened; the cycle is interrupted by the meal. These complexes do not spread into the colon after breakfast. Phase III force does not increase as the explanation for the complexes stopping. Slow-wave frequency does not double when the meal arrives.","boardPrep":"Step 1: peristalsis rises after a meal through duodenal stretch and the gastroenteric reflex (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: motilin is a candidate hormone secreted from the upper duodenum during fasting (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — hormonal control of gastric and duodenal motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The interval is not merely shortened; the cycle is","B":"These complexes do not spread into the colon after","D":"Phase III force does not increase as the explanation","E":"Slow-wave frequency does not double when the meal"}},{"id":"sdl25_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman needs to pass stool within 20 minutes after almost every breakfast. She has no weight loss, no blood in the stool, and no nocturnal diarrhea. Examination is normal. Hemoglobin is 13.8 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL). Which reflex best explains the timing of the urge?","choices":{"A":"Gastroileal reflex","B":"Rectosphincteric reflex","C":"Enterogastric reflex","D":"Gastrocolic reflex","E":"Peritoneointestinal reflex"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The urge soon after breakfast is the gastrocolic reflex, in which a meal prompts colonic mass movement. Absence of bleeding, weight loss, and night symptoms keeps this a physiologic response. The gastroileal reflex empties the ileum and does not produce the call to stool. The rectosphincteric reflex relaxes the internal sphincter when the rectum fills. The enterogastric reflex slows the stomach when the intestine is full. The peritoneointestinal reflex inhibits the gut when the peritoneum is irritated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an exaggerated gastrocolic reflex contributes to postprandial urgency in irritable bowel syndrome (First Aid Gastrointestinal, irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: the afferent limb of the gastrocolic reflex is parasympathetic (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — enterogastric reflexes and duodenal stimuli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The gastroileal reflex empties the ileum and does not","B":"The rectosphincteric reflex relaxes the internal sphincter when the rectum","C":"The enterogastric reflex slows the stomach when the intestine is","E":"The peritoneointestinal reflex inhibits the gut when the peritoneum is"}},{"id":"sdl25_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":5,"stem":"A 31-year-old man is passing 8 to 10 bloody stools a day during a flare of the same illness he has had for years. Temperature is 37.9°C and the pulse is 102/min. Hemoglobin is 11.2 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), C-reactive protein is 48 mg/L (reference range: <10 mg/L), p-ANCA is positive, and ASCA is negative. Which colonic motility pattern is most likely increased?","choices":{"A":"Haustral mixing","B":"Mass movements","C":"Migrating complexes","D":"Jejunal segmentation","E":"Reverse peristalsis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"During this colitis flare, colonic mass movements are persistently increased and drive the frequent bloody stools. Fever, a high C-reactive protein, and p-ANCA with a negative ASCA mark the inflammatory drive. Haustral mixing is not the pattern that produces many urgent bloody stools. Migrating complexes are a fasting small-bowel pattern, not this colonic flare. Jejunal segmentation mixes small-bowel contents and does not cause this colitis pattern. Reverse peristalsis would move content orad and does not explain frequent defecation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis begins in the rectum and extends proximally in continuous fashion (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel disease). COMLEX: a series of mass movements lasts 10 to 30 minutes and may recur half a day later (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 5 — lymphatic drainage and ulcerative colitis","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton","whyNot":{"A":"Haustral mixing is not the pattern that produces many","C":"Migrating complexes are a fasting small-bowel pattern, not this colonic","D":"Jejunal segmentation mixes small-bowel contents and does not cause","E":"Reverse peristalsis would move content orad and does not"}},{"id":"sdl25_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with mild constipation has colonic contractions that shift contents back and forth over a few minutes. Thyroid studies are normal and there is no mass. Serum calcium is 9.2 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.5 mg/dL). Which function do these contractions mainly serve?","choices":{"A":"Mixing contents for water absorption","B":"Propelling feces into the rectum","C":"Clearing residue during fasting","D":"Relaxing the internal anal sphincter","E":"Preventing backflow into the ileum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"These nonpropulsive contractions mix contents so water can be absorbed. Normal calcium and thyroid studies, and the lack of a mass, leave this as ordinary haustral mixing. Propelling feces into the rectum is the job of mass movements, not these short to-and-fro contractions. Clearing residue during fasting is the migrating complex, which is not this fed mixing pattern. Relaxing the internal sphincter is a rectoanal reflex, not a colonic mixing contraction. Preventing backflow into the ileum is the ileocecal sphincter, not these colonic rings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: teniae coli are three longitudinal muscle bands that create haustra (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: water absorption in the distal colon makes feces semisolid and harder to move (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — teniae coli and haustra coli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Propelling feces into the rectum is the job of","C":"Clearing residue during fasting is the migrating complex, which","D":"Relaxing the internal sphincter is a rectoanal reflex, not","E":"Preventing backflow into the ileum is the ileocecal sphincter,"}},{"id":"sdl25_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":5,"stem":"A 4-day-old boy has not passed meconium and has progressive abdominal distension. He was born at term. The abdomen is distended, and digital rectal examination releases an explosive gush of stool. Serum sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Absence of which cells in the distal colon most likely explains this?","choices":{"A":"Interstitial cells of Cajal","B":"Striated sphincter muscle fibers","C":"Enterochromaffin cells of the mucosa","D":"Sacral preganglionic parasympathetic neurons","E":"Ganglion cells of the myenteric plexus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Failure to pass meconium with an explosive release of stool means ganglion cells of the myenteric plexus are absent from the distal colon. A normal serum sodium excludes a metabolic cause of the ileus. Interstitial cells of Cajal set slow waves and are not the cells missing in this distal functional obstruction. Striated sphincter fibers are skeletal muscle and are not absent in this newborn pattern. Enterochromaffin cells release serotonin and are not the missing neurons. Sacral preganglionic neurons lie in the cord, not in the distal bowel wall that lacks ganglia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hirschsprung disease results from failed neural crest migration and is associated with Down syndrome and RET mutations (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: rectal suction biopsy is diagnostic, showing absent ganglion cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — myenteric and submucosal plexuses","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton","whyNot":{"A":"Interstitial cells of Cajal set slow waves and are","B":"Striated sphincter fibers are skeletal muscle and are not","C":"Enterochromaffin cells release serotonin and are not the missing","D":"Sacral preganglionic neurons lie in the cord, not in"}},{"id":"sdl25_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman leaks stool when she coughs and cannot voluntarily squeeze the anus. She had a difficult vaginal delivery and has no back pain. Limb strength is normal. Anal manometry shows reduced squeeze pressure with preserved resting tone. Dysfunction of which structure most likely explains the leakage?","choices":{"A":"External anal sphincter","B":"Internal anal sphincter","C":"Rectal circular muscle","D":"Sigmoid colon teniae","E":"Ileocecal sphincter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loss of voluntary squeeze with preserved resting tone localizes to the external anal sphincter. A difficult delivery is the usual reason that striated sphincter fails while the internal sphincter still holds resting pressure. The internal sphincter provides resting tone, which is preserved here. Rectal circular muscle does not provide the voluntary squeeze she has lost. Sigmoid teniae organize haustra and do not mediate voluntary continence. The ileocecal sphincter is far from the anus and does not control cough leakage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pudendal nerve can be blocked at the ischial spine for vaginal delivery (First Aid Reproductive, pudendal nerve). COMLEX: the external sphincter is held contracted subconsciously unless conscious signals inhibit it (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — vagus and pelvic nerve distribution","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The internal sphincter provides resting tone, which is preserved","C":"Rectal circular muscle does not provide the voluntary squeeze","D":"Sigmoid teniae organize haustra and do not mediate voluntary","E":"The ileocecal sphincter is far from the anus and"}},{"id":"sdl25_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has weak rectal emptying after pelvic surgery that divided the nerves beside the rectum. He is not taking opioids and has no spinal cord injury. Soft stool sits in the vault, and voluntary squeeze is intact. Serum potassium is 4.3 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Loss of which pathway most likely explains the weak propulsion?","choices":{"A":"Sympathetic fibers in the hypogastric nerves","B":"Vagal fibers supplying the proximal colon","C":"Intrinsic myenteric fibers in the rectum","D":"Parasympathetic fibers in the pelvic nerves","E":"Somatic fibers to the abdominal wall muscles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Rectal propulsion uses parasympathetic fibers in the pelvic nerves, which pelvic surgery can divide. Intact voluntary squeeze and soft stool in the vault show that the somatic sphincter and the stool consistency are not the problem. Sympathetic fibers in the hypogastric nerves are inhibitory and are not the propulsive pathway that was cut. Vagal fibers reach the proximal colon and do not drive the rectum. Intrinsic myenteric fibers remain in the wall and are not the pathway divided outside the rectum. Somatic fibers to the abdominal wall raise pressure but are not the rectal propulsive nerves.","boardPrep":"Step 1: defecation signals entering the cord also trigger a deep breath, glottic closure, and abdominal muscle contraction (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: sacral parasympathetic fibers via the pelvic nerves supply the distal colon, rectum, and anus (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — pelvic nerve parasympathetic distribution","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Sympathetic fibers in the hypogastric nerves are inhibitory and","B":"Vagal fibers reach the proximal colon and do not","C":"Intrinsic myenteric fibers remain in the wall and are","E":"Somatic fibers to the abdominal wall raise pressure but"}},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man starts a calcium-channel blocker for hypertension and develops constipation. He is not taking opioids, and imaging shows no obstruction. The abdomen is soft, the pulse is 60/min, and blood pressure is 128/76 mm Hg. The same drug slows atrioventricular conduction and relaxes vascular smooth muscle. Which current underlying intestinal slow-wave depolarization does this drug most directly reduce?","choices":{"A":"Inward sodium current","B":"Inward calcium current","C":"Outward potassium current","D":"Outward chloride current","E":"Inward magnesium current"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The upstroke of intestinal slow waves depends on inward calcium current, which this blocker reduces. Constipation together with slowed atrioventricular conduction and vasodilation is the same calcium-channel effect. The slow-wave upstroke in gut muscle is not an inward sodium current. Outward potassium current repolarizes the cell and is not the current this drug blocks. Outward chloride current is not the depolarizing current reduced here. Inward magnesium current does not generate the slow-wave upstroke.","boardPrep":"Step 1: constipation is a common adverse effect of nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers such as verapamil (First Aid Cardiovascular, calcium channel blockers). COMLEX: even subthreshold slow waves produce weak basal contractions (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The slow-wave upstroke in gut muscle is not an","C":"Outward potassium current repolarizes the cell and is not","D":"Outward chloride current is not the depolarizing current reduced","E":"Inward magnesium current does not generate the slow-wave"}},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":3,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman has constipation with infrequent pellet-like stools. She is not taking anticholinergic drugs, and thyroid studies are normal. The abdomen is soft, with stool in the left colon. Manometry shows mostly nonpropagating ring contractions, few propagated waves, and no mass movements. Serum calcium is 9.3 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.5 mg/dL). Which property of the dominant contraction pattern best explains the constipation?","choices":{"A":"Relaxing circular muscle ahead of contents","B":"Raising the frequency of mass movements","C":"Narrowing the segment behind the bolus","D":"Speeding transit toward the rectum","E":"Mixing without net forward movement"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The dominant rings mix contents without net forward movement, so stool is not propelled. Few propagated waves and absent mass movements leave mixing as the pattern that explains the pellets. Relaxing circular muscle ahead of contents would speed transit, which is not happening. Raising the frequency of mass movements would relieve constipation, and those movements are absent. Narrowing the segment behind a bolus is the propulsive step she lacks. Speeding transit toward the rectum is the opposite of this recording.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Segmentation mixes chyme without forward movement, whereas peristalsis contracts behind and relaxes ahead of the bolus to propel it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Opioid-induced constipation is expected and should be anticipated with a bowel regimen.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Relaxing circular muscle ahead of contents would speed transit,","B":"Raising the frequency of mass movements would relieve constipation,","C":"Narrowing the segment behind a bolus is the propulsive","D":"Speeding transit toward the rectum is the opposite of this"}},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old healthy man in a fasting study has intense contractions that sweep from the stomach through the small intestine about every 90 minutes while he is fasting. The complexes disappear for several hours after each meal. Blood glucose before the meal is 90 mg/dL (reference range: 70–99 mg/dL). Which mechanism best explains the missing pattern after he eats?","choices":{"A":"Gastrin release halts the slow waves","B":"Food keeps motilin release suppressed","C":"Vagal input silences Cajal pacemakers","D":"Mucosal serotonin stores are depleted","E":"Duodenal cells oversecrete motilin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Food suppresses motilin release, so the fasting complexes that depend on motilin stop after a meal. A healthy subject whose 90-minute sweeps vanish only after eating shows that suppression. Gastrin does not halt the slow waves when a meal arrives. Vagal input does not silence the pacemaker cells as the reason the complexes stop. Mucosal serotonin stores are not depleted by an ordinary meal. Duodenal cells do not oversecrete motilin after eating; release falls.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Motilin released cyclically during fasting drives migrating motor complexes every 90 minutes, and eating inhibits its secretion (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: Loss of the fasting clearance pattern predisposes to stasis and bacterial overgrowth.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gastrin does not halt the slow waves when a meal","C":"Vagal input does not silence the pacemaker cells as","D":"Mucosal serotonin stores are not depleted by an ordinary","E":"Duodenal cells do not oversecrete motilin after eating; release"}},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":5,"stem":"A 54-year-old man with an end ileostomy after colectomy notices that stoma output rises sharply within minutes of each meal. The stoma is pink and there is no obstruction. Serum sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Which reflex best explains the increase?","choices":{"A":"Gastrocolic reflex","B":"Enterogastric reflex","C":"Rectosphincteric reflex","D":"Renointestinal reflex","E":"Gastroileal reflex"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A meal increases ileal emptying through the gastroileal reflex, which raises ileostomy output within minutes. A pink stoma and no obstruction keep this a reflex rather than a mechanical problem. The gastrocolic reflex acts on the colon, which has been removed. The enterogastric reflex slows the stomach and would not increase stoma output. The rectosphincteric reflex concerns the anus, which is not in continuity here. The renointestinal reflex is a response to renal irritation, not to a meal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ileocecal sphincter normally holds chyme in the ileum, prolonging absorption (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: about 1500 to 2000 mL of chyme empties into the cecum each day (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton","whyNot":{"A":"The gastrocolic reflex acts on the colon, which has been","B":"The enterogastric reflex slows the stomach and would not","C":"The rectosphincteric reflex concerns the anus, which is not","D":"The renointestinal reflex is a response to renal irritation,"}},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman strains and cannot empty the rectum. She has no back injury and is not taking opioids. When the rectum is distended, the internal sphincter relaxes, but anal-canal pressure then rises and electromyography shows increased activity in the striated sphincter. Serum potassium is 4.0 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Which component of defecation is abnormal?","choices":{"A":"Reflex relaxation of the internal sphincter","B":"Sensory detection of rectal filling","C":"Voluntary external sphincter relaxation","D":"Generation of intraabdominal pressure","E":"Enteric ganglia in the rectal wall"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The internal sphincter relaxes as it should, but the external sphincter contracts instead of relaxing, so voluntary external-sphincter relaxation has failed. Rising anal pressure and increased striated-muscle activity during attempted defecation show that paradox. Reflex relaxation of the internal sphincter is intact on this recording. She senses the distention, so detection of rectal filling is present. Generation of intra-abdominal pressure is not the component that shows increased sphincter EMG. Enteric ganglia in the rectal wall are working, because the internal sphincter still relaxes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In defecation the rectosphincteric reflex relaxes the internal sphincter, and the external sphincter must be relaxed voluntarily while rectal and abdominal pressure rise (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Paradoxical contraction of the striated sphincter while straining is a treatable cause of chronic constipation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Reflex relaxation of the internal sphincter is intact on this","B":"She senses the distention, so detection of rectal filling is","D":"Generation of intra-abdominal pressure is not the component that","E":"Enteric ganglia in the rectal wall are working, because"}},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":5,"stem":"A 72-year-old man has marked cecal distention from an obstructing sigmoid mass. He has lost 5 kg and has had chronic constipation. The abdomen is distended and tympanitic, and the cecum is tender. Hemoglobin is 10.2 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and carcinoembryonic antigen is 22 ng/mL (reference range: <3.0 ng/mL). Which reflex response is expected where the small intestine joins the large intestine?","choices":{"A":"Contraction of the ileocecal sphincter","B":"Intensified peristalsis in the ileum","C":"Faster emptying of ileal chyme","D":"Increased jejunal segmentation","E":"Relaxation of the internal sphincter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cecal distention from a distal obstruction reflexly contracts the ileocecal sphincter, limiting further inflow. A sigmoid tumor with an elevated carcinoembryonic antigen is the blockage that distends the cecum. Intensified ileal peristalsis would push more content into an already distended cecum and is not the protective reflex. Faster ileal emptying is the opposite of the response to cecal distention. Increased jejunal segmentation does not guard the distended cecum. Relaxation of the internal anal sphincter is a rectal reflex and does not answer cecal distention.","boardPrep":"Step 1: large bowel obstruction is most often caused by colorectal cancer in older adults (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal obstruction). COMLEX: an inflamed appendix can cause ileocecal sphincter spasm and partial ileal paralysis (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton","whyNot":{"B":"Intensified ileal peristalsis would push more content into an","C":"Faster ileal emptying is the opposite of the response","D":"Increased jejunal segmentation does not guard the distended","E":"Relaxation of the internal anal sphincter is a rectal"}},{"id":"grid_sdl25_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":4,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has episodes of reflex bowel emptying at unpredictable times. A motorcycle crash one year ago completely transected the spinal cord at T6, and spinal shock has resolved. He is not taking anticholinergic drugs. Examination shows a T6 sensory level, no perianal sensation, and preserved knee jerks. Serum sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Which of the following sets of anorectal findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Reflex IAS relaxation","Voluntary EAS squeeze","Rectal fullness sensation"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↔","↔"],"B":["↔","↓","↔"],"C":["↔","↓","↓"],"D":["↔","↔","↔"],"E":["↔","↔","↓"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"Choice C (↔ reflex IAS relaxation, ↓ voluntary EAS squeeze, ↓ rectal fullness sensation) is correct. The intrinsic myenteric defecation reflex and the sacral parasympathetic reflex through the pelvic nerves lie below the T6 lesion, so rectal distention still relaxes the internal anal sphincter, and Guyton notes that people with transected cords have automatic emptying of the lower bowel. Conscious control of the external anal sphincter through the pudendal nerve needs descending cortical input, and the sensation of rectal fullness needs ascending tracts, so both are lost. Choice A (↓ reflex IAS relaxation, ↔ voluntary EAS squeeze, ↔ rectal fullness sensation) is Hirschsprung disease, in which aganglionic bowel fails to relax the internal sphincter. Choice B (↔ reflex IAS relaxation, ↓ voluntary EAS squeeze, ↔ rectal fullness sensation) is pudendal nerve injury, as after a difficult vaginal delivery. Choice D (↔ reflex IAS relaxation, ↔ voluntary EAS squeeze, ↔ rectal fullness sensation) is normal anorectal function, as in functional constipation. Choice E (↔ reflex IAS relaxation, ↔ voluntary EAS squeeze, ↓ rectal fullness sensation) is a rectal sensory neuropathy, as in long-standing diabetes. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Defecation; Defecation reflexes; transected spinal cords); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Defecation, rectosphincteric reflex); First Aid Gastrointestinal (Hirschsprung disease). The ↔ cells and the diabetic sensory row are inferred because the sources do not describe them as changed. Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the sacral parasympathetic defecation reflex runs from rectal afferents to cord segments S2 to S4 and back through the pelvic nerves (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: spinal cord lesions above the sacral segments leave reflex bowel emptying intact but remove voluntary sphincter control (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — pelvic nerve parasympathetic distribution","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q31","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has crampy pain and bloating after meals. He is not taking opioids, and thyroid studies are normal. The abdomen is soft, with rushes of bowel sounds but no mass. Hemoglobin is 14.2 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). Jejunal manometry shows the lumen failing to narrow behind a bolus, and a drug that blocks substance P receptors reproduces the finding. Which transmitter pair normally constricts circular muscle behind the bolus?","choices":{"A":"Nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide","B":"Secretin and gastric inhibitory peptide","C":"Acetylcholine and substance P","D":"Norepinephrine and somatostatin","E":"Dopamine and motilin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Behind a bolus, circular muscle contracts under acetylcholine and substance P from the myenteric plexus. Nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide relax circular muscle ahead of the bolus. Secretin and gastric inhibitory peptide are duodenal hormones that slow gastric emptying, not the orad circular-muscle pair. Norepinephrine is the sympathetic brake on motility, and somatostatin is not the orad excitatory pair. Dopamine inhibits gut motility, and motilin starts the fasting complex rather than the orad squeeze.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide relax circular muscle ahead of the bolus","B":"Secretin and gastric inhibitory peptide are duodenal hormones that slow gastric emptying, not the orad circular-muscle pair","D":"Norepinephrine is the sympathetic brake on motility, and somatostatin is not the orad excitatory pair","E":"Dopamine inhibits gut motility, and motilin starts the fasting complex rather than the orad squeeze"}},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q07","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has bloating without weight loss. She is not taking anticholinergic drugs. The abdomen is soft and nontender. Thyroid-stimulating hormone is 2.1 mIU/L (reference range: 0.4–4.0 mIU/L) and hemoglobin is 13.4 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL). Manometry shows mixing contractions at 12 per minute in the duodenum and 8 per minute in the ileum. What sets that ceiling?","choices":{"A":"The local slow-wave frequency","B":"The number of meals eaten that day","C":"Voluntary contraction of the external anal sphincter","D":"Portal venous pressure","E":"The titer of anti-tissue transglutaminase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The maximum rate of segmentation is limited by the local slow-wave frequency, about 12 per minute in the duodenum and 8 to 9 per minute in the ileum. Meal timing replaces the fasting complex with fed motility, but it does not set the 12-per-minute duodenal ceiling. The external anal sphincter is skeletal muscle of the anal canal and does not pace duodenal segmentation. Portal pressure does not set the frequency of intestinal slow waves. Anti-tissue transglutaminase is a celiac marker and does not determine slow-wave rate.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Meal timing replaces the fasting complex with fed motility, but it does not set the 12-per-minute duodenal ceiling","C":"The external anal sphincter is skeletal muscle of the anal canal and does not pace duodenal segmentation","D":"Portal pressure does not set the frequency of intestinal slow waves","E":"Anti-tissue transglutaminase is a celiac marker and does not determine slow-wave rate"}},{"id":"sdl25_q32","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old healthy woman is in a fasting motility study. She has not eaten since midnight and takes no prokinetic drugs. The abdomen is soft. Glucose is 86 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL) and potassium is 4.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Intense contraction bands sweep down the small bowel about every 90 minutes. Which cells release the hormone that starts those bands?","choices":{"A":"Parietal cells of the stomach","B":"Hepatocytes","C":"Pancreatic islet beta cells","D":"M cells of the upper duodenum","E":"Chief cells of the gastric fundus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Motilin, which initiates the interdigestive migrating complex, is released from M cells of the upper duodenum during fasting. Parietal cells secrete acid and intrinsic factor, not the hormone that starts the fasting complex. Hepatocytes make bile and plasma proteins, not motilin. Beta cells secrete insulin in response to glucose, and a meal abolishes the fasting complex. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen, not the peptide that starts migrating complexes.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Parietal cells secrete acid and intrinsic factor, not the hormone that starts the fasting complex","B":"Hepatocytes make bile and plasma proteins, not motilin","C":"Beta cells secrete insulin in response to glucose, and a meal abolishes the fasting complex","E":"Chief cells secrete pepsinogen, not the peptide that starts migrating complexes"}},{"id":"sdl25_q33","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman needs to pass stool within 20 minutes of breakfast on most days. She has no bleeding, no weight loss, and no nocturnal stools. The abdomen is soft and the rectal exam is normal. Hemoglobin is 13.5 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL) and C-reactive protein is 2 mg/L (reference range: <10 mg/L). Stretch receptors that start this meal-linked colonic response sit in which organ, and which nerve carries their afferents?","choices":{"A":"Rectum, carried by the pudendal nerve","B":"Cecum, carried by the phrenic nerve","C":"Duodenum, carried by the pelvic splanchnic nerves","D":"Sigmoid colon, carried only by sympathetic hypogastric nerves","E":"Stomach, carried by the vagus nerve"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The afferent limb of the gastrocolic reflex begins in gastric mechanoreceptors and travels in the vagus. Rectal stretch and the pudendal nerve belong to defecation and continence, not the meal-triggered colonic reflex. The cecum is not the organ whose filling starts this reflex, and the phrenic nerve is the diaphragm's nerve. The duodenum is not the source of the afferents, and pelvic splanchnics are sacral parasympathetic efferents. The sigmoid is a destination of mass movements, not the site of the stretch receptors that start the reflex.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Rectal stretch and the pudendal nerve belong to defecation and continence, not the meal-triggered colonic reflex","B":"The cecum is not the organ whose filling starts this reflex, and the phrenic nerve is the diaphragm's nerve","C":"The duodenum is not the source of the afferents, and pelvic splanchnics are sacral parasympathetic efferents","D":"The sigmoid is a destination of mass movements, not the site of the stretch receptors that start the reflex"}},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q08","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has an urge to stool about 40 minutes after breakfast. He has no bleeding and has not lost weight. The abdomen is soft and nontender. Hemoglobin is 14.6 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL) and potassium is 4.0 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). During the urge, a long segment of colon distal to a constricting ring flattens its sacculations and shortens as a single unit. What has happened to that segment?","choices":{"A":"It is only mixing contents back and forth over a few centimeters","B":"It has lost its haustra and is contracting as one segment","C":"Its external sphincter has been cut by the pudendal nerve","D":"Its slow waves have stopped, so the muscle cannot contract","E":"It is secreting bicarbonate into a closed diverticulum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In a mass movement the colon distal to the constricting ring loses its haustrations and contracts as one segment. Back-and-forth mixing over a short distance is haustration, not this long unified contraction. The pudendal nerve supplies the external anal sphincter and does not divide it during a colonic propulsive wave. The segment is contracting, so its muscle has not lost the ability to fire. Bicarbonate secretion into a diverticulum is not the motor event that clears haustra.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Back-and-forth mixing over a short distance is haustration, not this long unified contraction","C":"The pudendal nerve supplies the external anal sphincter and does not divide it during a colonic propulsive wave","D":"The segment is contracting, so its muscle has not lost the ability to fire","E":"Bicarbonate secretion into a diverticulum is not the motor event that clears haustra"}},{"id":"sdl25_q34","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has weak rectal emptying after pelvic surgery, but a strong urge still appears when the rectum fills. He is not taking opioids. The abdomen is soft, anal wink is reduced, and there is no spinal tenderness. Sodium is 140 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L) and glucose is 92 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL). The parasympathetic reflex that intensifies rectal contraction returns from which spinal levels?","choices":{"A":"S2 to S4","B":"C3 to C5","C":"T1 to T4","D":"L1 only","E":"The midbrain vagal nuclei"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The parasympathetic defecation reflex arcs through sacral cord segments S2 to S4 and returns in the pelvic nerves. C3 to C5 is the phrenic motor pool for the diaphragm, not the sacral defecation reflex. T1 to T4 is sympathetic outflow, not the sacral parasympathetic arc. L1 is not the sacral segment that returns the defecation reflex. Vagal nuclei are cranial and do not provide the sacral boost to rectal contraction.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"C3 to C5 is the phrenic motor pool for the diaphragm, not the sacral defecation reflex","C":"T1 to T4 is sympathetic outflow, not the sacral parasympathetic arc","D":"L1 is not the sacral segment that returns the defecation reflex","E":"Vagal nuclei are cranial and do not provide the sacral boost to rectal contraction"}}]};
